// Generated from docs/ by `kiln run site/tools/content`. Do not edit.

fn doc_sources() -> List<DocSource> {
    return [
        DocSource(path: "README", section: "Documentation", lines: [
            "# Kiln documentation",
            "",
            "This directory separates **current language and tooling reference** from",
            "**implementation plans**. The root [README](../README.md) contains the",
            "project overview, installation instructions and phase roadmap.",
            "",
            "## Language reference",
            "",
            "These pages document the syntax, types and APIs **currently supported or",
            "recognized** by Kiln's compiler and checker; some parsed/checkable features",
            "are not yet executable. Check their status notes before relying on a feature.",
            "",
            "- [Kiln syntax](reference/syntax.md) — source files, declarations and expressions",
            "- [Projects and modules](reference/projects.md) — local imports, visibility, roots and manifests",
            "- [Glazes](reference/glazes.md) — template/component syntax and type checking",
            "- [Databases, models and migrations](reference/data.md) — `sql`, `orm` and `kiln db`",
            "- [Type rules](reference/types.md) — inference, records, optionals and more",
            "- [Standard library](reference/stdlib.md) — available modules and APIs",
            "",
            "## Guides",
            "",
            "- [Editor setup](guides/editors.md) — LSP, VS Code, Neovim and Helix",
            "",
            "## Project direction",
            "",
            "- [Design overview](design.md) — architecture and technical decisions",
            "- [Long-term vision](vision.md) — principles, ecosystem and future scope",
            "- [Roadmap](../README.md#roadmap) — milestones and completion status",
            "",
            "## Implementation plans",
            "",
            "Plans describe work being implemented, upcoming milestones and intended",
            "APIs. **Proposed syntax in a plan is not automatically implemented syntax.**",
            "",
            "- [Phase 2.5 — VS Code developer experience](plans/phase-2.5.md) (completed)",
            "- [Phase 3 — Glazes, native styling, Finishes and themes](plans/phase-3.md)",
            "- [Phase 4 — Embers, WasmGC, reactive UI and typed server functions](plans/phase-4.md)",
            "- [Phase 4.1 — Explicit `.ember` files and server/client component boundaries](plans/phase-4.1.md) (complete; included in v0.9.0)",
            "- [Phase 4.2 — Multi-folder projects and local modules](plans/phase-4.2.md) (complete; included in v0.9.0)",
            "- [Phase 5 — SQL, ORM, migrations, forms, authentication and admin](plans/phase-5.md)",
            "- [Phase 6 — Wares, testing, tooling and agent integration](plans/phase-6.md)",
            "- [Moving the runtime to Rust](plans/runtime-rust.md)",
            "- [Integrated testing — unit, integration and browser](plans/testing.md)",
            "",
            "For changes to the language, update the relevant reference page as the",
            "feature lands. For scope/sequence changes, update the appropriate plan and",
            "the [roadmap](../README.md#roadmap).",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "reference/syntax", section: "Language reference", lines: [
            "# Kiln syntax reference",
            "",
            "This is the syntax the current Kiln parser accepts. It is the source of truth",
            "for the grammar until a formal specification exists; every example here is",
            "also covered by a test in `crates/kiln_syntax/tests/`.",
            "",
            "## Files and lines",
            "",
            "- Source files end in `.kiln`. Server-only pages and components end in",
            "  `.glaze`; browser-capable components end in `.ember`. See",
            "  [glazes.md](glazes.md).",
            "- A newline ends a statement. There are no semicolons.",
            "- Newlines are ignored inside `( )`, `[ ]` and record literals `{ name: value }`,",
            "  so long calls can span lines.",
            "- An expression continues onto the next line when the line ends with a binary",
            "  operator, or when the next line starts with `.` (method chains).",
            "- Comments: `// line` and `/* block */`.",
            "- A folder is one module. Its `.kiln` files share declarations; imports stay",
            "  per source file. `import posts` and `import posts.search` load explicit local",
            "  folder modules from the project root. `pub` exports declarations. See",
            "  [projects.md](projects.md) for roots, visibility and optional `kiln.toml`.",
            "",
            "## Declarations",
            "",
            "Only declarations may appear at the top level of a file.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import web, sql, web.router",
            "",
            "const PAGE_SIZE: Int = 20",
            "",
            "pub struct Post {",
            "    id: Int",
            "    title: String",
            "    published: Bool = false",
            "}",
            "",
            "enum Role {",
            "    Admin",
            "    Editor",
            "    Viewer(since: Date)",
            "}",
            "",
            "impl Post {",
            "    fn slug(self) -> String { self.title.lower() }",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int { a + b }",
            "",
            "fn load(id: Int) -> Post ! DbError { ... }   // can fail with DbError",
            "fn main() ! { ... }                            // can fail with the default error",
            "",
            "server fn like_post(id: Int) -> Int ! web.Error { ... }   // callable from the browser",
            "```",
            "",
            "- Function parameters always have types. Closure parameters may omit them.",
            "- `where` is a keyword only in `for` loops, so it can also name a method or",
            "  field after `.`, and a method in an `impl`: `orm.find<Post>(db).where(.published)`.",
            "- Members of a `struct`, `enum` or `impl` are separated by newlines or commas.",
            "",
            "## Types",
            "",
            "| Form | Meaning |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `Int`, `String`, `web.Error` | A named type, optionally qualified by module |",
            "| `List<Post>`, `Map<String, Int>` | A generic type |",
            "| `User?` | An optional: a `User` or `none` |",
            "| `fn(Request, Int) -> Response !` | A function type; `!` means it can fail |",
            "| `{ name: String, age: Int }` | An anonymous record type, with its fields in order |",
            "",
            "There is no `null`.",
            "",
            "## Statements",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "let name = \"Kiln\"          // immutable",
            "var count: Int = 0         // mutable",
            "count += 1                 // also -=, *=, /=, =",
            "return value",
            "for post in posts where post.published { ... }",
            "for i in 0..10 { ... }                        // 0 to 9: the end is excluded",
            "while count < 10 { ... }",
            "break",
            "continue",
            "```",
            "",
            "## Expressions",
            "",
            "Precedence, from loosest to tightest:",
            "",
            "| Level | Operators |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| 1 | `expr catch err { ... }` |",
            "| 2 | `or` |",
            "| 3 | `and` |",
            "| 4 | `not` (prefix) |",
            "| 5 | `==` `!=` `<` `<=` `>` `>=` `is` |",
            "| 6 | `??` |",
            "| 7 | `..` (range) |",
            "| 8 | `+` `-` |",
            "| 9 | `*` `/` `%` |",
            "| 10 | prefix `-`, `try`, `throw` |",
            "| 11 | calls `f(x)`, generic calls `f<T>(x)`, fields `a.b`, indexing `a[i]` |",
            "",
            "Other forms:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "\"Hi \${user.name}\"                        // string interpolation",
            "250ms  2s  5m  1h                        // durations",
            "[1, 2, 3]                                // list",
            "{ posts, title: \"Home\" }                 // record; `posts` means `posts: posts`",
            "web.json(post, status: 201)              // named arguments",
            "fn(p) { p.published }                    // closure",
            ".title                                   // field shorthand, same as fn(x) { x.title }",
            "if a { 1 } else if b { 2 } else { 3 }",
            "match role {",
            "    Admin => \"admin\"",
            "    Viewer(since) if since.year < 2020 => \"veteran\"",
            "    some(x) => x",
            "    _ => \"member\"",
            "}",
            "user is some(u) and u.active             // pattern test",
            "try db.one<Post>(sql, id)                // propagate an error",
            "row ?? throw DbError.NotFound            // default, or fail",
            "```",
            "",
            "A `{` in expression position always starts a record. Blocks appear only as",
            "the bodies of functions, closures, `if`, loops, `catch` and `match` arms.",
            "",
            "## Patterns",
            "",
            "`_`, a literal, a lowercase name (binds the value), `none`, and variants:",
            "`Admin`, `Role.Admin`, `some(x)`, `Viewer(since)`.",
            "",
            "## Habits from other languages",
            "",
            "These are errors with a suggested fix, because coding agents and people",
            "coming from other languages write them by reflex. Run `kiln explain <code>`",
            "for details.",
            "",
            "| You wrote | Write instead | Code |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| `null`, `nil`, `undefined` | `none` | E0106 |",
            "| `;` at line end | nothing | E0107 |",
            "| `function`, `func`, `def`, `let mut`, `elif` | `fn`, `fn`, `fn`, `var`, `else if` | E0108 |",
            "| `===`, `!==` | `==`, `!=` | E0109 |",
            "| `&&`, `\\|\\|`, `!x` | `and`, `or`, `not x` | E0110 |",
            "",
            "## Component source files",
            "",
            "`.glaze` is server-only; `.ember` is browser-capable and may also render on the",
            "server. Both use the [template grammar](glazes.md). Reactive `{% state %}` and",
            "`on:` handlers require `.ember`; ordinary `{% let %}` stays immutable. Components",
            "resolve by their same-folder filename, with an error if both suffixes define the",
            "same name. Page layouts remain `.glaze`.",
            "",
            "Qualified tags such as `<posts.search.Result />` explicitly reference components",
            "in reachable folder modules. Folder segments are exact module paths; the final",
            "capitalized name maps to the component's snake-case filename.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "reference/projects", section: "Language reference", lines: [
            "# Projects and local modules",
            "",
            "A folder is a module. Its `.kiln` files share functions, types and constants;",
            "imports belong to the source file that writes them. Child folders are separate",
            "modules and enter the application only through explicit imports.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import posts, posts.search",
            "",
            "fn main() {",
            "    print(posts.title())",
            "    print(search.find())",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "`posts/` supplies `posts`; `posts/search/` supplies `search` (the last import",
            "segment). Paths are always relative to the project root. A folder must contain",
            "source; importing its parent does not import its children. Imports perform no",
            "route registration or other initialization: call exported setup functions.",
            "",
            "Use `pub fn`, `pub struct`, `pub enum` and `pub const` for exports. Unmarked",
            "names are private to their folder. Public signatures, exported fields/variants",
            "and inferred public constants cannot expose private nominal types. Types from",
            "different modules remain distinct, even when their names and fields match.",
            "Struct fields/constructors and enum variants retain the existing access rules.",
            "Methods need `pub fn` to be callable from another folder.",
            "Generic functions, methods, errors and typed server functions retain their",
            "ordinary behavior across module boundaries.",
            "",
            "Imports must be acyclic. E0230 reports local-module/project errors, including",
            "private access, missing modules, cycles and manifest errors. Import aliases",
            "cannot collide with other imports or declarations. Standard-library names are",
            "reserved: rename a local `web/` rather than shadowing `import web`. Paths are",
            "case-sensitive on every platform. Case-folded filename collisions and source",
            "or module symlinks are rejected. Internal module symbol prefixes are reserved.",
            "",
            "## Roots and entrypoints",
            "",
            "`kiln run folder` and `kiln build folder` select that folder as the root. They",
            "never capture a manifest above an explicitly selected directory. Without a",
            "manifest, the root folder supplies the program's single `fn main()`; `main.kiln`",
            "is the conventional filename. Multiple definitions of `main` are diagnosed.",
            "A file target includes its sibling source files, preserving existing scripts",
            "and same-folder programs. If a file is inside a manifest project, the nearest",
            "ancestor manifest establishes its import root, while the explicit file selects",
            "its entry folder.",
            "",
            "`kiln check folder` checks the reachable project. Checking a collection of",
            "independent programs (such as `kiln check examples`) still checks each program.",
            "An explicit file check uses its siblings for context and reports that file's",
            "errors plus errors in imported folders.",
            "",
            "An optional root `kiln.toml` selects an entry folder:",
            "",
            "```toml",
            "[package]",
            "name = \"fieldnotes\"",
            "kind = \"app\"",
            "entry = \"app/main.kiln\"",
            "```",
            "",
            "The entry must name an existing `.kiln` file within the root; its folder's",
            "source files form the entry module. Explicit file targets override this default.",
            "`name` and `kind` are optional strings; the only supported kind is `app`.",
            "`entry` is required when a manifest exists. Absolute paths, parent traversal,",
            "unknown keys, unsupported sections and wrong value types are errors with source",
            "locations. This initial schema is unversioned and strict. Phase 6 will extend",
            "this same file for dependencies; `[wares]`, lockfiles and workspaces are not",
            "implemented here. No manifest or dependency installation is needed for local",
            "modules.",
            "",
            "## Components and assets",
            "",
            "Inside `posts/`, `web.render(\"index.glaze\", {})`, `<Editor />` and",
            "`{% layout \"layout.glaze\" %}` refer to that module's components. A slash-bearing",
            "render/layout path is explicitly project-root-relative, such as",
            "`\"accounts/index.glaze\"`. A qualified tag such as `<posts.search.Result />`",
            "names `posts/search/result.glaze` or `.ember` in a reachable module. Folder",
            "segments retain their exact spelling; the final capitalized component name uses",
            "the existing snake-case filename convention. Other modules' same-named files",
            "cannot hijack local lookup.",
            "",
            "`.glaze` remains server-only. `.ember` retains selective hydration and typed",
            "RPC. A page without an Ember loads no Ember JS or Wasm. Stylesheet imports and",
            "scoped CSS URLs resolve relative to the owning component; CSS asset paths remain",
            "inside the project. Root `static/` files use `/static/...`; a module's files use",
            "`/static/posts/...` (including nested module segments). Duplicate asset URLs are",
            "errors. Only imported modules contribute assets.",
            "",
            "The executable dogfood application is in",
            "`crates/kiln_cli/tests/fieldnotes/`: `posts` and `accounts` register routes,",
            "module-local CSS styles an Ember form, and a typed `server fn` validates its",
            "submission. `/accounts` is an SSR-only route. Run it with:",
            "",
            "```sh",
            "kiln run crates/kiln_cli/tests/fieldnotes",
            "```",
            "",
            "The LSP follows the project graph with unsaved editor text, resolves public",
            "exports for hover/definition/completion, and keeps diagnostics on original",
            "source paths. VS Code Check/Run/Build actions select the app root within the",
            "workspace and save dirty source files throughout the project.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "reference/types", section: "Language reference", lines: [
            "# Kiln type rules",
            "",
            "These are the rules the current `kiln check` enforces. Every rule here has a",
            "test in `crates/kiln_check/tests/check.rs`. Run `kiln explain <code>` for",
            "the details of any error.",
            "",
            "## Inference",
            "",
            "- Function parameters and return types are always written. A function without",
            "  `->` returns nothing.",
            "- `let` and `var` types are inferred from the value; an annotation such as",
            "  `let n: Int = ...` checks it.",
            "- Closure parameters are inferred from where the closure is passed:",
            "  `posts.filter(fn(p) { p.published })` knows `p` is a `Post`.",
            "- Generic functions infer their type arguments from the arguments or from the",
            "  expected type. When neither says, write them: `json.decode<Post>(text)`",
            "  (E0212). Inside a generic function, its type parameters can be used in",
            "  annotations: `var best: T? = none`.",
            "",
            "## Optionals",
            "",
            "- A `T` can be used where a `T?` is expected, but not the reverse (E0202).",
            "- The branches of an `if` or `match` share one type. When one branch is",
            "  `none` or another optional, or the surrounding code expects an optional,",
            "  that type is `T?`: `if found { item } else { none }`.",
            "- Unwrap with `??`, `match`, or `if x is some(v) { ... }`. Fields and methods",
            "  of a `T?` are not available until it is unwrapped (E0208).",
            "",
            "## Records",
            "",
            "- `{ name: \"Ada\", age: 36 }` is an anonymous record of type",
            "  `{ name: String, age: Int }`; `{ name }` is short for `{ name: name }`.",
            "  Fields are read and assigned like struct fields. Two records are equal",
            "  when their fields are, and they print as they are written.",
            "- A record type is written like the record: `{ name: String, age: Int }`.",
            "  Functions take and return records, and struct fields can hold them:",
            "  `fn stats(items: List<Int>) -> { min: Int, max: Int }`. Two record types",
            "  are the same when they have the same fields, with the same types, in the",
            "  same order.",
            "",
            "## Lists, maps and sets",
            "",
            "- `[1, 2]` is a `List<Int>`; `list[i]` panics when `i` is out of range, while",
            "  `list.get(i)` returns a `T?`.",
            "- Create an empty map or set with its type: `let m: Map<String, Int> = Map()`",
            "  or `Set<String>()`. `map[key]` is a `V?` (`none` when the key is missing),",
            "  and `map[key] = value` adds or replaces an entry. `map[key] += n` (and the",
            "  other operators) updates an entry that must already be there; the program",
            "  panics if it is not.",
            "- Map keys and set items can be numbers, strings, booleans, optionals, lists,",
            "  records, structs and enums, compared by value: two structs with equal",
            "  fields are the same key. Maps, sets and functions cannot be keys. Don't",
            "  change a list or struct while it is a key.",
            "- `[3, none]` is a `List<Int?>`: like the branches of an `if`, the items of",
            "  a list share one type.",
            "- Maps and sets keep their keys in the order they were first added, so",
            "  printing and looping are predictable. Two maps are equal when they hold the",
            "  same keys with equal values, in any order.",
            "- `for x in set` loops over a snapshot of the set's items.",
            "",
            "## Errors",
            "",
            "- `fn f() -> T ! E` can fail with an `E`. A bare `!` means the standard",
            "  `Error` type.",
            "- A call that can fail needs `try` (pass the error to the caller) or",
            "  `catch err { ... }` (handle it) (E0203). `try` on a call that cannot fail is",
            "  an error (E0204).",
            "- `try` and `throw` need the enclosing function to be able to fail (E0206).",
            "- Under `! E`, only errors of type `E` can be passed on (E0205). Convert others",
            "  with `catch`:",
            "",
            "  ```kiln",
            "  let row = db.one<User>(query, id) catch err {",
            "      throw DbError.Unavailable(err.message)",
            "  }",
            "  ```",
            "",
            "  Under a bare `!`, any error can be passed on. An error that is not an",
            "  `Error` becomes one on the way, with its shown value as the `message`, so",
            "  `catch err { err.message }` always works.",
            "- A closure can fail if its body uses `try` or `throw`; this is inferred.",
            "",
            "## Calls",
            "",
            "- Arguments are positional, then named: `web.json(post, status: 201)`.",
            "  Parameters with defaults may be left out; others may not (E0209).",
            "- A struct is built by calling its name with its fields as named arguments:",
            "  `Post(title: \"Hi\")`. Fields with defaults may be left out.",
            "- Methods with `self` are called on a value (`post.slug()`); methods without",
            "  `self` are called on the type (`Post.untitled()`).",
            "",
            "## Match",
            "",
            "- `match` must cover every case of an enum, of `Bool`, and both `some` and",
            "  `none` of an optional (E0210). Other types need a `_` arm.",
            "- All arms must have the same type (E0202).",
            "",
            "## Routes",
            "",
            "- A route path's `{name}` segments become handler parameters after the",
            "  request, in order. `{id: Int}` is an `Int`, `{name}` is a `String`:",
            "",
            "  ```kiln",
            "  app.get(\"/posts/{id: Int}\", fn(req, id) { ... })   // id: Int",
            "  ```",
            "",
            "  A handler with the wrong number of parameters is E0211.",
            "",
            "## Glazes",
            "",
            "A directory is one app: its glazes see the types and functions of its `.kiln`",
            "files, and its `.kiln` files see the props of its glazes.",
            "",
            "- `{value}` must have a text form: `String`, `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`,",
            "  `Decimal`, `Duration` or `Date` (E0217). Show a field of a struct, loop",
            "  over a list, unwrap an optional (`{note ?? \"\"}`), and `{% match %}` an enum.",
            "  `{raw value}` takes a `String`.",
            "- `{% if %}` conditions are `Bool`; `{% if x is some(v) %}` binds `v` inside.",
            "  `{% for %}` loops over a `List` or `Set`. `{% match %}` must cover every",
            "  case (E0210).",
            "- `web.render(\"post.glaze\", { post })` is checked against the page's",
            "  `{% props %}`: every prop without a default must be given, with the right",
            "  type (E0209, E0202). The file must exist (E0219).",
            "- A component tag `<PostCard post={post} />` is checked the same way against",
            "  `post_card.glaze`; a text attribute gives a `String`, a bare attribute a",
            "  `Bool`. A component takes children only if it has a `{% slot %}`.",
            "  `client:load`, `client:visible`, `client:idle` and `client:only` are",
            "  hydration hints, not props.",
            "- `{% layout \"base.glaze\" title: value %}` passes props to the layout, which",
            "  must contain `{% slot %}`.",
            "- `{% fill name %}` names a `{% slot name %}` of the layout or component it",
            "  fills, once (E0311). A glaze whose layout or components (outside blocks)",
            "  lead back to itself never finishes rendering (E0310).",
            "- A value in an attribute where escaping does not make it safe is E0309:",
            "  any value in `onclick` and the other `on...` attributes or in `srcdoc`,",
            "  and a value other than an `Int` or `Float` in `style`.",
            "- `ui={...}` on an element takes a `Ui`, and a record where a `Ui` is",
            "  expected is a style: its keys and values are checked (E0220), and its",
            "  tokens must be declared (E0221). See",
            "  [Typed styles](glazes.md#typed-styles).",
            "- An ember (a glaze with `{% state %}` or an `on:` handler) runs in the",
            "  browser: `sql` and `auth` are server-only (E0218). Call a `server fn`",
            "  instead. Handlers cannot fail, and a `server fn` called from one can (the",
            "  network may), so the call takes a `catch`. Props cannot be reassigned;",
            "  state can.",
            "- What crosses between the server and the browser has a JSON form: an",
            "  ember's props and state, and a `server fn`'s parameters and result.",
            "  Functions, `Ui` styles and handles such as `web.Request` or `js.Value`",
            "  are E0224.",
            "- `browser`, `js` and npm package modules exist only in the browser: only",
            "  an ember's handlers can use them (E0223).",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "reference/glazes", section: "Language reference", lines: [
            "# Glaze syntax reference",
            "",
            "Glazes (`.glaze` files) are server-only templates and components: HTML with Kiln code in",
            "it. This is the syntax the current parser accepts; every form here is covered",
            "by a test in `crates/kiln_syntax/tests/glaze.rs`. Code inside braces is",
            "ordinary Kiln, as in [syntax.md](syntax.md).",
            "",
            "`kiln check` type-checks glazes together with the `.kiln` files in the same",
            "directory: see [types.md](types.md#glazes) for the rules.",
            "",
            "Compiled Glazes, typed `ui` styles, Finishes, CSS Modules, design tokens and",
            "themes are implemented. Browser-capable components use explicit `.ember` files",
            "and support reactive state, WasmGC hydration and typed server calls. Local",
            "[folder modules](projects.md) keep their own components, styles and assets.",
            "This page documents the current syntax; [Rendering](#rendering) describes the",
            "compiled output. Implementation history is in the [Phase 3](../plans/phase-3.md),",
            "[Phase 4.1](../plans/phase-4.1.md) and [Phase 4.2](../plans/phase-4.2.md) plans.",
            "",
            "## A page",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{# post.glaze #}",
            "{% props post: Post, user: User? = none %}",
            "{% layout \"base.glaze\" title: post.title %}",
            "",
            "<article>",
            "  <h1>{post.title}</h1>                  {# escaped automatically #}",
            "  {raw post.html}                        {# trusted HTML, not escaped #}",
            "",
            "  {% if user is some(u) and u.can_edit(post) %}",
            "    <a href=\"/posts/{post.id}/edit\">Edit</a>",
            "  {% end %}",
            "",
            "  {% for tag in post.tags where tag.visible %}",
            "    <Tag name={tag.name} />              {# the component in tag.glaze #}",
            "  {% empty %}",
            "    <p>No tags</p>",
            "  {% end %}",
            "</article>",
            "```",
            "",
            "## Output",
            "",
            "| Form | Meaning |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `{value}` | Output a value, escaped for where it appears |",
            "| `{raw value}` | Output trusted HTML without escaping |",
            "| `{# comment #}` | Dropped from the output |",
            "| `{\"{\"}` | A literal brace in text |",
            "",
            "`<script>` and `<style>` contents are raw text: braces there are CSS or",
            "JavaScript, not values.",
            "",
            "## Rendering",
            "",
            "`web.render(\"post.glaze\", { post })` renders a glaze into a `200` response",
            "with `content-type: text/html; charset=utf-8`. Glazes are compiled into the",
            "program like any other code, with nothing interpreted at run time; the",
            "glaze must be named in quotes, so the compiler knows which one it is.",
            "",
            "- **Values are escaped.** `{value}` and values in attributes have `&`, `<`,",
            "  `>`, `\"` and `'` escaped, so a value cannot add markup or end an",
            "  attribute. Values that are not strings are shown as `print` shows them.",
            "  Text written in the glaze itself is copied as written.",
            "- **Links cannot run code.** In attributes that browsers follow as URLs",
            "  (`href`, `src`, `action`, `formaction`, `poster`, `cite` and the like),",
            "  a value that decides the URL's scheme must be relative or `http`,",
            "  `https`, `mailto` or `tel`; anything else, such as `javascript:...`,",
            "  becomes `about:invalid#kiln-unsafe-url`. A value after a fixed start,",
            "  as in `href=\"/posts/{post.id}\"`, is only escaped.",
            "  HTML character references in a static prefix cannot hide a scheme:",
            "  `href=\"java&#x73;{suffix}\"` checks the decoded prefix with the value.",
            "  Only authored text is decoded; character references supplied by a value",
            "  remain literal text.",
            "- **Some attributes take no values** (E0309): `on...` attributes such as",
            "  `onclick` run JavaScript, `srcdoc` is a whole page, and `style` is CSS,",
            "  which accepts only `Int` and `Float` values (`style=\"width: {pct}%\"`).",
            "- **`{raw value}` is the trust boundary.** It outputs a `String` as HTML,",
            "  unescaped: use it only for HTML your program made or trusts, never for",
            "  text from users.",
            "- A `Bool` attribute value adds the attribute or leaves it out:",
            "  `disabled={busy}` renders `disabled` or nothing. Void elements render",
            "  without a closing tag, and `<div />` renders as `<div></div>`.",
            "- A line holding only tags and comments, such as `{% if %}` or",
            "  `{% end %}`, renders as nothing rather than a blank line, and the line",
            "  break that ends the file is left out, so a component fits inside a line.",
            "- A layout renders around the page, with the page in its `{% slot %}`; a",
            "  component renders where its tag is, with its children in its slot. A",
            "  named slot renders the matching `{% fill %}`, or nothing. Slot content",
            "  is placed without the whitespace it starts and ends with.",
            "- A glaze that always renders itself again, through its layout or",
            "  components outside `if`, `for` and `match` blocks, is E0310. Inside a",
            "  block that ends, a component can render itself, as for a tree.",
            "- Browser-capable components use `.ember`. State or `on:` handlers in a",
            "  `.glaze` are E0314; rename the interactive component to `.ember`. A Glaze",
            "  can render an Ember without becoming an island itself (see [Embers](#embers)).",
            "",
            "## Tags",
            "",
            "| Tag | Meaning |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `{% props name: Type, other: Type = default %}` | The glaze's inputs. Once, at the top level |",
            "| `{% layout \"base.glaze\" title: value %}` | Wrap this glaze in a layout. Once, at the top level |",
            "| `{% slot %}`, `{% slot name %}` | In a layout or component: where the caller's content goes |",
            "| `{% fill name %} ... {% end %}` | Content for `{% slot name %}`: directly inside a component tag, or at the top level of a page with a layout |",
            "| `{% css \"site.css\" %}` | Use a global stylesheet. At the top level |",
            "| `{% css module name from \"card.module.css\" %}` | Use a CSS Module: `name.card` is its renamed class. At the top level |",
            "| `{% state name = value %}`, `{% state name: Type = value %}` | Mutable reactive state in `.ember` files only. At the top level |",
            "| `{% let name = value %}` | A local value |",
            "| `{% if c %} ... {% else if d %} ... {% else %} ... {% end %}` | Conditional |",
            "| `{% for x in xs where c %} ... {% empty %} ... {% end %}` | Loop; `where` and `empty` are optional |",
            "| `{% match v %} {% case pattern if guard %} ... {% end %}` | Pattern match, with the patterns of `match` |",
            "",
            "Every block ends with `{% end %}`.",
            "",
            "## Layouts and slots",
            "",
            "A layout marks where pages go with `{% slot %}`, and other places with",
            "named slots, which pages fill with `{% fill %}`:",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{# base.glaze #}",
            "{% props title: String %}",
            "<head><title>{title}</title>{% slot head %}</head>",
            "<body><aside>{% slot sidebar %}</aside><main>{% slot %}</main></body>",
            "```",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{# guide.glaze #}",
            "{% layout \"base.glaze\" title: \"Guide\" %}",
            "{% fill sidebar %}<a href=\"/\">Home</a>{% end %}",
            "<h1>Guide</h1>      {# everything else fills the default slot #}",
            "```",
            "",
            "Components fill their named slots the same way, among their children:",
            "`<Card>{% fill title %}Hi{% end %}<p>Body</p></Card>`. A fill must name a",
            "slot the glaze has, once each (E0311).",
            "",
            "## Styles",
            "",
            "Glazes bring CSS three ways, and the program serves all of it itself:",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{% css \"styles/site.css\" %}                      {# global #}",
            "{% css module card from \"styles/card.module.css\" %}",
            "<article class={card.card}>",
            "    <h2 class=\"title {card.card_title}\">A pot</h2>",
            "</article>",
            "<style>",
            ".title { color: #C65A34; }                       {# only this glaze's .title #}",
            ":global(.dark) .title { color: white; }",
            "</style>",
            "```",
            "",
            "- **`<style>` is scoped.** Its rules apply only to the glaze's own",
            "  elements, which get an attribute such as `data-k-1a2b3c`, so two glazes",
            "  can both style `.title`. `:global(...)` leaves a part of a selector",
            "  unscoped. Content a glaze passes into a component's slot belongs to that",
            "  glaze. The `<style>` element itself is not in the HTML.",
            "- **`{% css \"file.css\" %}` is global**, as if linked. Its `@import`s and",
            "  `url(...)`s are followed.",
            "- **`{% css module x from \"file.module.css\" %}`** renames each class so it",
            "  cannot clash, and gives the glaze a record `x` of the new names, one",
            "  field per class with `-` written `_`: `x.card_title`. A class the file",
            "  does not have is a type error.",
            "",
            "Every `<head>` gets a `<link>` to one stylesheet built from all of this, in",
            "CSS cascade layers in a fixed order: `kiln.global`, then `kiln.modules`,",
            "then `kiln.scoped` (and `kiln.ui`, for later). CSS outside layers, such as a",
            "framework's, still wins over them as CSS says. The stylesheet and the files",
            "it refers to are served from inside the program at `/_kiln/name-hash.ext`",
            "and cached for good, since a change gives a new name. The files in the",
            "program's `static/` folder are served at `/static/...`.",
            "",
            "Paths are relative to the component's folder, and must stay inside the project. CSS",
            "that does not parse, a missing file, or an `@import` in a `<style>` or CSS",
            "Module is E0312. CSS is read with",
            "[Lightning CSS](https://lightningcss.dev), and written minified.",
            "",
            "## Typed styles",
            "",
            "`ui={{ ... }}` styles an element with a record of keys, checked by the",
            "compiler and turned into CSS classes; no class names to invent and no",
            "Tailwind:",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{% css \"design.css\" %}",
            "<article class=\"card\" ui={{",
            "    grid: true, cols: 1, gap: space.md, p: space.lg,",
            "    bg: color.surface, rounded: radius.card,",
            "    borderColor: if featured { color.accent } else { .transparent },",
            "    hover: { shadow: shadow.lg },",
            "    at: { tablet: { cols: 2 } },",
            "}}>",
            "```",
            "",
            "In Kiln code, the same record is `ui({ ... })`, of type `Ui`, and any",
            "record where a `Ui` is expected is one. A function that returns styles is",
            "a **Finish**: an ordinary function, which may take an enum to choose a",
            "variant and return a struct of several `Ui`s for a glaze's parts:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "fn button(tone: Tone) -> Ui {",
            "    let base = ui({ px: space.md, py: space.sm, rounded: radius.card })",
            "    return ui.merge(base, match tone {",
            "        Tone.Plain => ui({ bg: color.surface })",
            "        Tone.Accent => ui({ bg: color.accent, hover: { opacity: 0.9 } })",
            "    })",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "`ui.merge(a, b, ...)` combines styles; for each property, state and",
            "breakpoint the later style wins, so `px` after `p` changes only the left",
            "and right padding. `ui.classes(style)` is a style's class names as a",
            "`String`. An element's own `class` stays, before the style's classes.",
            "",
            "| Keys | Values |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `bg`, `fg` (text colour), `borderColor` | `color.*` tokens, `.transparent`, `.current` |",
            "| `p`, `px`, `py`, `pt`, `pr`, `pb`, `pl`, `gap` | `space.*` tokens, `0` |",
            "| `m`, `mx`, `my`, `mt`, `mr`, `mb`, `ml` | `space.*` tokens, `0`, `.auto` |",
            "| `w`, `h`, `minW`, `maxW`, `minH`, `maxH` | `size.*` or `space.*` tokens, `0`, `.auto`, `.full`, `.min`, `.max`, `.fit` |",
            "| `rounded` | `radius.*` tokens, `.none`, `.full` |",
            "| `border` | `true` (1px solid), or a width |",
            "| `shadow` | `shadow.*` tokens, `.none` |",
            "| `flex`, `grid` | `true` (not both in one style) |",
            "| `direction` | `.row`, `.column`, `.rowReverse`, `.columnReverse` |",
            "| `cols`, `rows` | `1` to `12` equal tracks, `.none` |",
            "| `items` | `.start`, `.center`, `.end`, `.stretch`, `.baseline` |",
            "| `justify` | `.start`, `.center`, `.end`, `.between`, `.around`, `.evenly`, `.stretch` |",
            "| `wrap`, `underline` | `true` or `false` |",
            "| `font` | `font.*` tokens |",
            "| `textSize` | `text.*` tokens; `.lg` is `text.lg` |",
            "| `weight` | `.thin`, `.light`, `.normal`, `.medium`, `.semibold`, `.bold`, `.black`, `100`-`1000`, `weight.*` |",
            "| `lineHeight` | a number, `leading.*` tokens |",
            "| `align` | `.left`, `.center`, `.right`, `.justify`, `.start`, `.end` |",
            "| `tracking` | `tracking.*` tokens |",
            "| `opacity` | `0` to `1` |",
            "| `overflow` | `.hidden`, `.auto`, `.scroll`, `.visible`, `.clip` |",
            "| `transition` | `motion.*` tokens, `.none` |",
            "| `cursor` | `.pointer`, `.default`, `.text`, `.notAllowed`, `.grab`, `.move`, `.wait`, `.help`, `.none` |",
            "| `transform` | a CSS string, `.none` |",
            "| `hover`, `focus`, `focusVisible`, `disabled` | a record of the keys above |",
            "| `at` | a record of breakpoints, each a record of keys (and states) |",
            "",
            "Every key also takes a CSS string written out, such as `p: \"1rem 2px\"`,",
            "which is checked as CSS for its property. Values are written in place:",
            "code chooses between them with `if` and `match`, but cannot compute them",
            "(E0220), because each becomes an atomic CSS class when the program is",
            "compiled. Rarer CSS properties go in a `<style>`.",
            "",
            "**Tokens** are the CSS custom properties that global stylesheets declare,",
            "named `--<category>-<name>`: `--space-md` is `space.md` and",
            "`--color-on-accent` is `color.on_accent`. The categories are `color`,",
            "`space`, `size`, `radius`, `shadow`, `font`, `text`, `weight`, `leading`,",
            "`tracking`, `motion` and `breakpoint`. A style refers to the property",
            "(`var(--space-md)`), so a stylesheet that changes it changes every style.",
            "Breakpoints are widths: `at: { tablet: ... }` is",
            "`@media (min-width: <--breakpoint-tablet>)`, in the order they are",
            "declared. A token or breakpoint that is not declared is E0221.",
            "",
            "The classes are in the `kiln.ui` layer, after the other layers, and a",
            "class name is `k` and twelve letters and digits.",
            "",
            "### Themes",
            "",
            "A theme is a value of a struct of Finishes, so the type checker checks",
            "that every theme has every part, with the right arguments and results,",
            "and the glazes take the theme as a prop:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "struct SiteTheme {",
            "    page: fn() -> PageFinish",
            "    card: fn(Bool) -> CardFinish",
            "    button: fn(Tone) -> Ui",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn editorial() -> SiteTheme {",
            "    return SiteTheme(page: editorial_page, card: editorial_card, button: editorial_button)",
            "}",
            "",
            "// The one place that chooses the theme.",
            "fn active_theme() -> SiteTheme {",
            "    return editorial()",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "A theme's token values are a rule for `.theme-<name>` in a global",
            "stylesheet, and `theme: .<name>` in a style (at its top, not inside a",
            "state or breakpoint) adds that class, so the tokens take the theme's",
            "values on the element and inside it:",
            "",
            "```css",
            ".theme-editorial { --color-accent: #c65a34; --space-md: 1rem; }",
            ".theme-vibrant   { --color-accent: #7144bf; --space-md: 0.75rem; }",
            "```",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "fn editorial_page() -> PageFinish {",
            "    return PageFinish(root: ui({ theme: .editorial, bg: color.surface }), ...)",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "Every theme must set the tokens that any other theme sets, and every",
            "token's value must suit its category (a `color.*` token is a colour, a",
            "`space.*` token a length); breakpoints are the same in every theme, so a",
            "theme cannot set them. These are E0222, reported at the `{% css %}` that",
            "loaded the stylesheet. An unknown theme is E0221. Styles that every theme",
            "could produce are all in the stylesheet, so changing `active_theme()`",
            "and building again changes the site, layout included.",
            "",
            "## Elements and components",
            "",
            "- HTML elements are written as usual. Void elements (`<br>`, `<img>`,",
            "  `<input>`, `<meta>`, `<link>`, ...) need no closing tag; any element may",
            "  close itself with `/>`.",
            "- A capitalized tag is a component: `<LikeButton post={post} />` renders",
            "  `like_button.ember` with `post` as its prop. Its children fill its",
            "  `{% slot %}`.",
            "- Attributes:",
            "",
            "  | Form | Meaning |",
            "  | --- | --- |",
            "  | `class=\"post {extra}\"` | Text, with values inside |",
            "  | `disabled={busy}` | A value |",
            "  | `hidden`, `client:visible` | A flag |",
            "  | `on:click={ count += 1 }` | An event handler: statements run in the browser |",
            "  | `on:submit.prevent.latest={ ... }` | A handler with modifiers |",
            "  | `key={item.id}` | In a loop: which item an element is, kept across updates |",
            "",
            "## Embers",
            "",
            "An Ember is a component in an **`.ember` file**. It shares the Glaze markup",
            "grammar, but may declare `{% state %}` and `on:` handlers. Ordinary `{% let %}`",
            "values remain immutable. The suffix declares the kind even without state or",
            "events; template contents never promote a `.glaze` into an Ember.",
            "",
            "`<LikeButton>` resolves `like_button.ember` or `like_button.glaze` in the same",
            "folder. Having both is an ambiguity (E0315), regardless of directory order.",
            "Hydration hints on a Glaze target are E0314. To migrate an interactive Glaze,",
            "rename its file to `.ember` and update explicit filename references; component",
            "tags keep their names. Layouts and static pages remain `.glaze`.",
            "",
            "An Ember is not client-only by default. The server",
            "renders it like any glaze, inside a `<kiln-island>` element that carries",
            "its props and state as JSON, and the browser takes it over with the",
            "program's WebAssembly (WasmGC): after each event its handlers run, it",
            "renders again, and the page's DOM is updated to match, keeping focus,",
            "typed text and keyed items. A page with no Ember loads no script, even when",
            "unused `.ember` files exist in the project. Unreferenced components are excluded",
            "from the browser build. Layout files must be `.glaze` (E0316).",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{# like_button.ember #}",
            "{% props post: Post %}",
            "{% state likes = post.likes %}",
            "{% state saving = false %}",
            "",
            "<button on:click.latest={",
            "    saving = true",
            "    likes = like_post(post.id) catch err { likes }",
            "    saving = false",
            "}>♥ {likes}{% if saving %}…{% end %}</button>",
            "```",
            "",
            "- **State.** `{% state name = value %}` declares a value the browser",
            "  can change. The server computes the first value, so it may call a",
            "  `server fn` or use server modules; the browser gets it as JSON. Props",
            "  cannot be reassigned (E0207); state can. Props and state must have a",
            "  JSON form: functions, styles and handles such as `web.Request` or",
            "  `js.Value` are E0224.",
            "- **Handlers.** `on:<event>={ statements }` runs in the browser when the",
            "  element gets the event (`click`, `input`, `change`, `submit`, `keydown`",
            "  and so on). `event` is an `ember.Event`: its `kind`, the element's",
            "  `value` and `checked`, the `key` pressed, and a form's fields as `form`.",
            "  A form's `submit` never reloads the page.",
            "- **Modifiers.** `.prevent` stops the browser's own action, `.stop`",
            "  keeps the event from outer handlers, and `.latest` lets a new event",
            "  replace a call that is still waiting. Others are E0313.",
            "- **Server functions.** A handler statement that calls a `server fn` is a",
            "  typed call to the server (`POST /_kiln/rpc/<name>`): the statements",
            "  after it run when the reply comes. The call can fail (the network, or the",
            "  function's own error), so it takes a `catch` (E0210). It must be a",
            "  statement of its own, such as `x = f(...) catch err { ... }` (E0403).",
            "  While a call is waiting, its element has `aria-busy` and",
            "  `data-k-pending`, and the same event on it is ignored unless the handler",
            "  is `.latest`, which cancels the older call. Calls time out after 30",
            "  seconds and end when the page goes.",
            "- **Server checks.** The routes accept only JSON from the page's own origin",
            "  (`Origin` and `Sec-Fetch-Site`), up to 1 MiB, from a build of the same",
            "  functions (the `x-kiln-rpc` header), and decode the arguments as",
            "  untrusted input (400 when they do not fit). A program that defines",
            "  `fn rpc_guard(req: web.Request, name: String) !` has it",
            "  called first: an error it throws is the answer, such as",
            "  `throw web.error(403, \"...\")`.",
            "- **Hydration hints**, on the component's tag: `client:load` (the",
            "  default), `client:idle` when the browser is idle, `client:visible` when",
            "  the ember is on screen, and `client:only`, which the server does not",
            "  render.",
            "- **Lists.** `key={item.id}` on the elements of a `{% for %}` keeps each",
            "  item's DOM, and the state of embers inside it, when items move.",
            "- **Nesting and slots.** An ember inside an ember keeps its own state. A",
            "  component's slot content stays the caller's: an ember can show and hide",
            "  it, but does not render it again.",
            "- **Browser code.** Handlers can use the [`browser`, `js`](stdlib.md#browser)",
            "  and [npm](stdlib.md#npm-packages) modules; they need no import. Anywhere",
            "  else these are E0223, since the server has no browser.",
            "- **Without WebAssembly** (an old browser), the page stays as the server",
            "  rendered it, with working links and forms, and `<html>` gets",
            "  `data-k-embers=\"unsupported\"` so CSS can hide controls that need it.",
            "- An ember cannot have a layout (E0316): use it as a component in a page",
            "  that has one. An ember that the browser adds (inside another ember's",
            "  `{% if %}`, say) starts from its state's first values, so their code runs",
            "  in the browser; one whose state calls a `server fn` can only be started",
            "  by the server.",
            "",
            "## Habits from other template languages",
            "",
            "These are errors with a suggested fix. Run `kiln explain <code>` for details.",
            "",
            "| You wrote | Write instead | Code |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| `{{ value }}` | `{value}` | E0304 |",
            "| `{value \\| safe}` | `{raw value}` | E0304 |",
            "| `<?php ... ?>`, `<%= ... %>` | `{value}` and `{% %}` tags | E0304 |",
            "| `{% endif %}`, `{% endfor %}` | `{% end %}` | E0303 |",
            "| `{% elif %}`, `{% elseif %}` | `{% else if %}` | E0303 |",
            "| `{% extends %}`, `{% include %}`, `{% block %}` | `{% layout %}`, a component tag, `{% slot %}` | E0303 |",
            "| `className`, `htmlFor` | `class`, `for` | E0306 |",
            "| `onClick={...}`, `@click={...}` | `on:click={...}` | E0306 |",
            "",
            "## Folder modules",
            "",
            "Same-folder components and layouts keep their owning module's context.",
            "`<posts.Editor />` explicitly names `posts/editor.ember` in a reachable module;",
            "`web.render(\"posts/index.glaze\", {})` uses a project-root-qualified path.",
            "Module-local CSS, scoped URLs and static assets retain that context. See",
            "[projects.md](projects.md) for import reachability, path rules and examples.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "reference/stdlib", section: "Language reference", lines: [
            "# Standard library",
            "",
            "The standard library's signatures are Kiln files in",
            "[`crates/kiln_check/std/`](https://github.com/IZ737/kiln/blob/main/crates/kiln_check/std). The prelude is visible",
            "everywhere; every other module is imported by name (`import fs`). This page",
            "lists the modules the compiler can build into native programs today. The",
            "parts of `web` not listed here type-check, and building a program that uses",
            "them reports E0401 until their phase arrives. `sql` and `orm` have a page",
            "of their own: [databases, models and migrations](data.md).",
            "",
            "## Prelude",
            "",
            "`print`, `env` and `panic`, and the methods of `Int`, `Float`, `String`,",
            "`Duration`, `List`, `Map` and `Set`. The full list is in",
            "[`prelude.kiln`](https://github.com/IZ737/kiln/blob/main/crates/kiln_check/std/prelude.kiln); the ones most",
            "programs reach for:",
            "",
            "| Type | Methods |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `Int` | `to_string`, `to_float`, `abs`, `min`, `max`, `pow`, `clamp(lo, hi)` |",
            "| `Float` | `to_string`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil` (to `Int`), `abs`, `sqrt`, `min`, `max`, `fixed(digits)` (`3.14159.fixed(2)` is `\"3.14\"`) |",
            "| `String` | `len`, `upper`, `lower`, `trim`, `trim_start`, `trim_end`, `contains`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`, `index_of -> Int?`, `slice(start, end)`, `split`, `lines`, `chars`, `replace`, `repeat`, `pad_start(width, fill)`, `pad_end`, `to_int -> Int?`, `to_float -> Float?` |",
            "| `List<T>` | `len`, `first`, `last`, `get -> T?`, `push`, `pop -> T?`, `insert(index, item)`, `remove_at(index)`, `contains`, `index_of`, `slice`, `take`, `skip`, `concat`, `reverse`, `sort`, `sort_by(key)`, `min`, `max`, `filter`, `map`, `flat_map`, `find`, `any`, `all`, `count`, `fold(initial, combine)`, `join` |",
            "| `Map<K, V>` | `len`, `get`, `set`, `remove`, `contains`, `keys`, `values`, `entries -> List<{ key: K, value: V }>` |",
            "| `Set<T>` | `len`, `add`, `remove`, `contains`, `to_list` |",
            "| `Duration` | `to_millis -> Int`, `to_seconds -> Float` |",
            "",
            "String positions count characters, not bytes. `slice` on a string or a list",
            "clamps its bounds to the value, while `list[i]` and `remove_at` panic when",
            "`i` is out of range. `sort`, `min` and `max` order numbers by value,",
            "strings by their bytes, `false` before `true`, `none` before any value,",
            "lists item by item, structs and records field by field in the order they are",
            "written, and enums by variant and then fields. Maps, sets and functions",
            "have no order; sort those with `sort_by`.",
            "",
            "## `time`",
            "",
            "Times are UTC. A `time.Instant` is a point in time, stored as `unix_nanos`,",
            "nanoseconds since 1970-01-01.",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `time.now() -> Instant` | The current time |",
            "| `time.unix(seconds: Int) -> Instant` | The instant `seconds` after 1970 |",
            "| `time.since(start: Instant) -> Duration` | Time passed since `start` |",
            "| `time.sleep(duration: Duration)` | Pauses the program, or in a server only the request's task |",
            "| `time.today() -> Date` | Today's date |",
            "| `time.parse(text: String) -> Instant !` | Reads RFC 3339 (`2026-10-24T21:30:00Z`, `...+02:00`) or a date alone |",
            "",
            "`Instant` methods: `unix()` (whole seconds), `add(duration)`,",
            "`since(earlier)`, `date()`, `iso()` and `format(pattern)`.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import time",
            "",
            "fn main() {",
            "    let start = time.now()",
            "    print(start.format(\"EEEE, MMMM d, yyyy 'at' HH:mm\"))  // Saturday, October 24, 2026 at 21:30",
            "    print(time.since(start).to_millis())",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "Format patterns: `yyyy` and `yy` years; `MMMM` (October), `MMM` (Oct),",
            "`MM` and `M` months; `dd` and `d` days; `EEEE` (Saturday) and `EEE` (Sat);",
            "`HH`, `H`, `hh` and `h` hours with `a` for AM or PM; `mm` minutes; `ss`",
            "seconds; `SSS` fractions of a second. Text in single quotes is copied, and",
            "`''` is a quote.",
            "",
            "## `fs`",
            "",
            "Paths are relative to the working directory. Functions that touch the disk",
            "can fail with an `Error` whose message names the path and the reason, such",
            "as `cannot read notes.txt: No such file or directory`.",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `fs.read(path) -> String !` | The whole file |",
            "| `fs.read_lines(path) -> List<String> !` | Lines without `\\n` or `\\r\\n` |",
            "| `fs.write(path, text) !` | Replaces the file, creating it if needed |",
            "| `fs.append(path, text) !` | Adds to the end, creating the file if needed |",
            "| `fs.exists(path) -> Bool` | |",
            "| `fs.is_dir(path) -> Bool` | |",
            "| `fs.list(path) -> List<String> !` | Names in a directory, sorted |",
            "| `fs.mkdir(path) !` | Creates the directory and its parents |",
            "| `fs.remove(path) !` | Removes a file or an empty directory |",
            "| `fs.rename(from, to) !` | |",
            "",
            "## `os`",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `os.args() -> List<String>` | Command-line arguments, without the program's name |",
            "| `os.env(name) -> String?` | An environment variable, or none when unset |",
            "| `os.exit(code: Int) -> Never` | Ends the program |",
            "| `os.cwd() -> String` | The working directory |",
            "| `os.temp_dir() -> String` | `\$TMPDIR`, or `/tmp` |",
            "| `os.pid() -> Int` | The process id |",
            "| `os.platform() -> String` | `\"linux\"`, `\"macos\"`, `\"windows\"` or `\"unknown\"` |",
            "",
            "## `io`",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `io.read_line() -> String?` | The next line of standard input, or none at the end |",
            "| `io.read_lines() -> List<String>` | The remaining lines |",
            "| `io.read_all() -> String` | The rest of standard input |",
            "| `io.write(text)` | Writes to buffered standard output without a newline |",
            "| `io.flush()` | Flushes pending standard output without writing text or a newline |",
            "| `io.print_err(value)` | Prints on standard error, as `print` does |",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import io",
            "",
            "fn main() {",
            "    var total = 0",
            "    for line in io.read_lines() {",
            "        total += line.to_int() ?? 0",
            "    }",
            "    print(total)",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "`fs`, `os` and `io` need the server, so embers cannot use them; the",
            "browser's modules are [`browser`](#browser), [`js`](#js) and",
            "[npm packages](#npm-packages).",
            "",
            "`io.write()` may leave text buffered. Call `io.flush()` to make a prompt",
            "visible before waiting for input; `io.read_line()` does not automatically",
            "flush standard output.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import io",
            "",
            "fn main() {",
            "    io.write(\"> \")",
            "    io.flush()",
            "    let input = io.read_line()",
            "    print(input)",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "## `web`",
            "",
            "An HTTP/1.1 server. `web.app()` makes an app, each route adds a handler,",
            "and `listen` serves them:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import web",
            "",
            "fn main() ! {",
            "    let app = web.app()",
            "    app.get(\"/posts/{id: Int}\", fn(req, id) {",
            "        if id > 100 {",
            "            return web.not_found()",
            "        }",
            "        return web.text(\"post \${id}, sorted by \${req.query(\"sort\") ?? \"date\"}\")",
            "    })",
            "    app.post(\"/posts\", fn(req) {",
            "        print(\"new post: \${req.body}\")",
            "        return web.text(\"created\", status: 201)",
            "    })",
            "    try app.listen(\":8080\")",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "**Routes.** `app.get`, `post`, `put`, `patch` and `delete` take a pattern",
            "and a handler. Each `{name}` segment of the pattern is a handler parameter,",
            "after the request: a `String` by default, or `Int`, `Float` or `Bool` with a",
            "type (`{id: Int}`). A segment that does not convert, such as `/posts/abc`",
            "for `{id: Int}`, does not match. Segments are compared after `%XX` escapes",
            "are decoded. The first route that matches the method and the path handles",
            "the request; `HEAD` requests go to `GET` routes and get no body. A path",
            "that no route matches gets 404, and a path that matches only with other",
            "methods gets 405 with an `allow` header. A handler can also be a function:",
            "`app.get(\"/\", home)` with `fn home(req: web.Request) -> web.Response !`.",
            "",
            "**Requests.** A `web.Request` has `method`, `path` (without the query",
            "string), `query_string`, `headers` (a `Map<String, String>` with names in",
            "lower case) and `body`.",
            "",
            "| Method | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `req.query(name) -> String?` | A query parameter, decoded (`+` is a space) |",
            "| `req.header(name) -> String?` | A header; the name is not case-sensitive |",
            "| `req.cookie(name) -> String?` | A cookie from the `cookie` header |",
            "| `req.json<T>() -> T !` | The body read as JSON into a `T` (see [`json`](#json)) |",
            "| `req.form<T>() -> T !` | A submitted form read into a `T` (see [`validate`](#validate)) |",
            "",
            "When `req.json` or `req.form` fails and the handler passes the error on",
            "with `try`, the server answers 400 with `{\"error\": \"...\"}` naming what was",
            "wrong, instead of 500.",
            "",
            "**Forms.** `req.form<T>()` reads a body sent as",
            "`application/x-www-form-urlencoded` or `multipart/form-data` into a",
            "struct. A form carries text, and the fields' types say what to make of it:",
            "",
            "| Field type | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `String` | The value as it was sent |",
            "| `Int`, `Float` | The value as a number, or 400 when it is not one |",
            "| `Bool` | `on`, `true`, `1` or `yes` is true; a box nobody ticked is not sent at all, so a missing `Bool` is `false` |",
            "| `T?` | `none` when the field is missing or empty |",
            "| `List<T>` | Every value sent with that name, in order |",
            "| an enum | The name of a variant without fields |",
            "",
            "A field of another type, or a field that is missing and is neither",
            "optional, a `Bool` nor a `List`, is the client's mistake and answers 400.",
            "A `multipart` part's file name is read but its bytes are kept as the",
            "field's `String`; uploads to disk are not implemented yet.",
            "",
            "**Responses.** A `web.Response` has `status`, `headers` and `body`, and can",
            "be changed before it is returned (`res.headers[\"cache-control\"] = \"no-store\"`)",
            "or made directly with `web.Response(status: 204, headers: Map(), body: \"\")`.",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `web.json(value, status: 200)` | `value` as JSON, `application/json` |",
            "| `web.text(body, status: 200)` | `text/plain; charset=utf-8` |",
            "| `web.html(body, status: 200)` | `text/html; charset=utf-8` |",
            "| `web.render(\"page.glaze\", { props })` | The glaze rendered as HTML (see [Rendering](glazes.md#rendering)) |",
            "| `web.redirect(to, status: 302)` | Sends the client to `to` |",
            "| `web.not_found()` | 404, made like the server's own (see Errors) |",
            "",
            "The server adds `content-length` and `date`, and `connection: close` when it",
            "will close the connection.",
            "",
            "**Errors.** When a handler fails, the server logs the error on standard",
            "error (`error GET /posts/7 failed: ...`) and answers 500 with `Internal",
            "Server Error`, since an error's message can hold details the client should",
            "not see; a panic is logged the same way (`... panicked: ...`). The server",
            "goes on with the next request either way.",
            "",
            "To answer with another status, fail with `web.error(status, message)`, an",
            "`Error` whose status (400 to 599) and message are for the client. It can",
            "come from any function the handler calls with `try`:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "fn find(id: Int) -> Post ! {",
            "    if id != 1 {",
            "        throw web.error(404, \"there is no post \${id}\")",
            "    }",
            "    return Post(id: 1, title: \"Firing schedules\")",
            "}",
            "",
            "app.get(\"/posts/{id: Int}\", fn(req, id) {",
            "    let post = try find(id)",
            "    return web.json(post)",
            "})",
            "```",
            "",
            "Errors of 500 and over are also logged. Error responses (from `web.error`,",
            "`web.not_found()`, 404 and 405 when no route matches, failed handlers, and",
            "requests whose body is too large or malformed) are `{\"error\": \"message\"}`",
            "when the request's `accept` or `content-type` mentions JSON, and the",
            "message as text otherwise. A request whose head the server cannot read",
            "gets a bare 400, 414 or 431 with no body, and one whose head does not",
            "arrive in time gets 408 as text. `app.on_error` makes them yourself, such as an HTML error page:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "app.on_error(fn(req, status, message) {",
            "    return web.html(\"<h1>\${status}</h1><p>\${message}</p>\", status: status)",
            "})",
            "```",
            "",
            "Its handler gets the request, the status, and the message: a `web.error`'s",
            "message, or else the status's reason, such as `Not Found`. If it panics,",
            "the server makes the response itself.",
            "",
            "**Listening.** `app.listen(address)` serves until the program ends. The",
            "address is `host:port`: `:8080` is every IPv4 address, `localhost:8080`",
            "only this machine, `[::1]:8080` an IPv6 address, and port 0 any free port.",
            "It logs `info listening on http://localhost:8080` when it starts, and fails",
            "if it cannot listen, such as when the port is in use.",
            "",
            "**Access log.** The server logs a line on standard error for each",
            "request it answers, with the method, the path without the query string,",
            "the status and the time it took: `info GET /posts/7 200 1.2ms`.",
            "",
            "**Connections.** Each connection is served on a task of its own (see",
            "[tasks](#tasks)), so a handler that sleeps or a client that sends slowly",
            "holds up only its own request. Connections stay open for more requests",
            "(HTTP/1.1 keep-alive) until the client sends `connection: close`, and a",
            "handler can close one by setting that header on its response. The HTTP",
            "itself (reading requests, chunked bodies, `expect: 100-continue`,",
            "keep-alive and writing responses) is [hyper](https://hyper.rs)'s. Header",
            "names in responses are sent in lower case, a header whose name or value",
            "HTTP does not allow is left out (with an error logged), and the",
            "`content-length` of a response is its body's length, except in answer to",
            "`HEAD`.",
            "",
            "Query strings are decoded as forms are, path segments by their `%XX`",
            "escapes, and cookies as the `cookie` header lays them out; decoded bytes",
            "that are not UTF-8 become U+FFFD.",
            "`req.form` comes later.",
            "",
            "**Settings.** `web.app()` works as it is in production, and takes",
            "settings for when the defaults do not fit:",
            "",
            "| Setting | Default | |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| `max_body` | `16_777_216` | Bytes of body; a larger request gets 413 |",
            "| `max_header` | `65_536` | Bytes of request line and headers; more, or more than 100 headers, gets 431 |",
            "| `timeout` | `10s` | How long a client has to send a request's head (408 after that), and how long it may stall while sending the body or reading the response |",
            "| `keep_alive` | `5s` | How long an idle connection stays open; `0s` closes each after one response |",
            "| `access_log` | `true` | Whether to log each request |",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "let app = web.app(max_body: 1_048_576, timeout: 30s)",
            "```",
            "",
            "### Tasks",
            "",
            "The server runs on lightweight tasks, which share one core: a task runs",
            "until it waits for the network or for `time.sleep`, and then another",
            "runs. Waiting never stops the others, but a handler that computes for a",
            "long time without waiting holds up every request until it is done.",
            "",
            "Tasks are not yet something programs create themselves. Handlers may share",
            "values, but Kiln does not promise that changing a shared value from",
            "several handlers is safe: today's single-core scheduler only switches",
            "tasks where they wait, and a later one will run tasks on several cores.",
            "Keep shared values immutable; `Mutex`, `Atomic` and channels will come for",
            "the rest.",
            "",
            "## `auth`",
            "",
            "Passwords, sessions kept in the database, and the identity of a request.",
            "A password is stored as an Argon2id hash, never in a form it can be read",
            "back from. A session is a long random value the browser keeps in a",
            "cookie, and the database holds only its digest, so a copy of the table is",
            "not a set of usable sessions.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import web, sql, orm, auth",
            "",
            "struct Account {",
            "    id: Int",
            "    email: String",
            "    password: String",
            "    role: String",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn main() ! {",
            "    let db = try sql.open(env(\"DATABASE_URL\"))",
            "    let sessions = try auth.sessions(db)",
            "    let app = web.app()",
            "",
            "    app.post(\"/sign-in\", fn(req) {",
            "        let form = try req.form<{ email: String, password: String }>()",
            "        let found = try orm.find<Account>(db).where(fn(a) { a.email == form.email }).first()",
            "        if found is some(account) and auth.verify_password(form.password, account.password) {",
            "            let home = web.redirect(\"/\", status: 303)",
            "            return try sessions.sign_in(home, account.id.to_string(), roles: [account.role])",
            "        }",
            "        // The same answer either way, so the form cannot be used to find",
            "        // out which addresses have accounts.",
            "        return web.render(\"sign-in.glaze\", { wrong: true }, status: 401)",
            "    })",
            "",
            "    app.get(\"/notes\", fn(req) {",
            "        let who = try sessions.require(req)",
            "        let notes = try orm.find<Note>(db).where(fn(n) { n.author == who.subject }).all()",
            "        return web.render(\"notes.glaze\", { notes, csrf: try sessions.csrf(req) })",
            "    })",
            "",
            "    try app.listen(\":8080\")",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `auth.hash_password(password) -> String !` | The hash to store; a different one every time |",
            "| `auth.verify_password(password, hash) -> Bool` | Whether the password is the one the hash was made from |",
            "| `auth.token(bytes: Int = 32) -> String` | A value no one can guess, for a link in an email or an API key |",
            "| `auth.sessions(db, cookie: \"kiln_session\", idle: 12h, lifetime: 720h, secure: true) -> Sessions !` | Sessions in `db` |",
            "",
            "`auth.sessions` makes its table, `kiln_sessions`, when it is not there",
            "yet, and never changes it when it is; the migrations of",
            "[`kiln db`](data.md#migrations) do not describe it, because it is the runtime's and",
            "not one of the program's models. `idle` is how long a session may sit",
            "unused and `lifetime` how long it may last at all; `0s` is no limit.",
            "Keep `secure` on, so the cookie is only ever sent over HTTPS.",
            "",
            "| Method | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `sessions.sign_in(res, subject, roles: []) -> web.Response !` | A new session, and its cookie on `res` |",
            "| `sessions.sign_out(req, res) -> web.Response !` | Ends this request's session and clears its cookie |",
            "| `sessions.sign_out_all(subject) -> Int !` | Ends every session of a subject, after a password change |",
            "| `sessions.current(req) -> Identity?` ! | Who the request is from, or `none` |",
            "| `sessions.require(req) -> Identity !` | The same, or an error the server answers 401 with |",
            "| `sessions.csrf(req) -> String !` | The token a form on this page must send back |",
            "| `sessions.check(req, token) !` | Lets the request through only with this session's token, and otherwise fails with 403 |",
            "| `sessions.sweep() -> Int !` | Deletes the sessions that are over |",
            "",
            "An `Identity` has `subject` (what `sign_in` was given, usually a user's",
            "key as text), `roles` and `started`. Identity is a request's, never the",
            "program's: a server answers many requests at the same time, so there is no",
            "ambient signed-in user.",
            "",
            "**Forms that only this site can post.** Put `sessions.csrf(req)` in a",
            "hidden field of every form that changes something, and call",
            "`sessions.check(req, form.csrf)` in the handler before the change. Signing",
            "in starts a session of its own and leaves any old one behind, so a session",
            "someone else put in the browser cannot become a signed-in one, and",
            "`sign_out_all` ends the sessions a changed password should not keep.",
            "",
            "**Who an ember's call is from.** A `server fn` whose first parameter is a",
            "`web.Request` is given the request it was called by, and the browser sends",
            "the rest:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "server fn publish(req: web.Request, id: Int) -> Bool ! {",
            "    let who = try sessions.require(req)",
            "    ...",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "<button on:click={ done = publish(post.id) catch err { false } }>Publish</button>",
            "```",
            "",
            "## `validate`",
            "",
            "Checking submitted input. A rule is a function that returns the problem it",
            "found, or `none` when the value is fine, so a program's own functions are",
            "rules too: `validate.check` keeps the problems that are there.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import web, validate",
            "",
            "struct Signup {",
            "    name: String",
            "    email: String",
            "    age: Int",
            "    plan: String",
            "    newsletter: Bool",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn main() ! {",
            "    let app = web.app()",
            "    app.post(\"/signup\", fn(req) {",
            "        let input = try req.form<Signup>()",
            "        let checked = validate.check([",
            "            validate.required(\"name\", input.name),",
            "            validate.length(\"name\", input.name, min: 2, max: 60),",
            "            validate.email(\"email\", input.email),",
            "            validate.range(\"age\", input.age, 13, 120),",
            "            validate.one_of(\"plan\", input.plan, [\"free\", \"paid\"]),",
            "            validate.when(input.plan == \"paid\" and not input.newsletter,",
            "                \"newsletter\", \"a paid plan comes with the newsletter\"),",
            "        ])",
            "        if not checked.valid {",
            "            // The same form again, with the problems in it.",
            "            return web.render(\"signup.glaze\", { input, checked }, status: 422)",
            "        }",
            "        return web.redirect(\"/welcome\", status: 303)",
            "    })",
            "    try app.listen(\":8080\")",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "A form the server cannot read at all is the client's mistake, and answers",
            "400; input that reads but breaks a rule is the person's, and the handler",
            "shows the form again, usually with 422.",
            "",
            "| Rule | The problem it finds |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `validate.required(field, value)` | Empty, or only spaces |",
            "| `validate.length(field, value, min: 0, max: 0)` | Too short or too long in characters; a `max` of 0 is no limit |",
            "| `validate.range(field, value, min, max)` | A number outside the range |",
            "| `validate.email(field, value)` | Not an email address. An empty value is for `required` |",
            "| `validate.one_of(field, value, choices)` | Not one of the choices |",
            "| `validate.same(field, value, other)` | Two values that must match, such as a password and its confirmation |",
            "| `validate.when(wrong, field, message)` | `message`, when `wrong`. For a rule of the program's own |",
            "| `validate.problem(field, message)` | A `Problem` directly, for a check that is not a rule |",
            "",
            "`validate.check(problems) -> Checked` keeps the problems in the order they",
            "were written. A `Checked` has `valid` and `problems` (a",
            "`List<Problem>`, each with a `field` and a `message`), `checked.error(field)`",
            "for the first message about one field, next to the field in a Glaze, and",
            "`checked.messages()` for every message.",
            "",
            "Rules run on the server, where every write is checked. An ember gets the",
            "same answers by calling a `server fn` that runs these rules, rather than",
            "checking in the browser, which a client can skip.",
            "",
            "## `html`",
            "",
            "Rendered glazes escape every value themselves; these are for HTML a",
            "program builds by hand.",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `html.escape(text) -> String` | `&`, `<`, `>`, `\"` and `'` as entities, safe in HTML text and quoted attributes |",
            "| `html.safe_url(url) -> String` | The URL when it is relative or `http`, `https`, `mailto` or `tel`, otherwise `about:invalid#kiln-unsafe-url` |",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import html, web",
            "",
            "fn greeting(name: String) -> web.Response {",
            "    return web.html(\"<p>Hello, \${html.escape(name)}</p>\")",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "Escaping is the [html-escape](https://github.com/magiclen/html-escape)",
            "crate's, in the runtime's Rust part. `safe_url` finds the scheme as",
            "browsers do, ignoring leading spaces and control characters and tabs or",
            "line breaks inside it, so `java\\tscript:` is not safe either.",
            "",
            "## `base64`",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `base64.encode(data) -> String` | The bytes of `data` in standard Base64, with `=` padding |",
            "| `base64.decode(text) -> String !` | The bytes `text` encodes; fails when it is not Base64 |",
            "",
            "The decoded bytes need not be UTF-8, so a decoded image can be a",
            "response's body. Programs carry their binary files (see",
            "[Styles](glazes.md#styles)) this way. The runtime uses the",
            "[base64](https://github.com/marshallpierce/rust-base64) crate.",
            "",
            "## `json`",
            "",
            "JSON is derived from types when the program is compiled; there is no",
            "reflection at run time.",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `json.encode(value) -> String` | `value` as compact JSON |",
            "| `json.decode<T>(text) -> T !` | Reads `text` as a `T` |",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import json",
            "",
            "struct Post {",
            "    id: Int",
            "    title: String",
            "    tags: List<String>",
            "    summary: String?",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn main() ! {",
            "    let post = try json.decode<Post>(\"{\\\"id\\\": 1, \\\"title\\\": \\\"Hi\\\", \\\"tags\\\": []}\")",
            "    print(post.summary ?? \"no summary\")        // no summary",
            "    print(json.encode(post))  // {\"id\":1,\"title\":\"Hi\",\"tags\":[],\"summary\":null}",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "| Kiln | JSON |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `String`, `Bool` | string, `true` or `false` |",
            "| `Int` | an integer; decoding rejects fractions and numbers past 64 bits |",
            "| `Float` | a number; infinities and NaN encode as `null` |",
            "| `Duration` | an integer number of nanoseconds |",
            "| `T?` | `null` when none |",
            "| `List<T>`, `Set<T>` | an array (sets of strings or integers decode) |",
            "| `Map<K, V>` | an object; `Int` keys are written as strings |",
            "| structs, records | objects with a member per field |",
            "| enum variants | the name as a string (`\"Dot\"`) when it has no fields, otherwise `{\"Circle\": {\"radius\": 1.5}}`, `{\"Some\": 3}` for one unnamed field, or `{\"Rect\": [2, 3]}` for several |",
            "",
            "When decoding, members a struct does not have are ignored, and a missing",
            "field is an error unless its type is optional (it becomes `none`); a",
            "field's default value is not used yet. Errors say what was expected and",
            "where: `cannot read JSON: expected a string at \$.tags[1], found a number`,",
            "or `invalid JSON at line 1, column 10: ...` for text that is not JSON.",
            "",
            "The encoder and decoder are [serde_json](https://github.com/serde-rs/json),",
            "in the runtime's Rust part. Text to decode must be UTF-8, and string",
            "escapes must be well formed (a `\\u` surrogate needs its other half);",
            "encoding writes bytes of a string that are not UTF-8 as U+FFFD. Values",
            "nested past 128 levels in the type being read are rejected; nesting in",
            "members that are skipped has no limit and cannot overflow the stack.",
            "",
            "## `markdown`",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `markdown.to_html(text) -> String` | The HTML for CommonMark text, with GitHub's tables, strikethrough and task lists |",
            "",
            "HTML written in the Markdown is shown as text, links and images keep only",
            "the URLs `html.safe_url` keeps, and headings get ids from their words",
            "(`## Getting started` is `<h2 id=\"getting-started\">`), so `#getting-started`",
            "links to it. The result is safe to place in a glaze with `{raw ...}`:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import markdown, web",
            "",
            "fn about(text: String) -> web.Response {",
            "    return web.render(\"page.glaze\", { body: markdown.to_html(text) })",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{% props body: String %}",
            "<article>{raw body}</article>",
            "```",
            "",
            "The runtime uses the [pulldown-cmark](https://github.com/pulldown-cmark/pulldown-cmark)",
            "crate.",
            "",
            "## `browser`",
            "",
            "The browser, for embers. Only an ember's `on:` handlers can use it (E0223",
            "elsewhere), and they need no `import`.",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `browser.log(message)`, `browser.warn(message)` | Writes to the browser's console |",
            "| `browser.title() -> String`, `browser.set_title(title)` | The page's title |",
            "| `browser.location() -> String`, `browser.navigate(url)` | The page's address, and going to another (an address that would run script is refused) |",
            "| `browser.now() -> Float` | Milliseconds since the page started loading |",
            "| `browser.confirm(message) -> Bool` | Asks the user with the browser's dialog |",
            "| `browser.get_item(key) -> String?`, `browser.set_item(key, value) !`, `browser.remove_item(key)` | The site's local storage |",
            "| `browser.element(selector) -> js.Value?` | The first element that matches a CSS selector |",
            "| `browser.focus(selector)` | Moves the focus to the first element that matches |",
            "| `browser.fetch(url) -> String !` | GETs a URL and gives its text; an answer other than 2xx is an error. Waits |",
            "| `browser.copy(text) !` | Puts text on the clipboard. Waits |",
            "",
            "A function that waits is called like a `server fn`: as a statement of its",
            "own in a handler, whose later statements run when it is done.",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{% props text: String %}",
            "{% state copied = false %}",
            "<button on:click={",
            "    browser.copy(text) catch err { return }",
            "    copied = true",
            "}>{% if copied %}Copied{% else %}Copy{% end %}</button>",
            "```",
            "",
            "## `js`",
            "",
            "JavaScript values, for what `browser` and npm bindings cannot reach. A",
            "`js.Value` is a handle on a value that stays in the browser; it cannot be",
            "state, a prop or a `server fn` argument (E0224). Turning one into a Kiln",
            "value is checked, and fails with an error rather than guessing.",
            "",
            "| Function | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `js.global(name) -> js.Value` | `globalThis[name]`, such as `js.global(\"document\")` |",
            "| `js.string(text)`, `js.float(n)`, `js.bool(b)`, `js.null()` | JavaScript values of Kiln ones |",
            "| `value.get(name) -> js.Value`, `value.set(name, v)` | A property (`undefined` when there is none) |",
            "| `value.call(method, args = []) -> js.Value !` | Calls a method; an exception is an error |",
            "| `value.as_string() !`, `as_float() !`, `as_int() !`, `as_bool() !` | Checked conversions; `as_int` needs a whole number in `Int`'s range |",
            "| `value.is_nullish() -> Bool`, `value.type_of() -> String` | `null` or `undefined`, and `typeof` |",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "let doc = js.global(\"document\")",
            "let title = doc.get(\"title\").as_string() catch err { \"?\" }",
            "let n = doc.as_float() catch err { 0.0 }   // err.message: \"expected a number, found an object\"",
            "```",
            "",
            "## npm packages",
            "",
            "`kiln add npm:<package>@<version>` adds a browser library for embers:",
            "",
            "```sh",
            "kiln add npm:nanoid@5.0.9",
            "```",
            "",
            "It downloads the package from the registry (`KILN_NPM_REGISTRY`, or",
            "npm's), checks the tarball against the registry's sha512 integrity and runs",
            "none of its scripts. It keeps, in `npm/<package>/`, only the package's",
            "browser ES module and the files it imports, its TypeScript declarations,",
            "`package.json` and its licence, and writes `npm.lock` with the SHA-256 of",
            "each. A build refuses a file that changed. A package that imports other",
            "packages, uses Node.js modules (`node:fs`) or is CommonJS only is refused,",
            "with why.",
            "",
            "The declarations become a module named after the package",
            "(`canvas-confetti` is `canvas_confetti`, `@scope/name` is `scope_name`),",
            "which ember handlers use without an import. `string` is `String`,",
            "`number` is `Float`, `boolean` is `Bool`, `any` and `unknown` are",
            "`js.Value`, `T | null`, `T | undefined` and optional parameters are",
            "optional, and arrays of strings, numbers and booleans are lists; names",
            "become snake case. Every",
            "call can fail, as JavaScript can throw. A function that returns a",
            "`Promise` waits, like a `server fn`. A constant is a function with no",
            "arguments. Functions and constants Kiln cannot map yet (callbacks,",
            "overloads, other types) are left out, and `kiln add` and the checker say",
            "why; classes and interfaces are not bound yet, and `js.Value` reaches",
            "them.",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{% state id = \"\" %}",
            "<button on:click={ id = nanoid.nanoid(10.0) catch err { err.message } }>New id</button>",
            "<code>{id}</code>",
            "```",
            "",
            "The build serves the files at `/_kiln/npm/<package>@<version>/`, and the",
            "page loads them with the embers.",
            "",
            "## Adding to the standard library",
            "",
            "1. Declare the signature in `crates/kiln_check/std/<module>.kiln`, and list a",
            "   new module in `STD_MODULES` in `crates/kiln_check/src/checker.rs`.",
            "2. For the compiler, add the module to `LIB_MODULES` in",
            "   `crates/kiln_ir/src/lower.rs`; its calls become `ExprKind::Lib`. Build",
            "   each function in the runtime (`crates/kiln_codegen/runtime/kiln.c`, as a",
            "   `K_API` function) and in both backends: `lib_call` in",
            "   `crates/kiln_codegen/src/emit.rs` for C, and `lib_call` in",
            "   `crates/kiln_native/src/gen.rs` for native code, with the function's C",
            "   signature in `crates/kiln_native/src/runtime.rs`. A runtime function that",
            "   fails sets `k_lib_error` to its message. The runtime is also built for",
            "   Windows with mingw-w64: put code that differs there under",
            "   `#ifdef _WIN32`. Generated code that calls a C library function directly",
            "   (such as `round`) needs it listed in `kiln_keep_*` in",
            "   `crates/kiln_native/runtime/native.c`, so the Windows runtime has it.",
            "3. Add a conformance program.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "reference/data", section: "Language reference", lines: [
            "# Databases, models and migrations",
            "",
            "Kiln reaches a SQL database two ways, and they share one connection.",
            "[`sql`](#sql) is what the database speaks: a query written out, with its",
            "values sent apart from its text. [`orm`](#orm) is what the program means:",
            "a model is an ordinary struct, and a query on it is Kiln the compiler",
            "turns into SQL while it builds the program. [`kiln db`](#migrations)",
            "writes the migrations the models ask for, and reads a database that",
            "already exists into models.",
            "",
            "SQLite needs nothing on the system: `sqlite:` databases are files, or",
            "memory. `postgres://` and `mysql://` URLs are read and reported as not",
            "supported yet, rather than silently behaving like something else.",
            "",
            "## `sql`",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import sql",
            "",
            "fn main() ! {",
            "    let db = try sql.open(env(\"DATABASE_URL\"))",
            "    let posts = try db.query<Post>(\"select * from posts where published = \$1\", true)",
            "    let one = try db.one<Post>(\"select * from posts where id = \$1\", id)",
            "    let many = try db.scalar<Int>(\"select count(*) from posts\")",
            "    let changed = try db.exec(\"delete from posts where id = \$1\", id)",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "| | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `sql.open(url, max_connections: 4, busy_timeout: 5s) -> Db !` | A pool of connections, not one socket per query |",
            "| `db.query<T>(sql, ...) -> List<T> !` | Every row as a `T` |",
            "| `db.one<T>(sql, ...) -> T? !` | The first row, or `none` |",
            "| `db.scalar<T>(sql, ...) -> T !` | One value from one row; no row is an error |",
            "| `db.exec(sql, ...) -> Int !` | The number of rows it changed |",
            "| `db.insert<T>(table, value) -> T !` | Stores a value and returns it with the key the database made |",
            "| `db.transaction<T>(fn(tx) -> T !) -> T !` | Commits when the function returns, rolls back when it fails |",
            "| `db.dialect() -> String` | `\"sqlite\"`, `\"postgres\"` or `\"mysql\"` |",
            "| `db.close()` | Closes the pool's connections |",
            "| `sql.error_kind(err) -> String` | What kind of failure an error was (below) |",
            "",
            "Values go in `\$1`, `\$2`, ... whatever the database, and the extra",
            "arguments fill them in order; the driver writes the placeholders each",
            "database wants. **A value is never written into the SQL**, so a query",
            "cannot be changed by what a person types. A table or column name is not a",
            "value: write those out, or map them in a model.",
            "",
            "A row is read into the type the call names: a struct or record takes a",
            "column per field by name, and a single type (`Int`, `String`) takes the",
            "first column. A nullable column fits an optional. An `Instant` or a `Date`",
            "is stored as text in UTC (`2026-10-26T21:30:00Z`), and any other value",
            "with a JSON form is stored as its JSON.",
            "",
            "`sql.error_kind(err)` tells the kinds apart, so a handler can answer a",
            "duplicate email differently from a database that is not there:",
            "`connection`, `syntax`, `unique`, `foreign_key`, `not_null`, `check`,",
            "`constraint`, `decode`, `busy`, `transaction`, `unsupported` and `other`.",
            "Errors name the table and column the database named, and never the values",
            "that were sent.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "try db.transaction(fn(tx) ! {",
            "    try tx.exec(\"update accounts set balance = balance - \$1 where id = \$2\", amount, from)",
            "    try tx.exec(\"update accounts set balance = balance + \$1 where id = \$2\", amount, to)",
            "})",
            "```",
            "",
            "Everything inside a transaction runs on its connection, `orm` queries",
            "included. SQLite takes one writer at a time: a busy database is tried",
            "again until `busy_timeout` is over, and other requests keep running while",
            "it waits.",
            "",
            "## `orm`",
            "",
            "A model is an ordinary struct. `Post` is stored in the table `posts` (the",
            "name in snake case, in the plural), each field in the column of its name,",
            "and `id` is its key.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import orm",
            "",
            "struct Post {",
            "    id: Int",
            "    title: String",
            "    summary: String? = none",
            "    published: Bool = false",
            "    author_id: Int",
            "    // Relations are not columns.",
            "    author: Author? = none",
            "    comments: List<Comment> = []",
            "}",
            "",
            "let published = try orm.find<Post>(db)",
            "    .where(.published)",
            "    .where(fn(p) { p.title.starts_with(term) })",
            "    .order_by(.id, descending: true)",
            "    .limit(20)",
            "    .all()",
            "```",
            "",
            "| | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `orm.find<T>(db) -> Query<T>` | A query to build on |",
            "| `orm.get<T>(db, key) -> T? !` | The row with that key |",
            "| `orm.insert<T>(db, value) -> T !` | Stores a row and returns it with its new key |",
            "| `orm.update<T>(db, value) -> Int !` | Writes a row back by its key |",
            "| `orm.delete<T>(db, value) -> Int !` | Deletes a row by its key |",
            "",
            "| Step | |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| `.where(condition)` | Kept rows; several `where`s are `and` |",
            "| `.order_by(.field, descending: false)` | The order, which must be written here, not worked out |",
            "| `.limit(n)`, `.offset(n)` | A page of rows |",
            "| `.include(.relation)` | Loads a relation for every row, in one more query |",
            "| `.all()`, `.first()`, `.count()`, `.delete()` | Runs the query |",
            "| `.sql()` | The SQL it became, to read while writing it |",
            "",
            "**The conditions are compiled, not run.** `where` and `order_by` are read",
            "while the program is built: fields, literals, comparisons, `and`, `or`,",
            "`not`, arithmetic, and `contains`, `starts_with` and `ends_with` on text",
            "become SQL. A misspelled field or a condition that cannot become SQL is an",
            "error at `kiln check` (E0232), not a surprise at runtime. Any part that",
            "does not name the model is worked out in Kiln first and sent as a value.",
            "A query must be built and run in one chain, so that what runs is what the",
            "compiler read.",
            "",
            "**Relations are loaded, never guessed.** A field `author: Author?` next to",
            "`author_id` is the `Author` whose key is `author_id`, and",
            "`comments: List<Comment>` the comments whose `post_id` is this post's key.",
            "They are not columns: give them defaults, and load them with `include`,",
            "which fetches them for every row of the page in one more query. Reading a",
            "relation that was not included gives what the default says, never a",
            "hidden query.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "let page = try orm.find<Post>(db).include(.author).include(.comments).limit(20).all()",
            "```",
            "",
            "**A table that does not follow the conventions** is mapped once, anywhere",
            "in the program:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "const posts = orm.model<Post>(",
            "    table: \"blog_posts\",",
            "    columns: { title: \"post_title\" },",
            "    generated: [.id],      // the database fills these in",
            "    readonly: [.created],  // never written by insert or update",
            ")",
            "```",
            "",
            "A name that cannot be used as it is written — a reserved word, or not in",
            "`[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*` — is an error (E0231) that says to map it, rather than",
            "quoting identifiers differently per database behind the program's back.",
            "",
            "## Migrations",
            "",
            "`kiln db` keeps the database and the models in step. It never changes a",
            "database while a program builds, runs or starts.",
            "",
            "```sh",
            "kiln db status                        # what is applied, pending, or drifted",
            "kiln db diff --name add_post_summary  # writes the next migration",
            "kiln db migrate                       # applies the pending ones",
            "kiln db verify                        # checks the files against the ledger",
            "kiln db pull --out models.kiln        # models from a database that exists",
            "```",
            "",
            "The database comes from `--url`, or `DATABASE_URL`. `--path` picks the",
            "program's folder.",
            "",
            "`diff` compares the models with `migrations/schema.json`, the snapshot of",
            "what the migrations describe, and writes the difference as SQL in",
            "`migrations/sqlite/0001_add_post_summary.sql` for you to read and commit.",
            "A change that would throw data away — dropping a table or a column — is",
            "refused unless `--allow-destructive` asks for it. A new column that cannot",
            "be null is written with a default, so the rows already there stay valid.",
            "",
            "`migrate` applies each file once, in order, inside a transaction, and",
            "writes what it applied to a `kiln_migrations` table with the file's",
            "SHA-256. A migration that has run is history: editing or removing the file",
            "is an error, and so is a new migration numbered before one that has",
            "already been applied. `--dry-run` says what it would do.",
            "",
            "`status` and `verify` report what is pending and what the database has",
            "that no migration describes, which is how a change someone made by hand",
            "shows up instead of being quietly overwritten.",
            "",
            "`pull` reads a database that already exists and writes ordinary Kiln",
            "models for its tables, with a `<table>_id` field for each foreign key. It",
            "never overwrites a file that is there, and it changes nothing in the",
            "database. `pull --check` reports what the database has that the models do",
            "not.",
            "",
            "The `kiln_sessions` table [`auth`](stdlib.md#auth) keeps its sessions in",
            "is the runtime's own, not one of the program's models, so the migrations",
            "do not describe it.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "guides/editors", section: "Guides", lines: [
            "# Editor setup",
            "",
            "`kiln lsp` is a language server (LSP) that talks over standard input and",
            "output. A file is understood together with the other `.kiln`, `.glaze` and `.ember`",
            "files in its module and the folders it imports, so glazes see local types and",
            "pages see their components' props. Project discovery respects workspace bounds",
            "and optional manifests; the editor's unsaved text is used for open files.",
            "See [projects](../reference/projects.md) for module paths and visibility. It gives:",
            "",
            "- **Diagnostics**: the same errors as `kiln check`, while you type, with",
            "  each error's suggested fix as a quick fix.",
            "- **Completion**: fields and methods after `value.`, functions and types",
            "  after `module.`, variants after `Enum.`, the locals in scope and the",
            "  program's functions, types and constants, types where a type is written,",
            "  and standard library modules not imported yet (choosing one adds the",
            "  `import`). It works in glazes' `{...}` code too.",
            "- **Hover**: the type of a name or expression, a function's signature, a",
            "  type's fields or variants, and the doc comment (`//` lines just above the",
            "  declaration), including for the standard library.",
            "- **Go to definition**: for locals, functions, types, fields, methods and",
            "  variants, across the files of the program.",
            "- **Styles**: in a `ui(...)` or `ui={{ ... }}` record, completion offers",
            "  the style keys (with the CSS properties each sets), breakpoints inside",
            "  `at`, the design tokens after `space.` or `color.`, the keywords after",
            "  `.` and the themes after `theme: .`; CSS Modules' class names complete",
            "  after the module's name. Hover shows what a key sets and a token's",
            "  custom property and value, and go to definition opens the stylesheet",
            "  where a token or theme is declared.",
            "- **Symbols**: the outline of a file (document symbols), and the",
            "  declarations of every `.kiln` file in the workspace (workspace symbols).",
            "",
            "Code that does not parse yet still gets answers: whatever the parser",
            "recovered is analyzed, so completing `post.` on an unfinished line works.",
            "Find references, rename and deeper Glaze semantics come in phase 6; a",
            "debugger is not part of phase 2.5. See [the phase 2.5 plan](../plans/phase-2.5.md).",
            "",
            "Install the `kiln` binary first:",
            "",
            "```sh",
            "cargo install --path crates/kiln_cli",
            "```",
            "",
            "## VS Code",
            "",
            "The extension in [editors/vscode](https://github.com/IZ737/kiln/blob/main/editors/vscode) starts `kiln lsp` for",
            "you, highlights `.kiln`, `.glaze` and `.ember` files, gives them Kiln's file icons,",
            "and adds **Kiln: Check Program**, **Run Program** and **Build Program**",
            "commands and tasks. It finds `kiln` on your `PATH` or in `~/.kiln/bin`, or",
            "where the `kiln.path` setting says; its README shows how to install it. Its",
            "TextMate grammars (`editors/vscode/syntaxes`) also work in other editors",
            "that read them, such as Sublime Text.",
            "",
            "## Neovim (0.11 and later)",
            "",
            "```lua",
            "vim.filetype.add({ extension = { kiln = \"kiln\", glaze = \"glaze\", ember = \"ember\" } })",
            "vim.lsp.config(\"kiln\", {",
            "  cmd = { \"kiln\", \"lsp\" },",
            "  filetypes = { \"kiln\", \"glaze\", \"ember\" },",
            "  root_markers = { \".git\" },",
            "})",
            "vim.lsp.enable(\"kiln\")",
            "```",
            "",
            "With Neovim's default mappings, quick fixes are under `gra`, hover under",
            "`K`, go to definition under `<C-]>`, the outline under `gO` and completion",
            "under `<C-x><C-o>`.",
            "",
            "## Helix",
            "",
            "In `~/.config/helix/languages.toml`:",
            "",
            "```toml",
            "[language-server.kiln]",
            "command = \"kiln\"",
            "args = [\"lsp\"]",
            "",
            "[[language]]",
            "name = \"kiln\"",
            "scope = \"source.kiln\"",
            "file-types = [\"kiln\", \"glaze\", \"ember\"]",
            "comment-token = \"//\"",
            "language-servers = [\"kiln\"]",
            "```",
            "",
            "## Zed, Sublime Text, Emacs and others",
            "",
            "Any editor with a generic LSP client works: run `kiln lsp` for files ending",
            "in `.kiln`, `.glaze` and `.ember`. For Emacs `eglot`:",
            "",
            "```elisp",
            "(define-derived-mode kiln-mode prog-mode \"Kiln\")",
            "(add-to-list 'auto-mode-alist '(\"\\\\.\\\\(kiln\\\\|glaze\\\\|ember\\\\)\\\\'\" . kiln-mode))",
            "(add-to-list 'eglot-server-programs '(kiln-mode . (\"kiln\" \"lsp\")))",
            "```",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "design", section: "Project", lines: [
            "# Kiln design overview",
            "",
            "This is a summary of the language design. Decisions here can still change",
            "until 1.0; open questions are listed at the end. The broader product",
            "philosophy, frontend/backend developer experience, performance escape",
            "hatches, interoperability and adoption strategy are in",
            "[Kiln's long-term vision](vision.md). The [README roadmap](../README.md#roadmap)",
            "controls phase sequencing; aspirational ideas in the vision are not",
            "automatically commitments for 1.0.",
            "",
            "**Implementation status (v0.9.0):** native Linux/Windows builds, HTTP routing,",
            "JSON, server-rendered Glazes, typed styles/themes, explicit WasmGC Embers,",
            "typed RPC, browser/npm interop and local folder modules are implemented.",
            "SQL/ORM, sessions/auth/admin, Wares, public `kiln test`, hot reload and the",
            "`kiln fire`/deployment workflow remain planned. The goals and examples below",
            "include future design; use the [current references](/docs/#language-reference)",
            "for supported syntax and APIs.",
            "",
            "## Core principle",
            "",
            "Most web development today is gluing together problems that were solved",
            "long ago (routing, templates, the ORM, auth, forms, the admin, asset builds,",
            "deployment) and sometimes reinventing one. Kiln removes that work: every one",
            "of those pieces ships with the language and they already fit together, so a",
            "project that follows the conventions needs no configuration and no glue",
            "code. The conventions are defaults, not walls. Any piece can be configured",
            "in depth, or replaced, where it is used. Every design decision is checked",
            "against this rule.",
            "",
            "## Progressive integration",
            "",
            "Kiln provides a consistent developer experience whether used for an",
            "individual component, one application layer or an entire full-stack",
            "application.",
            "",
            "The compiler uses all available information to provide type checking, code",
            "generation and optimization. Full-stack Kiln applications benefit from",
            "deeper automatic integration, while applications containing external systems",
            "use explicit, typed contracts and adapters to unlock as many of the same",
            "capabilities as possible.",
            "",
            "No application feature should unnecessarily require ownership of the entire",
            "technology stack.",
            "",
            "## Developer experience, performance and interoperability",
            "",
            "**Frontend development matters as much as backend development.** Kiln aims",
            "to unify typed forms and validation, accessible Glaze components, native",
            "CSS, Ember interactivity, server functions and data access so full-stack",
            "developers do not recreate the glue between separate frontend and backend",
            "frameworks. The compiler should share types and generate the routine",
            "communication, while leaving explicit low-level APIs available.",
            "",
            "**Optimize hotspots, not whole applications.** Ordinary Kiln stays",
            "garbage-collected. Future compiler optimization, optional arena/region",
            "allocation and more specialized numeric/data-layout operations should allow",
            "performance-sensitive code to stay in Kiln where practical, with compiler",
            "checks preventing memory-lifetime errors. These advanced mechanisms are",
            "later design explorations, not mandatory ownership rules.",
            "",
            "**Interoperability goes both ways.** Kiln should call existing libraries,",
            "and existing applications should be able to call Kiln modules. Proposed",
            "paths include C-compatible FFI, generated Python/Node bindings, portable",
            "Wasm components and browser JavaScript/npm integration through typed",
            "bindings. For Phase 4 browser Embers, the finalized direction is **typed",
            "browser/npm bindings by default, with a deliberate checked `js.Value`",
            "dynamic escape hatch**; Kiln developers need not author JavaScript. Mixed",
            "stacks must remain viable, and interop boundaries must handle memory,",
            "errors, security, lifecycle and ownership explicitly. See the",
            "[Phase 4 interop contract](plans/phase-4.md#8-javascript-and-browser-interoperability).",
            "",
            "The complete reasoning and future scope live in [vision.md](vision.md).",
            "",
            "## Goals",
            "",
            "1. **One binary deploys**, with templates and assets embedded.",
            "2. **Batteries included for the web**: HTTP/1.1 and 2, TLS, routing,",
            "   templating, JSON, forms, SQL with an ORM, a generated admin UI, sessions,",
            "   auth and background jobs.",
            "3. **Errors at compile time**: routes, template variables, SQL results and",
            "   JSON shapes are type-checked.",
            "4. **Safe by default**: auto-escaping templates, parameterized SQL, signed",
            "   cookies, CSRF protection on.",
            "5. **Fast edit loop**: sub-second debug builds with live reload.",
            "6. **No async/await split**: every request runs on a lightweight thread.",
            "7. **One language for server and browser**: components render on the server",
            "   and become interactive through WebAssembly only where needed.",
            "8. **Easy for coding agents**: an LLM that has never seen Kiln can write",
            "   correct code from the material shipped with the compiler.",
            "",
            "Non-goals for 1.0: systems programming, an interpreter, and reimplementing",
            "the JavaScript ecosystem (browser code calls npm packages through generated",
            "bindings).",
            "",
            "## Types",
            "",
            "- Static types with local inference. Function signatures are always typed.",
            "- Primitives: `Int` (64-bit), `Float`, `Bool`, `String` (UTF-8), `Bytes`,",
            "  `Decimal` for money, `Duration` (`250ms`, `2s`).",
            "- `List<T>`, `Map<K, V>`, `Set<T>`; structs with defaults; enums with payloads",
            "  and exhaustive `match`; structural interfaces; generics by monomorphization.",
            "- No null. `T?` is optional and the missing value is `none`.",
            "- Errors are values: `fn f() -> T ! E`, propagated with `try`, handled with",
            "  `catch`. A bare `!` uses the standard `Error` and accepts any error; `! E`",
            "  only passes on errors of type `E`. See [types.md](reference/types.md). Panics are for bugs only, and the HTTP server recovers them per",
            "  request.",
            "- JSON, form and SQL encoding are derived at compile time, with no runtime",
            "  reflection.",
            "",
            "## Programs and folders",
            "",
            "A folder is one module. All of its `.kiln` files share one",
            "namespace, so splitting a file in two never needs an import, and the",
            "folder's glazes see the same types and functions. Imports stay per file.",
            "",
            "**Phase 4.2:** [multi-folder projects and local modules](plans/phase-4.2.md) moves project structure **before Phase 5 data/ORM/auth**. A subfolder is a separate local module, imported with `import posts` (nested: `import posts.search`), and `pub` exports its API. Same-folder `.kiln` files still share declarations, but imports remain per source file. Compiler resolution, type checking, builds, Glazes/Embers and assets, diagnostics and LSP navigation share folder-module binding; local imports require no registry, Ware installation, lockfile or compulsory manifest.",
            "",
            "**Phase 4.2 completion:** A manifest-free multi-folder application builds and runs offline with protected module visibility, deterministic path/cycle/name diagnostics and working module-local server Glazes and client Embers. The optional minimal `kiln.toml` may identify a project root and entrypoint; no project file is necessary for an unambiguous simple or multi-folder program.",
            "",
            "### Phase 4.2: minimal project manifest",
            "",
            "A declarative optional `kiln.toml` helps select the app entrypoint without procedural configuration or scripts:",
            "",
            "~~~toml",
            "[package]",
            "name = \"fieldnotes\"",
            "kind = \"app\"",
            "entry = \"main.kiln\"",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "Explicit CLI arguments take precedence over manifest defaults. Manifest-relative entry paths must stay inside the project root; parsing/diagnostics are source-linked. No plaintext secrets, per-folder manifests or automatic dependency installation. `kiln-deploy.toml` remains separate in Phase 7.",
            "",
            "See the [project reference](reference/projects.md) for the implemented root, entry, path and visibility rules.",
            "",
            "### Phase 6: extending the project manifest for Wares",
            "",
            "The [Phase 6 implementation plan](plans/phase-6.md) **reuses** Phase 4.2's folder/import/visibility model and extends the existing `kiln.toml` schema with package version/publishing identity, build/test defaults and versioned local/Git/registry **Wares**. It also adds `kiln.lock`: the resolved external dependency graph, exact releases/commits and integrity/source information for reproducible builds. Ordinary local folder modules require neither `[wares]` nor a lockfile.",
            "",
            "Illustrative future Phase 6 extension (these `[wares]` names and fields are proposals, not current working syntax):",
            "",
            "~~~toml",
            "[package]",
            "name = \"fieldnotes\"",
            "version = \"0.1.0\"",
            "kind = \"app\"",
            "entry = \"main.kiln\"",
            "",
            "[wares]",
            "\"kiln/ui\" = \"0.1\"",
            "\"example/markdown\" = \"1.2\"",
            "",
            "[build]",
            "target = \"linux\"",
            "",
            "[test]",
            "browser = true",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "`kiln add` / `kiln remove` should mutate the manifest and lockfile together, with explicit safe resolution. **`kiln upgrade` is for upgrading the compiler**, not Ware dependencies. Dependency verification and publishing do not execute arbitrary install scripts. Future `[workspace]` and multi-package publishing are optional later scope, not requirements for Phase 4.2 or basic Phase 6 Wares.",
            "",
            "### Phase 6: global Ware storage and caching",
            "",
            "**Decision:** Wares are stored in **one shared, machine-local cache per",
            "operating-system user** by default, **not** copied to a per-project",
            "`ware_modules/`, `node_modules/` or similar directory. Projects keep",
            "their small declarative `kiln.toml` and exact `kiln.lock` only; resolution",
            "finds an immutable Ware in the global store using the lockfile's source,",
            "version and verified content hash. Multiple projects can share the same",
            "cached package version without having to install it repeatedly. Multiple",
            "versions of a Ware must coexist safely and each project resolves its own",
            "dependency graph.",
            "",
            "- **Scope and paths:** Choose platform-standard per-user cache locations",
            "  (Windows: a suitable LocalAppData directory; Linux: the XDG cache",
            "  location, falling back to the user's home cache directory; macOS:",
            "  platform-standard cache directory). Make the root inspectable and",
            "  optionally configurable for CI and constrained/offline machines.",
            "  A truly **cross-user, machine-wide** cache can be explored later, but",
            "  needs stricter permissions, ownership and trust boundaries; don't",
            "  require admin privileges or share writable cache files across users.",
            "- **Store identity:** Use content-addressed, immutable package content,",
            "  verified by hashes and registry/source metadata. Download to temporary",
            "  files and atomically publish only after integrity verification; reject",
            "  tampered, incomplete or mismatched content. Use safe cross-process",
            "  coordination so simultaneous builds cannot corrupt a shared cache.",
            "  Cached source is untrusted until checked; prevent path traversal or",
            "  archive extraction outside its dedicated content directory.",
            "- **Project isolation:** The cache never determines the selected",
            "  dependency version. `kiln.lock` does. Two apps can depend on different",
            "  versions independently, and installing/removing a Ware in one project",
            "  must not alter another's lockfile or resolved graph. Unversioned",
            "  **local path modules** remain live source files and are not copied",
            "  into the immutable global Ware cache.",
            "- **Compiled artifacts:** Optionally cache compiled Ware/object outputs",
            "  separately from immutable source, keyed by content hash **plus** Kiln",
            "  compiler/runtime version, target triple, feature set, optimization and",
            "  relevant build configuration. Never reuse incompatible artifacts.",
            "- **Offline/reproducible builds:** An offline or locked mode such as",
            "  `kiln build --offline` must use locally available, verified locked",
            "  Wares without contacting the network; report a missing hash/version",
            "  clearly rather than silently upgrading or downloading alternatives.",
            "  CI should be able to prefill and reuse the same cache.",
            "- **Maintenance UX (proposed commands):** `kiln cache dir`,",
            "  `kiln cache stats`, `kiln cache verify` and `kiln cache prune`.",
            "  Cache pruning should preview candidates or make its effect explicit,",
            "  and avoid deleting data in active use. Stale package contents may be",
            "  evicted and fetched again later; do not promise to detect every",
            "  project on the machine or assume an unused version can never be",
            "  needed for offline builds.",
            "- **Deployment:** Compiler outputs remain self-contained. Production",
            "  executables must not need access to the developer's cache, Wares",
            "  registry or network to load their compiled Ware code at runtime.",
            "",
            "**Phase 6 acceptance:** Two unrelated projects locked to the same Ware",
            "version/hash download it **once** and share verified cached contents;",
            "one project can pin a different version without affecting the other;",
            "offline rebuild works after cache priming; integrity corruption is",
            "detected; concurrent cache access is safe; `kiln cache` offers usable",
            "inspection and pruning; and no per-project dependency directory is",
            "needed to run or build an application.",
            "",
            "## Memory and concurrency",
            "",
            "- A concurrent, non-moving, tri-color mark-and-sweep GC, as in Go. Phase 1",
            "  links the Boehm collector; phase 7 replaces it with a precise collector.",
            "- Escape analysis, and later request-scoped arenas for garbage that dies when",
            "  a response is sent.",
            "- An M:N scheduler of lightweight tasks; blocking I/O parks the task. Tasks",
            "  spawned in a request are cancelled when it ends. Values shared between tasks",
            "  must be immutable or wrapped in `Mutex`, `Atomic` or a channel.",
            "- Phase 2 runs tasks on one core, cooperatively: a task gives way only",
            "  where it waits. Programs must not rely on that. Changing a shared",
            "  mutable value from several tasks (such as a `var` captured by two",
            "  handlers) works today but is not a guarantee, and the compiler may later",
            "  reject it, so that tasks can move to several cores without changing",
            "  what programs mean.",
            "",
            "## Compilation",
            "",
            "The compiler is written in Rust. Source is parsed, type-checked and lowered",
            "to a typed mid-level IR, which feeds a backend:",
            "",
            "| Backend | Use | Phase |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| C (via clang, gcc or `zig cc`) | First backend; free cross-compilation | 1 |",
            "| Cranelift | Self-contained native compilation; fast debug builds | 1.5 |",
            "| WasmGC | Browser code (embers) | 4 |",
            "| LLVM | Optimized release builds | 7 |",
            "",
            "The IR (`crates/kiln_ir`) is a typed tree: names are resolved, closures and",
            "field shorthands are lifted into functions with their captures listed (a",
            "captured `var` lives in a heap cell), call arguments are in parameter order",
            "with defaults filled in, and every place a value changes representation,",
            "such as an `Int` stored as a list item, is an explicit coercion. Generic",
            "functions and methods are monomorphized here: each use gets a copy for its",
            "type arguments (`show<Int>`, `show<Post>`), so generic code can print,",
            "compare and hash its values. It stays",
            "a tree of blocks, loops and `if`s rather than a control-flow graph, so the",
            "C backend maps it almost line for line; later backends can flatten it.",
            "`kiln build --emit-ir` writes its readable form.",
            "",
            "The phase 1 C backend (`crates/kiln_codegen`) emits one C file with the",
            "runtime (`runtime/kiln.c`) in front, and builds it with `\$CC` or `cc`",
            "against the Boehm collector. Values keep their natural C types (`int64_t`,",
            "`double`, `bool`, pointers); list, map and set items, the fields of generic",
            "structs and enums, and closure arguments use a uniform 64-bit slot, so the",
            "runtime's collection code is shared by every item type. A call that fails sets a",
            "thread-local flag that `try` and `catch` check. Parts of the language the",
            "backend does not support yet are reported as E0401.",
            "",
            "The native backend (`crates/kiln_native`, phase 1.5) generates machine code",
            "from the same IR with Cranelift. For Linux it links the code into a static",
            "executable with the wild linker, run inside `kiln`. For Windows, `kiln`'s",
            "build links the runtime into an executable with no base relocations, and",
            "`kiln` adds each program's code and data to a copy of it as new sections,",
            "applying the relocations itself and writing the program's entry points into",
            "a table the runtime's `main` reads. Values are stored as in the C backend",
            "and both call the same runtime, which `kiln`'s build compiles once per",
            "target, with the Boehm collector and a C library (musl for Linux, the",
            "`msvcrt` that ships with Windows), for `kiln` to carry. Struct fields",
            "are slots, so a struct is an array of them. Where the C backend writes a",
            "show, equality, hash or ordering function for each type, the native one",
            "writes a description of the type into the executable's data, and runtime",
            "functions read it. Both backends report the same E0401 errors, and the",
            "conformance programs run with both.",
            "",
            "### Phase 1.5: self-contained toolchain and native executables",
            "",
            "Phase 1.5 comes after the working C backend and before phase 2's HTTP work.",
            "Its goal is **zero installation-time toolchain and runtime dependencies for",
            "end users**, not eliminating libraries from Kiln's implementation:",
            "",
            "- Ship a prebuilt `kiln` executable that can compile supported CLI programs",
            "  without requiring a separately installed C compiler, linker, Rust toolchain",
            "  or garbage collector. Building the compiler itself from source may still",
            "  use Rust, Cargo and third-party libraries.",
            "- Bring the Cranelift backend forward from a later phase to generate native code",
            "  directly from Kiln's typed IR. Provide an integrated or bundled linking",
            "  path rather than invoking a system-installed linker. Retain the C backend",
            "  as a supported alternative and correctness reference.",
            "- Bundle and statically link the Kiln runtime, Boehm GC and required system",
            "  support into generated executables. Kiln's standard-library API remains",
            "  available; users simply do not install its implementation separately.",
            "- Target Linux x86-64 first, with an explicit follow-up for native Windows",
            "  x86-64. Windows should ship a standalone `kiln.exe` that generates Windows",
            "  `.exe` programs using a bundled or integrated PE/COFF linking path and",
            "  a bundled Kiln runtime and GC. Neither compilation nor execution should",
            "  require users to install a C compiler, linker, Rust, GC or redistributable",
            "  runtime package. Normal system libraries included with a supported Windows",
            "  installation are allowed. Other platforms may follow separately.",
            "- Run the existing phase 1 conformance suite against the new backend and",
            "  verify the installation guarantee in clean environments; add Windows CI",
            "  coverage as the Windows target is implemented.",
            "",
            "**Done when:** On a clean, compatible Linux x86-64 machine, downloading",
            "one `kiln` executable is sufficient to compile supported CLI programs",
            "without installing build tools or runtime libraries, and the resulting",
            "executables run in a compatible minimal environment without separately",
            "installed shared libraries (for example, a `FROM scratch` container).",
            "Compiler-from-source dependencies do not count against this goal.",
            "",
            "**Windows follow-up (not a gate on phase 1.5 or phase 2):** On a clean,",
            "compatible Windows x86-64 machine, downloading only `kiln.exe` is sufficient",
            "to compile supported CLI programs to Windows `.exe` files, and the outputs",
            "run without separately installing development tools, Boehm GC or additional",
            "runtime redistributables. Validate with the same conformance programs in",
            "Windows CI. The C backend can remain available separately where its external",
            "toolchain dependencies are acceptable.",
            "",
            "**Progress.** The Cranelift backend, the built-in linker and the bundled",
            "runtime work on Linux x86-64 and are the default there: every conformance",
            "program gives the same output as with the C backend, a build runs with no",
            "`PATH`, and CI runs the executable in an empty root directory (no libc, no",
            "`/proc`). `kiln` itself is built against musl into one static executable",
            "(about 18 MB) that runs on any Linux x86-64 system. CI and every release",
            "run all the conformance programs with it inside an empty `FROM scratch`",
            "container (`scripts/clean-check.sh`), and pushing a `v*` tag publishes it",
            "as a GitHub release. That meets the phase 1.5 goal for Linux x86-64.",
            "",
            "For Windows, `kiln build --target windows` makes `.exe` files that use only",
            "`kernel32`, `msvcrt`, `user32` and `ws2_32` (sockets), with a manifest that makes file names,",
            "arguments and the console UTF-8. `kiln.exe` is cross-compiled from Linux",
            "(`x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`) and carries both runtimes, as the Linux `kiln`",
            "does, so each builds for both systems. CI runs every conformance program",
            "with `kiln.exe` on a Windows machine with no C compiler on the `PATH`",
            "(`scripts/windows-check.sh`), and releases ship it next to the Linux",
            "`kiln`.",
            "",
            "### What phase 1 leaves for later",
            "",
            "Phase 1 is done when native CLI programs compile and run correctly; the",
            "programs in `conformance/` are the evidence. These gaps are known and",
            "deliberate:",
            "",
            "- The `sql` and `auth` module stubs type-check but report E0401 when built; their implementation belongs to Phase 5, after Phase 4's browser codegen and RPC.",
            "  Phase 2 has started with `web`'s server and routing; see below.",
            "- Built-in methods cannot have default arguments yet, so `pad_start` and",
            "  `pad_end` take their fill text explicitly.",
            "- `upper` and `lower` change only ASCII letters.",
            "- Maps, sets and functions cannot be map keys or sorted by value.",
            "- Methods cannot be used as values (`ps.map(P.get)`); write a closure.",
            "- The collector is Boehm's conservative one until phase 7.",
            "",
            "### Phase 2: the HTTP server",
            "",
            "Phase 2 is done when a real HTTP API runs as a native binary, and it is",
            "done: [examples/api](https://github.com/IZ737/kiln/blob/main/examples/api/main.kiln) is one, and the `web_*`",
            "conformance programs run as servers on Linux and Windows with both",
            "backends. It was built in steps, each on both backends and both targets:",
            "",
            "1. **The server and routing (done).** `web.app()`, routes with typed",
            "   parameters (`/posts/{id: Int}`), `text`, `html`, `redirect` and",
            "   `not_found` responses, request headers, query parameters, cookies and",
            "   bodies, and `listen`. The server is HTTP/1.1, written in the shared",
            "   runtime (`kiln.c`) over the system's sockets (Winsock on Windows). At",
            "   first it served one connection at a time; step 3 changed that. A",
            "   handler that fails or panics gets a 500 response and the error is",
            "   logged; the server keeps running. Requests with no matching route get",
            "   404, and a path that matches with another method gets 405 with `allow`.",
            "2. **JSON (done).** `json.encode`, `json.decode<T>`, `web.json` and",
            "   `req.json<T>()`. The compiler writes a description of each type",
            "   involved (the same `kdesc` the native backend uses for showing and",
            "   comparing, now also written by the C backend), and one encoder and",
            "   decoder in the runtime walk it, so both backends behave the same. A",
            "   body that `req.json` cannot read gets 400 with the reason as JSON.",
            "3. **Concurrency (done).** Each connection runs on a lightweight task, so",
            "   a slow request does not hold up the others, and connections are kept",
            "   alive. Tasks are cooperative and share one core: the scheduler in",
            "   `kiln.c` switches stacks where a task waits for a socket or for",
            "   `time.sleep`, and polls the sockets (`poll`, `WSAPoll` on Windows) when",
            "   no task is ready. Sockets never block. The collector scans the stacks",
            "   of waiting tasks. Running tasks on several cores is a later milestone;",
            "   the runtime's task functions (spawn, wait, sleep) and Kiln's future",
            "   task API are meant to stay the same when it comes, which is why Kiln",
            "   does not promise that tasks can share mutable values (see Memory and",
            "   concurrency).",
            "4. **Errors and settings for real deployments (done).** `web.error(status,",
            "   message)` is a standard `Error` that the server answers with its status",
            "   and message; the runtime marks it with hidden slots after the `Error`'s",
            "   field, so it keeps its status through `try` and `catch`. Other errors",
            "   get 500 and keep their message out of the response. Error responses are",
            "   JSON for clients that speak JSON, or come from `app.on_error`.",
            "   `web.app` takes the limits, the timeout, the keep-alive time and",
            "   whether to keep an access log, each with a default that suits",
            "   production.",
            "",
            "### Phase 2.5: VS Code developer experience (done)",
            "",
            "Phase 2.5 follows the completed HTTP milestone and precedes phase 3's",
            "frontend work. It improves the editing and feedback loop without changing",
            "Kiln's language or moving Glaze rendering ahead of phase 3.",
            "",
            "- Build a dedicated VS Code extension with `.kiln` and `.glaze` language",
            "  registration, highlighting and brand-aligned file icons.",
            "- Use `kiln lsp` as the single source of language intelligence: existing",
            "  diagnostics and quick fixes, plus completion, hover types and signatures,",
            "  go to definition and symbol navigation for Kiln programs.",
            "- Provide VS Code commands for checking, building and running Kiln projects,",
            "  with a configurable compiler path and clear error/output reporting.",
            "- Preserve the LSP's usefulness for other editors. Test unsaved files,",
            "  shared-directory programs, incomplete source and Linux/Windows behavior.",
            "- Defer debugging (including DAP), rename and find references, advanced",
            "  Glaze semantics, formatting, hot reload and broader tooling to separate",
            "  future milestones (with the existing phase 6 scope retained).",
            "",
            "**Done when:** A developer can install the extension and comfortably",
            "write, navigate, check, build and run Kiln programs from VS Code without",
            "setting up the LSP manually, while other LSP editors benefit from the",
            "same compiler-backed language features.",
            "",
            "For the feature boundaries, implementation stages and validation plan, see",
            "[the phase 2.5 plan](plans/phase-2.5.md).",
            "",
            "## Web standard library",
            "",
            "`web` (server, router, static files), `template` (glazes), `json`, `form`,",
            "`validate`, `sql` (SQLite first, so local development needs no database",
            "server, then Postgres and MySQL; migrations), `orm`, `admin`, `session`, `auth`",
            "(Argon2, CSRF, rate limiting, JWT, OAuth 2/OIDC), `http.client`, `log`,",
            "`metrics`, `trace`, `cache`, `jobs`, `ws`, `sse`, `mail`, `crypto`, `time`,",
            "`fs`, `os`, `env`, `io`, `text`, `test`.",
            "",
            "### ORM",
            "",
            "See the [Phase 5 implementation specification](plans/phase-5.md) for the",
            "proposed typed query APIs, dialect handling, schema import, migrations,",
            "validation, security and acceptance gates. Examples in that plan are",
            "**target syntax**, not current executable features.",
            "",
            "The `orm` module ships with the standard library, so a database-backed site",
            "needs no third-party package.",
            "",
            "- **Models are ordinary structs.** Table and column names are derived",
            "  (`Post` maps to `posts`, `author_id` to a foreign key) and can be",
            "  overridden. Relations are typed fields: `author: User` and",
            "  `comments: List<Comment>` load on request, never implicitly in a loop.",
            "- **Typed queries.** `orm.find<Post>(db).where(.published).order_by(.created).limit(20)`",
            "  is checked at compile time: a misspelled field or a comparison of the",
            "  wrong type is a build error. Raw SQL stays available through `sql.query<T>`.",
            "- **Migrations from models.** `kiln db diff` compares models with the database",
            "  and writes a migration; `kiln db migrate` applies it.",
            "- **Existing databases.** `kiln db pull` reads the schema of an existing",
            "  Postgres, MySQL or SQLite database, including keys, foreign keys,",
            "  indexes, defaults and enums, and generates `.kiln` model files with their",
            "  relations. The generated models are normal code to edit; later pulls",
            "  report drift instead of overwriting changes.",
            "",
            "### Admin UI",
            "",
            "The `admin` module generates a Django-style admin site from ORM models, in",
            "one line:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "app.mount(\"/admin\", admin.site(db, models: [Post, User, Comment]))",
            "```",
            "",
            "- List, search, filter, create, edit and delete pages for every model, with",
            "  forms and validation derived from field types and relations.",
            "- Protected by `auth`, with per-model permissions.",
            "- **Fully customizable, in layers:** per-model options in code (columns,",
            "  search fields, filters, read-only fields, custom actions); any field",
            "  widget replaced by a component; and any page replaced by placing a glaze",
            "  at the matching path, such as `admin/post/edit.glaze`. The admin is built",
            "  from glazes, so overrides use the same component system as the rest of the",
            "  site.",
            "",
            "## Glazes and embers",
            "",
            "`.glaze` declares a server-only component; `.ember` declares a browser-capable",
            "component that renders initial HTML on the server and hydrates selectively.",
            "Only `.ember` permits reactive state and event handlers. Both use the same",
            "markup grammar. See the [implemented source contract](reference/glazes.md#embers).",
            "",
            "Glazes (`.glaze`) are templates compiled to typed functions, with",
            "context-aware escaping, typed props, layouts, components and reverse routing.",
            "Phase 3 implements server-side Glaze rendering without a browser runtime.",
            "Phase 4 adds Embers compiled to WebAssembly (WasmGC) that hydrate selected",
            "components; `server fn` and generated typed RPC belong to that phase,",
            "**without relying on SQL, ORM, sessions or auth**.",
            "See the [Phase 4 implementation plan](plans/phase-4.md).",
            "Phase 5 adds database-backed and authenticated uses of those RPC contracts,",
            "sharing validation across SSR and reactive forms.",
            "",
            "### Phase 3: native styling, Finishes and themes",
            "",
            "See [the full Phase 3 plan](plans/phase-3.md) for the ordered implementation steps,",
            "exact scope and acceptance tests:",
            "",
            "- Glazes may co-locate markup, logic, scoped `<style>` CSS and typed",
            "  `ui` expressions, or import ordinary CSS and CSS Modules.",
            "- Native style values use a built-in `Ui` type, with concise properties",
            "  such as `bg`, `fg`, `p`, `gap` and `rounded`. In Glazes use",
            "  `ui={{ bg: color.surface, p: space.lg }}`; in Kiln functions construct",
            "  the same type with `ui({ ... })`. Styles compile to standard CSS.",
            "- **Finishes** are ordinary functions that return `Ui` or records of",
            "  named `Ui` parts, accept typed parameters/enums and compose through",
            "  deterministic `ui.merge`. No special `finish` keyword or DSL.",
            "- **Themes** provide compatible Finish functions and typed CSS design",
            "  tokens. Switching the selected theme and rebuilding can transform",
            "  colours, typography and layout while keeping Glazes/content unchanged.",
            "- The compiler/LSP will understand style values, tokens, Finish functions,",
            "  CSS Modules and CSS imports. Kiln's docs site is the Phase 3 dogfood",
            "  acceptance target.",
            "",
            "These are Phase 3 *targets*, not currently implemented syntax. Embers,",
            "reactive style updates, SQL/auth, Wares and SSG remain later milestones.",
            "",
            "### Static site generation",
            "",
            "The same app can be built as a static site, for blogs, docs and marketing",
            "pages that need no server. Planned for phase 7:",
            "",
            "- `kiln fire --static` renders every route to HTML files, with assets, ready",
            "  for any static host or CDN. Routes with parameters list their pages:",
            "  `app.get(\"/posts/{id: Int}\", show_post).static(fn() { all_post_ids() })`.",
            "- Mixed apps prerender the routes marked `.static(...)` at build time and",
            "  serve the rest dynamically from the same binary, optionally re-rendering a",
            "  static page on a schedule (`.static(..., refresh: 10m)`).",
            "- Embers still work on static pages: they hydrate in the browser as usual.",
            "  Server functions need a server, so a fully static export reports any ember",
            "  that calls one as a build error.",
            "",
            "### Installable apps (PWAs)",
            "",
            "Planned for phase 8+, after 1.0. Marking an app installable",
            "(`app.pwa(name: \"Notes\", icon: \"icon.png\")`) makes `kiln fire` generate the",
            "web manifest, icons at every size and a service worker that caches the app",
            "shell and assets, versioned with each release. A standard library `local`",
            "module gives embers a typed store in the browser (IndexedDB underneath) for",
            "local-first data: changes queue while offline, and a `sync` layer pushes",
            "them to the server and pulls other changes when the connection returns, with",
            "a conflict rule chosen per model (last write wins, merge fields, or a custom",
            "function). The same ORM models describe both sides. Push notifications and",
            "background sync come with it.",
            "",
            "## Ecosystem and application delivery",
            "",
            "Planned Wares are versioned source packages using the optional",
            "[`kiln.toml` project manifest](#phase-6-project-manifests-and-ware-dependencies)",
            "and generated `kiln.lock`; fetching them should not run arbitrary package",
            "install scripts. Official Wares are intended to be especially auditable and",
            "self-contained. Project templates remain a separate mechanism. A future",
            "Ware registry and Kiln's own documentation website are important",
            "dogfooding projects, not just showcase demos.",
            "",
            "The proposed `kiln deploy` and `kiln release` workflows should work with",
            "existing infrastructure defined in `kiln-deploy.toml` rather than attempt",
            "to replace Terraform. They should coordinate builds, migrations, process",
            "activation and health checks with an inspectable plan and explicit",
            "confirmation (with a flag for noninteractive CI); database rollback and",
            "destructive changes need separate safeguards. See",
            "[the full vision](vision.md#8-one-command-builds-releases-and-deployment)",
            "for the intended operating model. These commands are not promises of",
            "current implementation.",
            "",
            "## Editor support",
            "",
            "`kiln lsp` is a language server (LSP) for any editor. The first version, in",
            "phase 1, reports the same diagnostics as `kiln check` as you type and offers",
            "their suggested fixes as quick fixes; [editors.md](guides/editors.md) shows how to",
            "set it up. Phase 2.5 added completion, hover types and signatures, go to",
            "definition and symbol navigation, plus a VS Code extension with",
            "Kiln/Glaze highlighting, icons and Check/Run/Build commands. These language features stay in the Rust",
            "LSP and benefit other LSP clients. Phase 6 expands refactoring (find",
            "references and rename), deep Glaze prop/component navigation and the rest",
            "of the developer workflow. Debugging remains a separate later milestone.",
            "",
            "## Integrated testing (Phase 6)",
            "",
            "Kiln's planned `kiln test` command will run **unit**, **integration** and",
            "**real-browser frontend/end-to-end** test suites through one native tooling",
            "experience, using Kiln-authored assertions and shared fixtures. The",
            "compiler/toolchain should own test discovery, isolated app startup,",
            "results, screenshots and CI output. Browser automation should reuse",
            "an established browser/protocol implementation rather than inventing",
            "another browser or requiring a separate Node testing project.",
            "",
            "Phase 4 adds real-browser CI smoke and conformance tests for Embers,",
            "including hydration, browser events, reactive state and typed RPC.",
            "Phase 5 extends that coverage with authenticated database-backed RPC and",
            "shared client/server validation.",
            "Phase 6 delivers the public `kiln test` runner for unit, integration and",
            "real-browser tests, including Ember behaviour from the outset.",
            "Browser binaries may need downloading/caching and are not bundled into",
            "Kiln server executables. Detailed scope and acceptance criteria:",
            "[testing.md](plans/testing.md).",
            "",
            "## Built for coding agents",
            "",
            "- Familiar syntax and one way to do each thing, so model priors transfer.",
            "- Targeted errors for habits from other languages (`null`, `;`, `&&`,",
            "  `function`), each with a machine-applicable fix.",
            "- `kiln check --json` and `kiln explain <code>` from phase 0; later an agent",
            "  guide (`kiln docs --llm`, `llms.txt`), a generated `AGENTS.md` in new",
            "  projects, an MCP server (`kiln mcp`), task examples tested in CI, and an",
            "  agent benchmark that gates releases.",
            "",
            "## Moving the runtime off C",
            "",
            "The network-facing parts of the runtime (HTTP, JSON, later TLS) are moving",
            "from hand-written C to maintained Rust libraries (hyper, serde_json,",
            "rustls), with hyper running on Kiln's own tasks. JSON and the HTTP server",
            "have moved. The plan, its rules and",
            "its order are in [runtime-rust.md](plans/runtime-rust.md).",
            "",
            "The runtime's Rust part is `crates/kiln_rt`, a static library that every",
            "program links next to the C runtime, for both backends and both targets.",
            "It exports C functions, so the backends call it like the rest of the",
            "runtime.",
            "",
            "## Open questions",
            "",
            "- Should a `.glaze` page with inline code be able to serve a route directly",
            "  (PHP style), or should every route go through a handler?",
            "- Whether the exact WasmGC browser support matches the plan should be",
            "  confirmed before Phase 4's WasmGC implementation.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "vision", section: "Project", lines: [
            "# Kiln — long-term vision",
            "",
            "> **Write the business logic, not the glue.**",
            "",
            "Kiln is an experimental, compiled, garbage-collected, web-first programming",
            "language and integrated application platform. The motivating question is:",
            "**what if building a full-stack application felt as cohesive as writing one",
            "program, while producing fast native executables that are simple to deploy?**",
            "",
            "Kiln is not intended to invent new versions of every solved problem. Routing,",
            "forms, validation, SQL, authentication, background jobs, frontend components",
            "and deployments already exist. The opportunity is to make them **fit together",
            "by default**, remove repetitive configuration and duplicated type",
            "definitions, and let developers focus on the parts unique to their",
            "applications.",
            "",
            "This document describes the **destination and design principles**, not a",
            "claim that every capability exists today. For implementation status and",
            "milestones see the [README roadmap](../README.md#roadmap); for detailed type,",
            "compiler and API decisions see [design.md](design.md). The concrete Phase 2.5",
            "editor milestone has its [own plan](plans/phase-2.5.md). Syntax and commands labeled",
            "*proposed* below are not guaranteed to work yet. Decisions remain changeable",
            "before 1.0.",
            "",
            "## 1. Product identity",
            "",
            "Kiln aims to combine:",
            "",
            "- **High-level productivity:** familiar, readable syntax and a",
            "  batteries-included application framework, closer to the convenience of",
            "  Django than to assembling a library stack by hand.",
            "- **Native performance:** ahead-of-time compilation and an efficient runtime,",
            "  with Go-like performance as an aspiration to validate through benchmarks,",
            "  not an established guarantee.",
            "- **A coherent full stack:** one type system and set of conventions for",
            "  backend, server-rendered UI, interactive browser components and tooling.",
            "- **Simple distribution:** one downloadable compiler for supported targets,",
            "  and one self-contained executable for a deployed server application.",
            "- **Progressive adoption:** use one Kiln function, module, service, frontend",
            "  component or a whole application; never require a wholesale rewrite.",
            "",
            "Kiln is **web-first**, but should remain enjoyable for CLI tools and other",
            "small programs. Desktop apps, games and TUIs are possible future extensions,",
            "not reasons to delay the web platform.",
            "",
            "The ambition is not \"another language that can build a website.\" It is a",
            "language that eliminates a substantial amount of the integration work that",
            "normally sits *between* individually capable tools.",
            "",
            "## 2. Principles that guide every decision",
            "",
            "1. **Convention over configuration; configuration over restriction.** The",
            "   ordinary application follows sensible defaults and needs little setup.",
            "   Every major subsystem should remain inspectable, configurable or",
            "   replaceable where a real application requires it.",
            "2. **One application, not two loosely coupled codebases.** A full-stack Kiln",
            "   app should not feel like separate frontend and backend projects forced to",
            "   communicate through hand-written glue.",
            "3. **Progressive integration.** Kiln can own part of a stack without",
            "   demanding the rest. All-Kiln projects gain deeper compiler-assisted",
            "   integration, but mixed-language projects remain first-class.",
            "4. **Low complexity floor, high performance ceiling.** Start with ordinary",
            "   GC-managed code and high-level APIs. Only optimize particular hotspots",
            "   when measurement justifies it; do not impose systems-language concepts on",
            "   routine web development.",
            "5. **No artificial abstractions.** If raw HTML, CSS, HTTP handlers, SQL or",
            "   manual state management is more suitable, make it possible. Defaults",
            "   should save work rather than hide important behaviour.",
            "6. **Integrate before reinventing.** Prefer interoperating with trustworthy",
            "   existing implementations and ecosystems over rebuilding every library,",
            "   cloud provider or developer tool.",
            "7. **Compiler-backed confidence.** Share definitions across boundaries and",
            "   catch mismatches as early as possible. Diagnostics should be useful to",
            "   humans and machine-applicable by coding agents.",
            "8. **Safe and observable by default.** Secure defaults, predictable errors,",
            "   explicit consequential actions, testable builds and visible deployment",
            "   plans matter more than a short demo.",
            "9. **One maintained way to do ordinary tasks.** Avoid multiplying tools,",
            "   configuration languages and competing conventions without a concrete",
            "   developer benefit.",
            "",
            "The standard for success is **less glue code and less cognitive overhead**,",
            "not simply more items in the standard library.",
            "",
            "## 3. Backend: the batteries are actually included",
            "",
            "The intended standard experience includes typed HTTP handlers and routing,",
            "request/response handling, JSON, error propagation, logging, tracing,",
            "metrics, configuration, filesystem and time utilities, and a lightweight",
            "concurrent runtime. A small API should work without assembling a framework.",
            "",
            "### Data, forms and authentication",
            "",
            "- **SQL and ORM:** SQLite for a zero-service local start, with PostgreSQL and",
            "  MySQL as application needs grow. Ordinary Kiln structs describe models;",
            "  typed query composition catches field and type mistakes, and raw SQL",
            "  remains available.",
            "- **Existing databases:** `kiln db pull` can import an existing schema into",
            "  editable typed models, not force a new database or a greenfield schema.",
            "- **Migrations:** `kiln db diff` generates changes for review; `kiln db",
            "  migrate` applies explicit migrations. Production deployment should not",
            "  invent an unreviewed migration from current model definitions.",
            "- **Forms and validation:** one schema/type definition should inform server",
            "  validation, input constraints, field errors and frontend form generation",
            "  rather than duplicating each rule in several packages.",
            "- **Auth and sessions:** built-in paths for sign-in, session management,",
            "  permissions, CSRF protection, password security and common identity",
            "  integrations, while permitting specialized policies and external providers.",
            "- **Generated admin:** a useful, secured CRUD administration interface",
            "  derived from models, customizable from individual fields through whole",
            "  Glaze pages, not an uneditable generated site.",
            "",
            "### Durable background work",
            "",
            "The job system is a core example of eliminating glue: a developer should not",
            "need to introduce and configure an entirely separate Celery-like ecosystem",
            "just to perform routine asynchronous or scheduled tasks.",
            "",
            "The direction is an integrated `jobs` API with retries, schedules,",
            "observability and durable storage. SQLite can serve small installations,",
            "with a PostgreSQL-backed option for larger systems and a separate",
            "`./app worker` process when scaling. Transactional scheduling and",
            "outbox-style correctness matter: saying a job was queued should mean the",
            "system can recover it after process failure. Exact queue design remains a",
            "future implementation decision.",
            "",
            "The same compiled application can expose server, worker and migration entry",
            "points without needing Kiln installed on the destination host.",
            "",
            "## 4. Frontend DX is equally important",
            "",
            "Frontend developers repeatedly rebuild forms, validation feedback, data",
            "fetching, loading/error states, authentication screens, interactivity,",
            "styling conventions and the client/server transport layer. Kiln should",
            "remove as much *frontend* integration work as it removes from the backend.",
            "",
            "### Glazes: server-first components without a separate UI language",
            "",
            "Glazes (`.glaze`) are typed, HTML-oriented templates and components. The",
            "planned frontend experience includes layouts, slots, reusable components,",
            "scoped styles, design tokens, context-aware escaping and server rendering",
            "by default. Phase 3 adds native typed `Ui` records with concise",
            "Tailwind-inspired keys (`bg`, `fg`, `p`, etc.), co-located `<style>`,",
            "ordinary CSS/CSS Modules and **Finishes**: ordinary functions returning",
            "reusable styling values or multi-part records. Compatible theme contracts",
            "can radically restyle the same content and Glazes, including layout.",
            "See the [Phase 3 implementation plan](plans/phase-3.md) for the agreed scope,",
            "API sketches and acceptance gates.",
            "",
            "- **CSS-first:** full native CSS must always work. Provide helpful design",
            "  tokens, responsive defaults and optionally attractive, accessible",
            "  component primitives, not a mandatory proprietary styling dialect.",
            "- **Accessible defaults:** forms, controls, focus/keyboard interactions and",
            "  validation errors should have sound defaults while remaining overridable.",
            "- **Code-defined routes:** routing should remain expressible explicitly in",
            "  application code; a mandatory file-based routing convention is not the",
            "  goal. Glaze files define views/components rather than automatically",
            "  claiming URLs.",
            "- **Typed forms:** a field, validation rule and server operation should not",
            "  require independent descriptions in the database, HTTP API and browser",
            "  client. Generate the usual binding, submission and error-handling",
            "  machinery, with override points for nonstandard behaviour.",
            "- **Progressive frontend use:** a project should remain free to serve",
            "  ordinary HTML, CSS and JavaScript; adopting a Glaze should not require",
            "  converting the whole frontend.",
            "",
            "An optional [official component Ware](#first-official-ware-accessible-ui-components)",
            "will offer a polished starting point for semantic controls such as",
            "`<Input>` and `<Button>`, without making them mandatory or hiding the",
            "underlying HTML.",
            "",
            "### Embers: interactivity when and where needed",
            "",
            "Kiln's browser-capable components are **Embers**, compiled to browser",
            "WebAssembly using WasmGC. The planned explicit `.ember` source suffix,",
            "separate from server-only `.glaze`, is specified in [Phase 4.1](plans/phase-4.1.md);",
            "the Phase 4 implementation currently infers Embers from interactive Glazes.",
            "A static or mostly server-rendered page should not pay for a full client",
            "application just because one control is interactive.",
            "",
            "The intended experience includes reactive state, lifecycle/event handling,",
            "typed server functions, generated client/server RPC and ergonomic error",
            "handling. Caching, invalidation, optimistic updates and live updates",
            "(SSE/WebSockets) should have good defaults where the compiler can justify",
            "them, but observable and explicit overrides. Avoid hidden invalidation",
            "behaviour that is impossible to inspect or debug.",
            "",
            "The key promise is that **a Kiln server function and the UI calling it share",
            "one type contract**, without hand-authoring route wrappers, fetch boilerplate,",
            "duplicate DTOs or serialization code. Phase 4 implements this boundary using",
            "pure or ephemeral-state functions, with Phase 5 adding database-backed,",
            "authenticated server operations.",
            "",
            "#### Future JavaScript compiler fallback",
            "",
            "**WasmGC is Kiln's only browser compilation target through Phase 4 and the",
            "initial 1.0 release.** If a browser does not support the required WasmGC",
            "features, Kiln should preserve useful server-rendered content and ordinary",
            "HTML link/form behaviour wherever possible. Ember-only interactivity may be",
            "unavailable in such browsers. The runtime should feature-detect support and",
            "avoid breaking otherwise usable pages.",
            "",
            "A **future optional JavaScript fallback compiler backend is part of the",
            "long-term vision**, not the Phase 4 implementation or a 1.0 delivery gate.",
            "It would compile the same checked Kiln/browser-reachable source to JavaScript",
            "rather than require developers to write a second implementation; equivalent",
            "observable semantics, safe server/client boundaries, compatible generated RPC,",
            "asset selection and cross-backend conformance tests are requirements before",
            "shipping it. Actual development timing is subject to browser support data,",
            "feasibility, generated bundle/performance costs and ongoing maintenance.",
            "",
            "This possible JavaScript **code-generation backend** is distinct from the",
            "minimal JavaScript bootstrap and browser interop used to execute WasmGC",
            "Embers today.",
            "",
            "### Static and offline variants",
            "",
            "The same component system should support SSR, selective interactivity and,",
            "later, `kiln fire --static` for static-site generation or hybrid",
            "prerendering. Much later, optional PWA/local-first capabilities could reuse",
            "typed models for offline data and synchronization. These are distinct",
            "roadmap milestones, not required for the first useful Glaze application.",
            "",
            "## 5. Performance without leaving the language",
            "",
            "Most applications should work well with ordinary managed Kiln values.",
            "The developer who discovers one unusually CPU- or allocation-intensive",
            "function should be able to optimize *that function* rather than extract it",
            "into a Rust/C service with a new build process and foreign-language glue.",
            "",
            "The planned performance ladder is:",
            "",
            "1. **Simple default:** GC-managed objects and readable code, compiled to",
            "   native instructions with no manual ownership bookkeeping.",
            "2. **Compiler optimization:** improved code generation, generic",
            "   specialization, escape analysis and LLVM-backed optimized release builds.",
            "   Profile and benchmark rather than assume a particular speedup.",
            "3. **Targeted low-level tools:** optional SIMD/vectorized operations,",
            "   efficient data layouts and allocation-aware APIs where they materially",
            "   help computation or memory locality.",
            "4. **Opt-in alternative allocation:** scoped arenas/regions or request-scoped",
            "   allocation for suitable temporary data, with the compiler enforcing",
            "   non-escaping references or explicit promotion/copying where necessary.",
            "5. **External escape hatch:** foreign-function interfaces if a specialized",
            "   existing implementation is still the right tool.",
            "",
            "These mechanisms solve different problems. Arenas can reduce GC pressure but",
            "do not inherently speed up CPU-bound arithmetic; SIMD does not help a",
            "database-bound endpoint. The optimization path must remain empirical.",
            "",
            "**Garbage collection remains the default.** The roadmap currently uses",
            "Boehm, with a planned future precise GC. Opt-in memory control is **not** a",
            "proposal to make ownership types or manual frees mandatory for ordinary",
            "Kiln applications, nor a promise to match every systems-language feature.",
            "",
            "Where practical, improved internals should leave a function's public Kiln",
            "signature—and the code calling it—unchanged.",
            "",
            "### Freestanding Kiln: bare-metal and hobby operating systems",
            "",
            "Kiln is web-first, but a compelling **long-term experiment** is to compile",
            "Kiln programs for **freestanding (bare-metal) targets** with no underlying",
            "operating system. One particularly interesting dogfooding project would be",
            "to write a **small hobby operating system in Kiln**, first running under",
            "an emulator such as QEMU and, perhaps later, on real hardware. This is",
            "an exploratory direction **beyond the 1.0 web-platform goals**, not a",
            "near-term roadmap commitment or a promise of a general-purpose",
            "systems-programming language.",
            "",
            "The architectural principle is to **keep the language, IR and machine-code",
            "generation independent of any particular host OS where practical**.",
            "Hosted applications retain their normal batteries-included runtime;",
            "a separately selected freestanding target would provide only the",
            "platform facilities available at that stage. Both could reuse the",
            "same compiler frontend and architecture-specific instruction generation,",
            "while differing in startup code, linking, ABI assumptions and runtime",
            "services.",
            "",
            "A freestanding environment would need explicit, narrowly scoped",
            "low-level capabilities rather than pretend an operating system exists:",
            "",
            "- Startup/linker and bootloader integration; appropriate bare-metal",
            "  target triples and executable formats.",
            "- Hardware access through carefully designed raw-pointer, volatile",
            "  memory and interrupt facilities, with unsafe operations clearly",
            "  isolated from ordinary Kiln code.",
            "- A staged runtime: early boot cannot assume heap allocation or Boehm",
            "  GC; memory allocation and any collector must be initialized on top",
            "  of available physical/virtual memory services. Garbage collection",
            "  remains the default for normal hosted Kiln programs.",
            "- Clear availability checks for OS-dependent standard-library features,",
            "  plus emulator-based tests and reproducible cross-compilation.",
            "",
            "An eventual hobby-OS progression could be: **boot and draw to a",
            "framebuffer → memory management → interrupts and input → scheduling →",
            "a tiny shell and filesystem → running Kiln applications on a Kiln OS**.",
            "These are ideas for learning and experimentation, not acceptance criteria",
            "for Kiln's primary full-stack mission. The challenge is to make special",
            "low-level code possible **without imposing systems-language complexity on",
            "ordinary web developers**.",
            "",
            "## 6. Interoperability in both directions is an adoption strategy",
            "",
            "A new language cannot expect developers to discard existing code or wait",
            "for a duplicate of every popular ecosystem library. Interoperability is",
            "therefore a product feature, not a grudging escape hatch.",
            "",
            "### Kiln consumes existing libraries and services",
            "",
            "- **Native interop:** planned C-ABI FFI and generated or declared bindings",
            "  for C libraries, with Rust usable through a stable C-compatible bridge.",
            "  Python/Node libraries with native wrappers may be reachable where the",
            "  runtime and ABI permit.",
            "- **Service interop:** HTTP, typed API contracts and potential OpenAPI",
            "  generation/import let Kiln coexist with existing Django, Node, Go and",
            "  other services.",
            "- **Portable components:** explore the WebAssembly Component Model and WIT",
            "  for typed cross-language components where runtime support makes sense.",
            "",
            "### Existing applications consume Kiln",
            "",
            "Kiln should also be able to export callable native functionality and",
            "generate wrappers or headers so an existing Rust, C, Python or Node",
            "application can adopt **one Kiln component** without rewriting the rest.",
            "A performance-sensitive feature can be introduced incrementally.",
            "",
            "Cross-language work must specify ownership, allocation, GC roots,",
            "strings/collections, errors, callbacks, threading and version compatibility.",
            "A C ABI alone does not make arbitrary Python or Rust types seamlessly",
            "interchangeable. Keep these boundaries explicit and testable.",
            "",
            "### JavaScript and npm for browser Embers",
            "",
            "**Finalized Phase 4 direction (2026-10-25): typed by default, explicit dynamic escape hatch (Option B).** Kiln authors can use native browser standards and compatible existing npm libraries from Ember/Kiln without authoring JavaScript glue. The compiler manages typed browser wrappers, generated JavaScript/WasmGC bindings and production bundling.",
            "",
            "- Generate type-safe bindings from supported TypeScript declarations; report unsupported shapes rather than silently dropping into dynamic typing. Browser-only imports are checked against server/SSR usage.",
            "- Provide explicit opaque `js.Handle<T>`-style host references and an opt-in `js.Value`-style dynamic API for unusual packages, with checked conversions and typed failures. Exact spelling is subject to language grammar validation; the architecture is decided.",
            "- Translate values, retained object identities, closures/callbacks, promises and JS exceptions predictably across the bridge; integrate cleanup with Ember disposal and keep external DOM ownership explicit.",
            "- Compile/bundle pinned browser-side JS dependencies without forcing developers to maintain a separate npm/Node project or deploy Node with their server. Phase 6's manifest/Ware package infrastructure can mature the CLI and lockfile workflow.",
            "- Use maintained browser implementations rather than reimplementing libraries. Framework-independent browser packages are the starting point; React-specific or Node-only modules are not universally compatible. Package scripts must not execute automatically by default.",
            "- Browser APIs remain subject to web platform permissions, CSP and origin rules. Third-party JS is not sandboxed merely by passing through a typed bridge.",
            "",
            "**“Access to npm” does not mean “every npm package works everywhere.”** See the [Phase 4 interop contract](plans/phase-4.md#8-javascript-and-browser-interoperability) for conversion, fallback, error and acceptance requirements.",
            "",
            "The adoption path is intentionally incremental: **try one Kiln module,",
            "service or component → keep existing dependencies → choose Kiln more broadly",
            "if its integrated developer experience proves better.**",
            "",
            "## 7. Wares, templates and a real ecosystem",
            "",
            "[Phase 4.2](plans/phase-4.2.md) defines local multi-folder modules, `pub`, root discovery and a minimal optional `kiln.toml` **before the data framework**. [Phase 6](plans/phase-6.md) then extends that foundation for Ware dependencies/lockfiles, registry/cache, official UI components, public test runner, tooling and MCP. Planned syntax is not a claim of current support.",
            "",
            "**Wares** are source packages, managed through an optional **`kiln.toml`",
            "project manifest** and generated **`kiln.lock`** and eventually an official",
            "registry. The initial implementation can support local/Git dependencies",
            "and reproducible immutable versions before a full registry exists.",
            "",
            "**Phase 4.2 project configuration** starts as optional declarative",
            "`kiln.toml` for project identity/root and application entrypoint,",
            "not a procedural `Kilnfile`. **Phase 6 extends this same manifest**",
            "with versioned Wares and build/test defaults; `kiln.lock` then records",
            "the fully resolved, hash-verified external dependency graph for reproducible",
            "local/CI builds. Tooling should manage both automatically when adding/removing",
            "dependencies. Command names for updating Wares are still to be designed;",
            "the existing `kiln upgrade` command specifically updates the compiler.",
            "",
            "**No manifest should be required to run one `.kiln` file, a simple",
            "single-folder project, or a multi-folder app with an unambiguous root/entry.**",
            "Phase 4.2 local modules need no registry",
            "or individual package manifests; the same resolution foundation can",
            "later support Wares and optional multi-package workspaces. A project",
            "manifest must not contain secrets or execute arbitrary dependency",
            "install hooks. Deployment stays separate in `kiln-deploy.toml`.",
            "See [the Phase 4.2 module and manifest design](design.md#phase-42-minimal-project-manifest)",
            "and [Phase 6 Ware extensions](design.md#phase-6-extending-the-project-manifest-for-wares)",
            "for example manifests and acceptance criteria.",
            "",
            "**Shared Ware storage, without project-local dependency trees.** Phase 6",
            "should cache downloaded Wares once in a machine-local **per-user global",
            "store**, keyed by verified immutable contents, rather than creating",
            "`ware_modules/` or similar dependency directories in each project.",
            "Each project's `kiln.lock` independently selects exact versions/hashes,",
            "allowing multiple versions to coexist while identical package content",
            "is shared between apps. Cache compiled artifacts separately with keys",
            "covering compiler/runtime version, build target and options. Offline",
            "builds should work when the locked dependencies are already cached.",
            "Provide inspect/verify/prune tooling, safe parallel downloads and a",
            "configurable cache location; a cache shared *between different OS",
            "accounts* is only an optional future extension because of the",
            "security/permission implications. Native application binaries still",
            "include their dependencies and do not need this cache at runtime.",
            "See [global Ware storage and caching](design.md#phase-6-global-ware-storage-and-caching).",
            "",
            "- The compiler/toolchain handles package compilation; avoid npm-style",
            "  arbitrary `postinstall` scripts and surprising code execution merely",
            "  from fetching a dependency.",
            "- Package provenance, locked versions, dependency auditing and transparent",
            "  capability/effect reporting should help developers understand what they",
            "  are introducing. Compile-time reporting is **not** a claim of OS-level",
            "  sandboxing.",
            "- Official Kiln-maintained Wares should be especially small, auditable and",
            "  preferably self-contained without third-party transitive dependencies.",
            "  Powerful permissions or extension hooks should be transparent and require",
            "  explicit user approval.",
            "- **Project templates are different from Wares:** `kiln new --template`",
            "  could instantiate files/variables from trusted templates without executing",
            "  arbitrary initialization code.",
            "",
            "### First official Ware: accessible UI components",
            "",
            "The **first official Kiln Ware** should be a **shadcn-inspired component",
            "collection** built with Kiln's own Glazes, typed `Ui` styling, Finishes",
            "and design tokens. A working description is \"Kiln UI\"; any actual Ware",
            "package name and install command should be chosen when Wares are",
            "implemented. This is an intended **flagship official Ware**, not a",
            "requirement to use a framework for every application.",
            "",
            "The experience should be straightforward: where a developer would write",
            "bare HTML such as `<input>` or `<button>`, they can optionally use",
            "capitalized Glaze components such as `<Input>`, `<Button>`, `<Select>`,",
            "`<Checkbox>` and `<FormField>`. These components provide well-considered",
            "defaults, cover common use cases through typed props, and remain fully",
            "customizable through `Ui` overrides, Finishes, tokens, CSS or direct",
            "component edits.",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{# Illustrative future API — not yet implemented #}",
            "<form method=\"post\">",
            "    <Input",
            "        name=\"email\"",
            "        type=\"email\"",
            "        label=\"Email address\"",
            "        required",
            "    />",
            "    <Button tone={.Primary} type=\"submit\">",
            "        Save changes",
            "    </Button>",
            "</form>",
            "```",
            "",
            "The goal is **less repetitive UI plumbing, not less control**:",
            "",
            "- **Good defaults without magic:** usable visual styling, responsive",
            "  sizing, meaningful focus and disabled states, error/help text,",
            "  consistent token/Finish integration and sensible component variants",
            "  out of the box. Ordinary HTML remains valid and first-class.",
            "- **Accessibility as an engineering requirement:** semantic native",
            "  elements where possible, correct label/input association, keyboard",
            "  navigation and focus management, appropriate ARIA only when needed,",
            "  screen-reader-compatible validation/errors, contrast and reduced-motion",
            "  considerations. Complex widgets need interaction tests, not just",
            "  impressive screenshots; no component library guarantees accessibility",
            "  automatically in every application.",
            "- **Typed and overridable common cases:** component props for size,",
            "  tone, intent, state and validation presentation; compose with ordinary",
            "  Kiln logic. Developers can pass normal HTML attributes, choose a",
            "  different Finish or theme, supply local CSS, replace parts, or just",
            "  use the raw element when the abstraction is unhelpful.",
            "- **Source-first, shadcn-like ownership:** make component source,",
            "  Glazes and Finishes inspectable and editable. Ideally developers can",
            "  install the Ware for shared components **or add/copy selected component",
            "  sources into their project** for complete local ownership; avoid a",
            "  sealed, opaque runtime dependency or requiring all components at once.",
            "  Exact package/CLI commands are future Wares design decisions.",
            "- **Small, consistent foundation:** prioritize highly used primitives,",
            "  form controls, navigation, surfaces, dialogs and feedback components",
            "  with documentation and examples rather than shipping a huge collection",
            "  of brittle one-off widgets. Prefer native platform behaviour over",
            "  custom JavaScript.",
            "- **Build with Kiln, not alongside it:** reuse the same Glaze semantics,",
            "  scoped CSS, native style values, theme contracts and eventual typed",
            "  form bindings. Do not introduce a separate component DSL, styling",
            "  runtime, application router or incompatible theme mechanism.",
            "- **Progressive interactivity:** the first components that only need",
            "  server-rendered markup/CSS can use Phase 3 facilities. Rich keyboard-",
            "  interactive widgets and stateful behaviours can depend on **Embers**",
            "  once Phase 4 supplies Embers; don't smuggle a proprietary JavaScript",
            "  framework into the initial Ware. Phase 5 adds forms/auth integration",
            "  without making simple input components depend on a database.",
            "",
            "The desired developer experience is **bare HTML when you want it, a",
            "production-minded `<Input>` when you don't, and source you can understand",
            "and modify whenever the defaults stop fitting**. The Ware should",
            "demonstrate Kiln's UI system by dogfooding it, not become a second UI",
            "system developers have to learn.",
            "",
            "**Scheduling:** Phase 3 lays the rendering/styling/Finishes groundwork;",
            "the component collection ships when **Wares** support it (Phase 6 on",
            "the current roadmap), after Embers in Phase 4 and data/auth in Phase 5. Its initial components",
            "may therefore use Kiln's own interactive system; this does **not** expand",
            "Phase 3's accepted scope.",
            "",
            "Possible optional official Wares include game development",
            "(`@kiln/game`), desktop apps and perhaps TUIs. CLI programming itself",
            "should remain straightforward through the standard library. Some future",
            "non-web UI targets might reuse Glaze/Ember concepts, but web development is",
            "the core priority, and target-specific UI differences must stay explicit.",
            "",
            "A future official Ware registry should itself be a Kiln application; it is",
            "both useful infrastructure and a substantial dogfooding target. A minimal",
            "static registry or Git-backed index may come first.",
            "",
            "## 8. One-command builds, releases and deployment",
            "",
            "### Distribution and developer setup",
            "",
            "The normal supported-platform experience should be to install one compiler",
            "binary, use the same CLI for checking/building/running, and upgrade it",
            "without separately installing Rust, a C compiler, a linker or a GC. The",
            "compiler may depend on Rust and other tools **when built from source**.",
            "The produced executable includes its necessary runtime/GC and does not",
            "require Kiln on the deployment host.",
            "",
            "On Linux, a genuinely statically linked server binary should fit in a",
            "minimal `FROM scratch` container, subject to normal application resources",
            "such as trusted CA certificates, storage and time-zone data. Windows has",
            "its own platform linking constraints. The C backend remains useful as an",
            "alternative; Cranelift is the current native backend, with LLVM intended",
            "for later optimized releases.",
            "",
            "### Build, deploy and release are separate operations",
            "",
            "The proposed roles are:",
            "",
            "| Command | Responsibility | Status |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| `kiln upgrade` | Update the installed compiler through checksum-verified releases | Implemented; see the [README install instructions](../README.md#install) |",
            "| `kiln fire` | Produce an optimized release artifact | Planned |",
            "| `kiln deploy <target>` | Deliver and activate an artifact on configured infrastructure | Proposed |",
            "| `kiln release <target>` | Run checks/tests, build, prepare, confirm, deploy and verify | Proposed |",
            "| `kiln deploy <target> --dry-run` | Explain intended changes without applying them | Proposed |",
            "",
            "The update/install workflow was developed ahead of Phase 2.5. The",
            "future commands and spellings are illustrative until implemented;",
            "the [roadmap](../README.md#roadmap) remains authoritative for scheduling.",
            "",
            "### Application deployment, not another Terraform",
            "",
            "The preferred model is an explicit `kiln-deploy.toml` describing **existing",
            "infrastructure**, deployment targets and overrides, rather than Kiln owning",
            "the entire cloud resource lifecycle. Targets might include an existing VPS",
            "over SSH, containers or adapter-backed hosts. Existing systemd units,",
            "reverse proxies and infrastructure-as-code tools should remain usable.",
            "Provider integrations may live outside the compiler.",
            "",
            "A deployment may coordinate a server process, background workers, assets,",
            "health checks, versioned releases and **database migrations**. It should:",
            "",
            "- Generate an inspectable plan and ask for confirmation before modifying a",
            "  deployed environment.",
            "- Require an explicit `--yes` (or equivalent) in non-interactive CI rather",
            "  than treating an absent prompt as consent. Skipping a prompt does **not**",
            "  skip tests, integrity verification or safety checks.",
            "- Treat potentially destructive database changes as a distinct high-risk",
            "  operation, with explicit approval and appropriate backup/compatibility",
            "  policy.",
            "- Apply previously reviewed migrations with locking and appropriate",
            "  ordering for the deployment strategy; never claim that rolling back an",
            "  executable automatically reverses a destructive schema change.",
            "- Be explicit about ownership of existing infrastructure, secrets,",
            "  credentials, downtime expectations, worker draining and rollback.",
            "- Make common deployments one command, with transparent low-level control",
            "  for applications that need it.",
            "",
            "A future AI agent may inspect a release plan through structured tooling, but",
            "must not silently deploy or authorize destructive operations.",
            "",
            "## 9. First-class human and AI developer experience",
            "",
            "The developer experience starts before the code is running: install,",
            "editor support, compiler messages, tests, a fast feedback loop, docs,",
            "debugging and deployment should feel like parts of one toolchain.",
            "",
            "- **Editors:** the basic LSP is implemented. Phase 2.5 adds a dedicated",
            "  VS Code extension, icons, syntax highlighting and enhanced compiler-backed",
            "  completion/navigation; intelligence belongs in the shared LSP so other",
            "  editors benefit.",
            "- **Built-in tests from logic to browser:** Phase 6 plans one `kiln test`",
            "  workflow covering Kiln unit tests, isolated integration tests for",
            "  API/database/auth flows and **automated real-browser/frontend E2E",
            "  tests** for Glazes, navigation, forms, keyboard/focus behaviour and",
            "  theme styling. Kiln owns test discovery, assertions, fixtures,",
            "  application startup, reporting, screenshots and CI integration; it",
            "  reuses a maintained browser automation engine underneath rather than",
            "  reinventing Chromium or requiring users to maintain a Node-based test",
            "  project. See the [integrated testing plan](plans/testing.md).",
            "- **Ember coverage from day one:** Phase 4 adds browser CI coverage for",
            "  Ember hydration, reactive state, events and typed RPC; Phase 5 adds",
            "  database/auth/form integration coverage; Phase 6's public",
            "  test runner includes them from its initial release. Formatter, hot reload",
            "  and richer editor refactoring are also Phase 6 work.",
            "- **Agent-friendly contracts:** structured JSON diagnostics, stable error",
            "  codes, machine-applicable fixes, generated docs/examples and explicit",
            "  tool schemas minimize guesswork for coding agents.",
            "- **Compiler/tooling MCP:** `kiln mcp` could expose check, build, test,",
            "  introspection, generated documentation and eventually debugging as",
            "  structured tools. MCP is a convenience interface to the same semantics,",
            "  not an alternative compiler.",
            "- **Application MCP:** separately, future Kiln standard-library support",
            "  could let applications implement MCP servers/clients, with Kiln types",
            "  supplying tool input/output schemas. This is not the same as the",
            "  compiler's own MCP server.",
            "- **Debugger (later, not Phase 2.5):** a future debugger should allow",
            "  ordinary human workflows via CLI/editor/DAP and structured inspection for",
            "  AI agents via MCP: frames, variables, typed values, step/breakpoints and",
            "  perhaps snapshots/diffs. It must be designed against the real backends,",
            "  optimized-code limitations and source mappings.",
            "",
            "The goal is **better outcomes per developer hour**, not maximal automation",
            "with hidden behavior. AI-generated code still needs conformance coverage,",
            "reviewable changes and sound engineering decisions.",
            "",
            "## 10. Dogfooding, credibility and adoption",
            "",
            "Kiln began as a personal hobby and learning project. Keeping it fun, with",
            "no artificial public deadlines or obligations before it is ready, is",
            "important. At the same time, sustained real applications are essential if",
            "Kiln is eventually to earn trust beyond its creator.",
            "",
            "A sensible progression:",
            "",
            "1. Finish the core language/tooling milestones, then build real small",
            "   applications, not only compiler probes.",
            "2. Build the Kiln **documentation website in Kiln** as the Phase 3",
            "   acceptance target.",
            "3. Build increasingly demanding internal services, such as the future",
            "   **Ware registry in Kiln**, to exercise HTTP, persistence, auth, admin,",
            "   package APIs, runtime behavior and deployment.",
            "4. Eventually use Kiln for a personal site or useful production project,",
            "   where its reliability can be measured over time.",
            "5. Publish clear examples, honest performance measurements and",
            "   side-by-side integration-code comparisons with mainstream stacks; invite",
            "   outside adoption only when documentation, security and compatibility",
            "   are credible.",
            "",
            "Using Kiln only for a backend, a single embedded module or a frontend",
            "component must remain a valid adoption path. Broad adoption is an",
            "**aspiration**, not a success condition for early milestones or a reason to",
            "rush a 1.0 release.",
            "",
            "The project also demonstrates compiler architecture, product design,",
            "testing and AI-directed engineering. Those benefits stand even if uptake",
            "is modest.",
            "",
            "## 11. Scope boundaries and decision status",
            "",
            "### Established direction",
            "",
            "- Web-first, convention-driven, tightly integrated but deeply configurable.",
            "- Garbage-collected and native by default; no mandatory manual memory model.",
            "- Server-only Glazes and browser-capable Embers, with native CSS support and progressive adoption.",
            "- Wares as extension packages, with provenance and disciplined installs.",
            "- Strong tooling, agent accessibility and real-project dogfooding.",
            "- Full-stack Kiln should reduce hand-written frontend/backend boundaries.",
            "- Both **Kiln calling external code** and **external code calling Kiln**",
            "  matter for eventual adoption.",
            "",
            "### Implemented foundation",
            "",
            "Native Linux and Windows compilation, server-rendered Glazes, typed styles and",
            "themes, explicit WasmGC Embers, typed server RPC, browser/npm bindings, local",
            "folder modules and project-aware editor support are implemented. See the",
            "[language reference](/docs/#language-reference) for their current scope.",
            "",
            "### Designed but not yet implemented end to end",
            "",
            "Integrated ORM/auth/admin/jobs, broader npm interop, full Wares/registry, `kiln fire` release workflow,",
            "`kiln deploy`/`kiln release`, optimized LLVM builds, and much of the planned",
            "developer tooling. Existing parser/type-checker support for a construct is",
            "not the same as a working native runtime implementation; consult current",
            "[stdlib status](reference/stdlib.md) and tests.",
            "",
            "### Later explorations, not fixed promises",
            "",
            "Opt-in arenas and explicit allocation controls, SIMD/data-layout APIs,",
            "general bidirectional FFI/binding generation, Wasm Component Model/WIT,",
            "fully featured MCP application support, AI-assisted debugger snapshots,",
            "PWA offline synchronization, cross-target Glaze/Ember reuse in desktop,",
            "TUI and games, a JavaScript fallback compiler backend for non-WasmGC browsers,",
            "freestanding/bare-metal targets and an experimental small OS written in Kiln,",
            "and additional platforms. These require design, compatibility and maintenance",
            "trade-off reviews.",
            "",
            "### Deliberate non-goals",
            "",
            "- Reinventing all infrastructure provisioning as a Kiln-owned Terraform.",
            "- Recreating the entire npm/Python/Rust ecosystem in Kiln.",
            "- Requiring use of every Kiln layer to benefit from any one layer.",
            "- Forcing unusual applications through fixed templates or styling systems.",
            "- Making low-level ownership complexity mandatory just to write a CRUD app.",
            "- Sacrificing correctness, transparency or long-term maintainability merely",
            "  to shorten demonstrations or hit arbitrary dates.",
            "",
            "## 12. How to judge progress",
            "",
            "The [phased roadmap](../README.md#roadmap) defines executable outcomes:",
            "CLI programs, a real HTTP API, the Kiln docs site, an authenticated",
            "database-backed app, an ergonomic development workflow, an interactive",
            "full-stack app, and eventually Kiln 1.0 ready for personal production use.",
            "",
            "Across those milestones, ask:",
            "",
            "- How much application-specific **business logic** was written relative to",
            "  adapters, configuration and repeated declarations?",
            "- Can a developer see, test and override Kiln's generated decisions?",
            "- Can Kiln coexist with an existing system without a rewrite?",
            "- Does an optimized hotspot remain part of the same application and type",
            "  system?",
            "- Do native outputs behave correctly and predictably on clean machines?",
            "- Can a newcomer and a coding agent diagnose problems without guesswork?",
            "- Does real dogfooding reveal useful simplifications rather than new hidden",
            "  complexity?",
            "",
            "**Kiln does not have to reinvent what developers can do. It should",
            "dramatically reduce how much work they have to do to accomplish it.**",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/phase-2.5", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Phase 2.5 — VS Code developer experience",
            "",
            "**Status:** Done (0.5.0). Phase 2 (HTTP) is complete; this milestone sits before",
            "phase 3 (glazes and server-rendered frontend development).",
            "",
            "**Goal:** A developer can install the Kiln VS Code extension and comfortably",
            "write, navigate, check, build and run Kiln programs without manually wiring",
            "up an LSP client. Language intelligence must come from the existing Rust",
            "compiler/LSP, not from a second parser or type checker in the extension.",
            "",
            "## Scope",
            "",
            "### VS Code extension",
            "",
            "- Register `.kiln` and `.glaze` languages, with syntax highlighting for",
            "  Kiln constructs and embedded Kiln expressions in glazes.",
            "- Use Kiln's existing SVG brand assets for appropriate file icons; add",
            "  recognizable icons/associations for source and glaze files, and use",
            "  VS Code file-icon theme support rather than hard-coded editor UI icons.",
            "- Launch and manage `kiln lsp` using the installed Kiln binary. Let users",
            "  configure its path, and give actionable guidance if it cannot be found.",
            "  The extension must not reimplement the checker in TypeScript.",
            "- Expose convenient Command Palette actions for `kiln check`, `kiln run`",
            "  and `kiln build` for the current program, with errors and output visible",
            "  through VS Code. Reuse existing CLI behavior.",
            "- Document local development, packaging and installing the extension.",
            "",
            "### Enhanced LSP (usable by all LSP editors)",
            "",
            "- Preserve existing diagnostics and suggested quick fixes, including",
            "  unsaved-buffer checking.",
            "- Add context-aware completion for visible declarations, variables,",
            "  functions, types and members, using resolved symbol/type information.",
            "- Add hover information for inferred types, declarations and signatures.",
            "- Add go-to-definition and document/workspace symbol navigation for Kiln",
            "  declarations and local references where resolution is available.",
            "- Handle multiple source files in a shared program directory and edits",
            "  without needing a full compiler rebuild. Prefer meaningful empty",
            "  responses over crashes for incomplete source and unresolved symbols.",
            "- Maintain LSP protocol correctness for positions/ranges, especially",
            "  UTF-16 coordinates, unsaved documents and incremental editor use.",
            "",
            "## Implementation plan",
            "",
            "1. **Editor foundation (done).** Add a VS Code extension project, Kiln/Glaze",
            "   language registrations, basic syntax grammars, brand-based file icons",
            "   and a local extension launch configuration. Smoke-test associations",
            "   on both light and dark VS Code themes.",
            "2. **Compiler-backed language intelligence (done).** Extend `kiln lsp` to",
            "   surface completions, hovers, definitions and symbols from shared",
            "   compiler name/type resolution. Add focused protocol tests for",
            "   same-file, cross-file and incomplete/unsaved Kiln programs. Keep",
            "   diagnostics and quick fixes working unchanged.",
            "3. **Extension integration (done).** Connect the extension to `kiln lsp`, add",
            "   binary path configuration and Run / Build / Check commands. Surface",
            "   missing compiler and command failures clearly; don't run arbitrary",
            "   project-defined scripts.",
            "4. **Polish and validation (done).** Document installation, settings, supported",
            "   commands and limitations. Add extension packaging checks and a VS Code",
            "   integration smoke test; run relevant LSP tests on Linux and Windows.",
            "   Ensure other LSP editors continue to work.",
            "",
            "## Done when",
            "",
            "A developer can install the Kiln VS Code extension, open a project containing",
            "`.kiln` and `.glaze` files, see appropriate icons and highlighting, get",
            "live compiler diagnostics and quick fixes, use completion, hover, go to",
            "definition and symbol navigation in Kiln code, and check/build/run the",
            "project from VS Code. The Rust LSP continues to provide its intelligence",
            "to other editors, with automated regression coverage.",
            "",
            "## Deliberately left for later",
            "",
            "- **No debugger in phase 2.5:** no DAP adapter, breakpoints, stepping,",
            "  variable inspection or runtime debugging UI. Debugger design belongs in",
            "  a separate later milestone.",
            "- Full find-references and rename refactoring, richer Glaze component/prop",
            "  semantics and cross-language navigation, formatter, hot reload and",
            "  testing workflow integration remain in phase 6.",
            "- Wares/package tooling, MCP tooling and the coding-agent guide also",
            "  remain in phase 6.",
            "- Phase 3 still owns implementing and serving Glazes as frontend",
            "  components; phase 2.5 only improves editing support for their syntax.",
            "",
            "## Design rule",
            "",
            "The VS Code extension is a thin client over `kiln lsp` and the Kiln CLI.",
            "Any feature that benefits other editors should live in the language server,",
            "not be implemented only for VS Code.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/phase-3", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Phase 3 — Glazes, native styling, Finishes and themes",
            "",
            "**Status:** In progress. Step 1 (compiled Glaze rendering) is done; see",
            "[Rendering](../reference/glazes.md#rendering) for what works today. The",
            "[Rust runtime migration](runtime-rust.md) it waited for is complete.",
            "",
            "**Goal:** Build Kiln's multi-page documentation website **in Kiln**, using",
            "compiled server-rendered Glazes, scoped and imported CSS, typed `ui` style",
            "records, design tokens and reusable **Finishes**. The site must be",
            "restylable—including layout changes—by selecting another compatible theme",
            "without rewriting its content or Glazes.",
            "",
            "This is the **agreed Phase 3 scope and acceptance plan**, not a statement",
            "that these facilities already work. New code examples are **target syntax**.",
            "Existing [Glaze syntax](../reference/glazes.md), [type rules](../reference/types.md) and",
            "[design decisions](../design.md) remain authoritative for implemented features.",
            "See the [roadmap](../../README.md#roadmap) for overall phase sequencing and",
            "[vision](../vision.md) for the wider aims.",
            "",
            "## 1. Design decisions",
            "",
            "1. **Server rendering first.** Kiln owns routes explicitly in source files.",
            "   Continue using `web.render(\"page.glaze\", { props })`; no automatic",
            "   file-based routing, interpreter or required client-side framework.",
            "2. **Preserve the existing Glaze language.** Render the already-checked",
            "   props, layouts, slots, components, loops, branches, matches and",
            "   interpolation. Do not replace the template grammar with a new one.",
            "3. **Styling is co-located when useful.** A Glaze may contain markup,",
            "   logic and scoped `<style>`, or import ordinary CSS/CSS Modules.",
            "   Both are first-class; developers never have to choose one approach",
            "   globally.",
            "4. **Native `ui` follows structured approach B.** Styles are typed",
            "   Kiln record expressions using short, Tailwind-inspired keys:",
            "   `bg`, `fg`, `p`, `px`, `gap`, `rounded`, etc. No space-separated",
            "   class string is necessary, and no additional mini-language.",
            "5. **Logic may select styling seamlessly.** Use ordinary expressions,",
            "   `if` and `match` to produce `Ui` or choose Finish variants.",
            "6. **Finishes are simply functions.** \"Finish\" is the name for an ordinary",
            "   Kiln function returning `Ui` or a record of `Ui` values. No",
            "   `finish` keyword, dedicated declaration kind or hidden evaluator.",
            "7. **Themes are typed contracts.** A theme groups token values and",
            "   compatible Finishes; a theme can change layout as well as colour.",
            "   Phase 3 requires **build-time theme replacement**, not reactive",
            "   browser-side theme switching.",
            "8. **CSS is the standard and the output.** Generate conventional CSS",
            "   assets; preserve cascade, selectors and ordinary HTML `class`.",
            "   Native styling should not require Tailwind, Node or another runtime.",
            "9. **Compiler and LSP own the integration.** Type-check properties,",
            "   tokens, CSS Module class exports, Finish arguments/parts and theme",
            "   compatibility. Reuse maintained Rust CSS tooling for CSS parsing and",
            "   transformations.",
            "10. **Keep future phases separate.** Embers, reactive hydration and",
            "    typed RPC are Phase 4; full ORM/auth/forms are Phase 5; comprehensive",
            "    refactoring and hot reload are later milestones.",
            "",
            "### Terminology",
            "",
            "| Concept | Meaning |",
            "| --- | --- |",
            "| **Glaze** | A template/component in a `.glaze` file |",
            "| **`Ui`** | Built-in type representing a validated style value |",
            "| **`ui({ ... })`** | Typed style constructor used in Kiln functions |",
            "| **`ui={{ ... }}`** | Contextual Glaze attribute shorthand for a style record |",
            "| **Finish** | Ordinary function returning a `Ui` or typed multi-part `Ui` record |",
            "| **Finish variant** | Normal function parameter, often an enum |",
            "| **Theme** | Compatible group of Finishes and design-token values |",
            "| **Token** | Typed CSS-custom-property reference, e.g. `color.surface` |",
            "",
            "## 2. End-to-end developer experience",
            "",
            "### A page with markup, logic and styling",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{# post_card.glaze — target Phase 3 syntax #}",
            "{% props post: Post, featured: Bool = false %}",
            "{% let s = active_theme().post_card(featured) %}",
            "",
            "<article ui={ui.merge(s.root, ui({ p: space.lg }))}>",
            "    <h2 ui={s.title}>{post.title}</h2>",
            "    <p class=\"summary\" ui={{ fg: color.muted }}>",
            "        {post.summary}",
            "    </p>",
            "    <a href={post.url} ui={s.action}>Read more</a>",
            "</article>",
            "",
            "<style>",
            "    .summary {",
            "        text-wrap: pretty;",
            "    }",
            "</style>",
            "```",
            "",
            "This should be easy to understand in one editor view. Styling can be",
            "extracted into a Finish without changing the Glaze's structure.",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "enum CardTone { Normal, Featured }",
            "",
            "struct PostCardFinish {",
            "    root: Ui",
            "    title: Ui",
            "    action: Ui",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn editorial_post_card(featured: Bool) -> PostCardFinish {",
            "    {",
            "        root: ui({",
            "            grid: true,",
            "            cols: 1,",
            "            gap: space.md,",
            "            bg: color.surface,",
            "            rounded: radius.none,",
            "            at: { desktop: { cols: 2 } }",
            "        }),",
            "        title: ui({",
            "            fg: color.heading,",
            "            weight: .bold,",
            "            textSize: if featured { .xl } else { .lg }",
            "        }),",
            "        action: ui({ fg: color.accent, underline: true })",
            "    }",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "**Syntax contract:** In a Glaze, `ui={{ ... }}` means an attribute",
            "expression containing an ordinary record literal, contextually converted",
            "to `Ui`. In a Kiln function, use `ui({ ... })`. Existing style values",
            "pass as `ui={s.root}`. These are two entry points to the *same* type",
            "and CSS generator, not parallel styling systems.",
            "",
            "## 3. Glaze compilation and render semantics",
            "",
            "### Rendering pipeline",
            "",
            "- Extend the existing AST/type-checker and lower Glazes into typed render",
            "  functions in Kiln IR. Compile with the native Cranelift backend and",
            "  retain parity with the C backend as supported.",
            "- Render HTML with proper output escaping. Handle static text,",
            "  interpolated expressions, typed props and defaults, HTML attributes,",
            "  void elements, locals, conditional blocks, loops/filters/empty",
            "  branches and exhaustive matches.",
            "- Compile component calls with typed props, and layout nesting with",
            "  named/default slots. Reuse current component resolution rules from",
            "  `docs/reference/glazes.md` instead of inventing a new layout language.",
            "- Implement `web.render` to execute the compiled template and return an",
            "  HTML response with `text/html; charset=utf-8`. Route failures through",
            "  existing web error handling; a JSON or text route should still work",
            "  without any Glaze.",
            "- Keep routes explicit, e.g. `app.get(\"/guide\", fn(req) {",
            "  web.render(\"guide.glaze\", { ... }) })`. One folder remains a program;",
            "  general directory modules belong to the later Wares/module work.",
            "- Make generated output inspectable and deterministic, with compiler",
            "  diagnostics tied to the original Glaze source spans.",
            "",
            "### Security and correctness",
            "",
            "- Escape interpolation according to **HTML text vs quoted attribute**",
            "  context. Validate dynamic URL-bearing attributes to prevent",
            "  `javascript:` and other dangerous protocol injection.",
            "- `{raw ...}` stays an explicit trust boundary, with tests and clear docs",
            "  about untrusted HTML.",
            "- Never concatenate untrusted inputs into CSS selectors, generated class",
            "  identifiers, stylesheet text or raw style attributes.",
            "- Detect unknown component/layout references, invalid props, malformed",
            "  nesting and recursive template cycles with useful errors rather than",
            "  crashing the compiler.",
            "- Handle special characters, Unicode, empty branches and render failures",
            "  consistently on Linux/Windows. Do not emit partial responses on",
            "  recoverable template errors where headers have not been sent.",
            "- Avoid uncontrolled recursion/output growth through reasonable",
            "  runtime/compiler safeguards and targeted tests.",
            "",
            "## 4. Typed `Ui` styling",
            "",
            "### Initial shorthand surface",
            "",
            "| Area | Keys | Intended semantics |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| Colour | `bg`, `fg`, `borderColor` | Background, foreground/text and border colour |",
            "| Spacing | `p`, `px`, `py`, `pt`, `pr`, `pb`, `pl`, `m`, `mx`, `my`, `gap` | Typed spacing and margins |",
            "| Sizing | `w`, `h`, `minW`, `maxW`, `minH`, `maxH` | Typed dimensions |",
            "| Surface | `rounded`, `border`, `shadow` | Radius, border and elevation |",
            "| Layout | `flex`, `grid`, `direction`, `cols`, `rows`, `items`, `justify`, `wrap` | Common flex/grid patterns |",
            "| Typography | `font`, `textSize`, `weight`, `lineHeight`, `align`, `tracking` | Type and readability |",
            "| Effects | `opacity`, `overflow`, `transition`, `cursor`, `transform`, `underline` | Common visual interaction |",
            "| States | `hover`, `focus`, `focusVisible`, `disabled` | Nested typed style records |",
            "| Responsive | `at: { tablet: { ... } }` | Named breakpoints from token declarations |",
            "",
            "Names use Tailwind-inspired brevity for common operations, but prefer",
            "unambiguous words such as `textSize` over introducing dozens of obscure",
            "one-letter aliases. `fg` always means text colour; `bg` means the",
            "background paint. Rare CSS properties belong in ordinary CSS, not in an",
            "ever-growing duplicated CSS vocabulary.",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "<article ui={{",
            "    grid: true,",
            "    cols: 1,",
            "    gap: space.md,",
            "    p: space.lg,",
            "    bg: color.surface,",
            "    fg: color.text,",
            "    rounded: radius.card,",
            "    hover: { shadow: shadow.lg },",
            "    at: { tablet: { cols: 2 } },",
            "    borderColor: if featured {",
            "        color.accent",
            "    } else {",
            "        color.transparent",
            "    }",
            "}}>",
            "    {post.title}",
            "</article>",
            "```",
            "",
            "### Type rules",
            "",
            "- `Ui` is a compiler-recognized style type. Ordinary records stay",
            "  ordinary records, except where a `Ui` is expected or `ui({ ... })`",
            "  explicitly constructs one.",
            "- `bg` accepts appropriate paint/colour values, `p` expects spacing,",
            "  `rounded` expects radius/length, `cols` expects a suitable grid",
            "  column count, etc. Wrong types are compiler errors with useful",
            "  diagnostics and suggested alternatives.",
            "- Static variants and scoped responsive/pseudo-state declarations are",
            "  represented in a typed style IR, not flattened into strings early.",
            "- Mutually incompatible keys (e.g. `flex: true` and `grid: true` in",
            "  one effective state) get a diagnostic. Unknown breakpoints/tokens",
            "  also get diagnostics.",
            "- Use existing Kiln expressions, enums, conditionals and matches for",
            "  style selection, without a separate conditional styling syntax.",
            "- Keep CSS units explicit and typed; don't quietly accept arbitrary",
            "  unvalidated interpolated strings as styles.",
            "",
            "### Generation and state",
            "",
            "- Compile statically enumerable styles into deterministic CSS classes.",
            "  Deduplicate identical rules and ship them as standard CSS assets.",
            "- Request-time conditions choose among **finite precompiled variants**,",
            "  switching class IDs rather than generating a stylesheet per request.",
            "- `hover`, `focusVisible` etc. become standard CSS selectors;",
            "  responsive `at` blocks become media queries. No JavaScript required.",
            "- Truly arbitrary dynamically computed CSS values may be deferred or",
            "  supported only through safe typed CSS custom-property bindings;",
            "  arbitrary runtime CSS generation is **not required** in Phase 3.",
            "- Generated styles must preserve inherited CSS custom properties so",
            "  theme values can affect components across Glaze boundaries.",
            "",
            "### Composition: `ui.merge`",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "ui.merge(",
            "    ui({ p: space.lg, bg: color.surface }),",
            "    ui({ px: space.sm }),",
            "    if selected {",
            "        ui({ borderColor: color.accent })",
            "    } else {",
            "        ui({})",
            "    }",
            ")",
            "```",
            "",
            "- Merge **left to right**; later arguments override earlier values for",
            "  the same normalized CSS property and state/breakpoint.",
            "- Normalize `p` into individual edges so `px` overrides only left/right,",
            "  not top/bottom. Do the same for other overlapping aliases.",
            "- Nested states and breakpoint values merge property-by-property.",
            "- Merge semantics apply to `Ui` values, not to arbitrary imported CSS:",
            "  ordinary stylesheet cascade rules still determine external overrides.",
            "- Generated CSS order, identifiers and output remain reproducible.",
            "",
            "## 5. Standards-based CSS and token system",
            "",
            "### Scoped local CSS",
            "",
            "A Glaze can include ordinary `<style> ... </style>`. CSS is parsed as",
            "CSS, **not** Kiln code; Glaze interpolation does not activate inside",
            "it. By default, styles are scoped to the owning Glaze by generated",
            "selectors/attributes, **without Shadow DOM**.",
            "",
            "- Inheritance and custom-property cascade must continue across",
            "  component boundaries.",
            "- Support pseudo-classes/elements, nested selectors, keyframes,",
            "  media/container queries and modern CSS syntax through a maintained",
            "  CSS parser/transformer.",
            "- Provide a documented `:global(...)` escape for selectors that must",
            "  intentionally cross component boundaries.",
            "- Do not double-apply styles, leak selectors across same-named classes,",
            "  rewrite CSS custom-property names incorrectly or change global reset",
            "  semantics unintentionally.",
            "",
            "### Plain CSS and CSS Modules",
            "",
            "Use explicit, narrowly scoped Glaze import directives:",
            "",
            "```glaze",
            "{% css \"./site.css\" %}",
            "{% css module styles from \"./card.module.css\" %}",
            "",
            "<article class={styles.card} ui={{ p: space.md }}>",
            "    <h2 class={styles.title}>Hello</h2>",
            "</article>",
            "```",
            "",
            "- Plain `.css` import stays **global** and respects the CSS cascade.",
            "  Standard `@import` in CSS must also be handled or passed through",
            "  safely with resolvable asset paths.",
            "- `.module.css` import generates hashed class identifiers with",
            "  *typed* keys. `styles.card` works; `styles.unknown` fails in",
            "  `kiln check` and should offer close suggestions.",
            "- CSS files belong to the dependency graph, with watched changes,",
            "  source spans, path completion, go-to-definition and helpful",
            "  diagnostics. Guard against reading files outside allowed roots.",
            "- Preserve plain HTML `class`, `class={...}` and `style` attributes,",
            "  so CSS frameworks and existing markup remain interoperable.",
            "",
            "### Design tokens",
            "",
            "The **source of values is ordinary CSS custom properties**; Kiln",
            "indexes them into typed token namespaces using documented categories",
            "rather than requiring duplicate declarations in a bespoke token DSL.",
            "",
            "```css",
            "/* design.css: baseline theme tokens */",
            ":root {",
            "    --color-surface: #faf9f6;",
            "    --color-text: #282828;",
            "    --color-accent: #7144bf;",
            "    --color-muted: #696969;",
            "    --space-md: 1rem;",
            "    --space-lg: 1.5rem;",
            "    --radius-card: 0.75rem;",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "ui({",
            "    bg: color.surface,     // var(--color-surface)",
            "    fg: color.text,        // var(--color-text)",
            "    p: space.lg,           // var(--space-lg)",
            "    rounded: radius.card  // var(--radius-card)",
            "})",
            "```",
            "",
            "- Index and type token categories such as colours, spacing, radii,",
            "  typography, shadows, motion and breakpoints. Prefer a small clear",
            "  naming scheme (`--color-*`, `--space-*` etc.) over a new styling",
            "  language. Type-check token references and CSS values where possible.",
            "- Tokens resolve to CSS variables, not compile-time fixed values:",
            "  changing the active CSS theme value should propagate everywhere.",
            "- Provide an LSP-visible token catalog; hover can show its definition,",
            "  expected kind and currently selected default value.",
            "- Different compatible themes must supply the required tokens with the",
            "  same categories. Missing or mistyped token definitions are errors.",
            "- Tokens alter appearances and some layouts, but cannot change DOM",
            "  structure. Finishes handle more substantial style/layout changes.",
            "",
            "### Cascade and conflicts",
            "",
            "Define deterministic CSS output layers and order for Kiln-controlled",
            "styles, conceptually **theme/global → component CSS → scoped local CSS",
            "→ Finish/inline `ui`**. Specify actual layer names and specificity in",
            "tests; standard CSS cascade always applies (including unlayered rules",
            "and `!important`). Do not promise that `ui` overrides arbitrary",
            "external CSS regardless of browser semantics.",
            "",
            "## 6. Finishes and themes",
            "",
            "### A Finish is ordinary Kiln",
            "",
            "No special declaration, annotation, interpreter or separate recipe",
            "configuration. A Finish can return one style or a record of styles:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "enum ButtonTone { Primary, Danger }",
            "",
            "fn button(tone: ButtonTone) -> Ui {",
            "    let base = ui({",
            "        p: space.md,",
            "        rounded: radius.button,",
            "        weight: .medium",
            "    })",
            "    let variant = match tone {",
            "        .Primary => ui({ bg: color.primary, fg: color.onPrimary })",
            "        .Danger => ui({ bg: color.danger, fg: color.onDanger })",
            "    }",
            "    ui.merge(base, variant)",
            "}",
            "",
            "struct CardFinish {",
            "    root: Ui",
            "    title: Ui",
            "    summary: Ui",
            "    action: Ui",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn card(featured: Bool) -> CardFinish {",
            "    {",
            "        root: ui({ flex: true, direction: .column, gap: space.md }),",
            "        title: ui({ fg: color.heading, weight: .bold }),",
            "        summary: ui({ fg: color.muted }),",
            "        action: ui({ fg: color.accent })",
            "    }",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "The multi-part names `root`, `title`, `summary` and `action` are just",
            "**ordinary typed record fields**. They are not Glaze content slots,",
            "and they cannot create markup. A Glaze owns its structure and may",
            "apply several fields from the same Finish.",
            "",
            "### Typed theme contracts",
            "",
            "The smallest composable theme contract is a Kiln type describing the",
            "Finish functions and their parameter/return types:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "struct SiteTheme {",
            "    card: fn(Bool) -> CardFinish",
            "    button: fn(ButtonTone) -> Ui",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn active_theme() -> SiteTheme {",
            "    editorial_theme()",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "The concrete functions `editorial_theme()` and `vibrant_theme()` each",
            "produce a compatible `SiteTheme` value using regular Kiln functions.",
            "No extra `theme` keyword or compiler-only Finish subclass is needed.",
            "",
            "- Theme contract checks include function signature compatibility,",
            "  enum parameters, return style types, multi-part record fields and",
            "  required token categories.",
            "- For Phase 3, allow implementations within the existing same-folder",
            "  Kiln program model; do not require the later subdirectory module",
            "  system or a theme package registry.",
            "- **Theme switch acceptance:** change one `active_theme()` selection",
            "  and rebuild. Both themes must render the **same Glazes and content**",
            "  with appreciably different visual identities, including a layout",
            "  change such as grid versus flex.",
            "- Local overrides can merge over themed Finishes explicitly. Theme",
            "  switching must not require changing the content model or component",
            "  props.",
            "- Static analysis gathers the finite styles reachable from candidate",
            "  theme functions. Including both themes' CSS in the initial bundle",
            "  is acceptable; tree-shaking unused themes and live browser toggling",
            "  are later optimizations.",
            "- Don't execute arbitrary Kiln application code at build time to",
            "  discover styles: compile literal style branches and enumerate",
            "  statically representable variants; report limitations explicitly.",
            "",
            "## 7. Build pipeline, linking and assets",
            "",
            "1. Parse/type-check Kiln files and Glazes together, including",
            "   component/layout and Finish references.",
            "2. Lower Glaze rendering to IR and compile for both native targets,",
            "   keeping the reference C backend tested where supported.",
            "3. Create style IR from contextual `Ui` values, normalize aliases,",
            "   collect token dependencies, extract finite styles and emit",
            "   deterministic CSS with hashed identifiers.",
            "4. Process imported/local styles using maintained CSS tooling;",
            "   scope local rules, resolve modules and produce CSS assets.",
            "5. Bundle HTML render code and required CSS/fonts/images into the",
            "   supported standalone executable, preserving one-command",
            "   `kiln run`/`kiln build` semantics.",
            "6. Serve resources with correct MIME types, stable URLs/cache",
            "   behaviour and no source/config/secrets exposure. Guard against",
            "   path traversal and accidental inclusion of private files.",
            "",
            "**Do not add a Node/Tailwind runtime or require a separate CSS builder",
            "on users' machines.** Build-from-source may still use Rust tools;",
            "downloaded Kiln and generated executables remain self-contained",
            "on their supported targets.",
            "",
            "A single CSS style appearing in multiple components should not",
            "generate duplicate runtime copies; generated output must be",
            "reproducible given identical inputs.",
            "",
            "## 8. Editor and diagnostics",
            "",
            "Extend the existing **Rust Kiln LSP** and the VS Code extension,",
            "not a second independent checker.",
            "",
            "- Highlight mixed Glaze HTML, Kiln expressions, `ui` keys/values and",
            "  embedded CSS correctly.",
            "- Complete `Ui` keys based on expected style type, and tokens by",
            "  category; provide errors for unknown/incompatible properties,",
            "  invalid breakpoints and conflicting aliases.",
            "- Complete, hover and navigate Finish functions, parameters,",
            "  multi-part fields and token declarations.",
            "- Use mature CSS language tooling where possible for embedded CSS",
            "  syntax, property completion, diagnostics and import navigation.",
            "- Resolve CSS Modules, give typed class completion and diagnostic",
            "  for missing classes, and navigate to declarations.",
            "- Handle multiple Glazes, live/unsaved buffers, UTF-16 LSP offsets",
            "  and recovering from malformed work-in-progress code.",
            "- Full generated-CSS hover previews, refactoring and cross-file",
            "  rename remain later enhancements, not completion blockers.",
            "",
            "## 9. Implementation steps and review gates",
            "",
            "Make these separate, reviewable PR-sized steps, with tests in every",
            "step and no expansion into Embers or the general module system.",
            "",
            "**Step 1 — Compiled Glaze rendering**",
            "",
            "- AST → typed IR → native HTML render functions.",
            "- `web.render`, props, interpolation, attributes, escaping and HTML",
            "  content type.",
            "- **Gate:** a minimal page renders from a compiled native server,",
            "  on Linux and Windows, with no template interpreter.",
            "",
            "**Step 2 — Composable components and layouts**",
            "",
            "- Typed components, props/defaults, layouts, nested/default/named",
            "  slots, locals, loops/empty branches, conditions and matches.",
            "- Preserve current checker rules and clean diagnostics.",
            "- **Gate:** multi-page SSR app with shared layout and reusable",
            "  components; HTML/XSS regression tests pass.",
            "",
            "**Step 3 — Standards CSS pipeline**",
            "",
            "- Rust CSS parser/transformer, scoped local `<style>`,",
            "  global CSS directives, CSS Modules, assets and cascade tests.",
            "- **Gate:** duplicate local class names do not collide; CSS Module",
            "  exports are typed; CSS/fonts/images load from standalone binary.",
            "",
            "**Step 4 — Typed `Ui` and tokens**",
            "",
            "- Style value/type and contextual record conversion; shorthand",
            "  properties, states, responsive `at`, static/finite CSS extraction,",
            "  design token indexing and deterministic `ui.merge`.",
            "- **Gate:** nontrivial responsive component and conditional styling",
            "  work without Tailwind; wrong style/token types fail at compile time.",
            "",
            "**Step 5 — Finishes and theme contracts**",
            "",
            "- Finish functions, typed multi-part records, enum variants,",
            "  theme-shape function fields, compile-time theme choice and",
            "  compatible token value sets.",
            "- **Gate:** switching one theme-selection function changes",
            "  typography/colours/spacing **and layout** without editing any",
            "  application Glaze or content.",
            "",
            "**Step 6 — Editor, packaging and examples**",
            "",
            "- LSP token/CSS/Finish intelligence, VSIX smoke tests,",
            "  CSS output integrity and cross-target build tests.",
            "- Document style alias names/types, escaping, cascading, scoping,",
            "  token naming, CSS imports and theme contracts.",
            "- **Gate:** editing Glazes with live diagnostics and style",
            "  completion in VS Code feels like editing an established language.",
            "",
            "**Step 7 — Kiln docs site (dogfood acceptance)**",
            "",
            "- Build Kiln's **actual documentation website** with Kiln routing",
            "  and Glazes: landing, installation, getting started, language",
            "  reference and navigation/shared layouts. Content can initially",
            "  reuse authored docs through a controlled content-loading path.",
            "- Use native `ui`, local CSS, an external CSS file, CSS Modules,",
            "  design tokens, Finishes and two compatible themes.",
            "- Run e2e tests for rendered HTML, static assets, responsiveness,",
            "  style output, accessibility basics and security.",
            "- **Gate:** all checks below are met. Only then mark Phase 3 done",
            "  in the README.",
            "",
            "## 10. Definition of done",
            "",
            "- [ ] The compiler **renders**, not merely checks, existing",
            "      Glaze templates through `web.render`, including typed props,",
            "      components, layouts, slots, loops, conditionals and matches.",
            "- [ ] HTML and attributes are safely escaped, raw HTML is explicit,",
            "      security/injection and error-path tests pass.",
            "- [ ] `Ui`, `ui({ ... })`, `ui={{ ... }}`, conditional variants,",
            "      `ui.merge` and native aliases generate valid deterministic CSS.",
            "- [ ] Design tokens are indexed from CSS into **typed** Kiln names;",
            "      invalid/missing tokens or style property types give useful",
            "      compiler diagnostics.",
            "- [ ] Embedded `<style>` has predictable scoping; global CSS and",
            "      CSS Modules import and bundle correctly, with type-checked",
            "      module class names and CSS-standard cascade behaviour.",
            "- [ ] Finishes are regular functions with typed variants and",
            "      optional multi-part records; theme contracts reject",
            "      missing/incompatible Finish functions and parts.",
            "- [ ] Two compatible themes substantially reskin the same",
            "      Glazes (including layout), selected in one place and rebuilt.",
            "- [ ] LSP/VS Code support inline CSS, token names, Finish calls,",
            "      imports and styling errors in real multi-file workspaces.",
            "- [ ] Kiln's genuine **multi-page docs website runs in a self-contained",
            "      native executable** on supported Linux and Windows targets,",
            "      serving CSS, images and fonts without Node/Tailwind/extra",
            "      build dependencies for end users.",
            "- [ ] Existing CLI, HTTP API and both compilation backends remain",
            "      covered by CI as applicable; cross-target HTML/CSS smoke and",
            "      conformance tests pass.",
            "- [ ] A developer can start from a documented Glaze+Finish example,",
            "      style inline, extract a Finish, use CSS imports and switch",
            "      themes without learning a second style language.",
            "",
            "## 11. Explicitly deferred",
            "",
            "- **Phase 4:** Embers/WasmGC, reactive UI and style updates, selective",
            "  hydration, generated server RPC and JS/browser interop; see the",
            "  [Phase 4 plan](phase-4.md).",
            "- **Phase 5:** SQL/ORM, database migrations, auth/sessions, generated",
            "  admin UI and rich typed forms, integrating with existing Embers/RPC;",
            "  see the [Phase 5 plan](phase-5.md). Basic HTML forms are fine in Phase 3.",
            "- **Phase 6:** Wares, hot reload/formatter, advanced editor refactoring",
            "  and the integrated [`kiln test` runner](testing.md), covering **unit,",
            "  integration and automated browser/Glaze/Ember tests**. Phase 3 still",
            "  needs compiler/HTML/CSS smoke and regression tests but does not depend",
            "  on the finished public test command.",
            "- **Phase 7:** Static site export, deployment automation,",
            "  major runtime/compiler optimization and stability guarantees.",
            "- **Later:** automatic runtime theme switching, user theme",
            "  preferences, theme registry/distribution, style previews,",
            "  arbitrary dynamic computed CSS and new CSS-replacement DSLs.",
            "",
            "Do **not** use Phase 3 to reimplement every CSS feature in native",
            "`Ui`, introduce a mandatory styling framework, rewrite routing,",
            "or invent a dedicated language for Finishes.",
            "",
            "## 12. Final review principles",
            "",
            "- A Glaze should make its **structure, logic and common styling**",
            "  understandable together.",
            "- A Finish should feel like **ordinary Kiln code**, not configuration.",
            "- A theme should change a site's **visual identity** without",
            "  owning its content or business logic.",
            "- CSS should remain **native, familiar, accessible and inspectable**.",
            "- Styles should be **type-checked, secure and deterministic**.",
            "- New abstractions are justified only when they actually eliminate",
            "  glue; avoid novelty for its own sake.",
            "",
            "**In one sentence:** **Glazes define structure and behaviour, typed",
            "`Ui` records express local appearance, Finishes compose reusable",
            "presentation through ordinary functions, and themes change the",
            "visual identity—while CSS remains the underlying standard.**",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/phase-4", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Phase 4 — Embers, WasmGC and typed full-stack interactivity",
            "",
            "**Status:** Planned implementation and acceptance specification. Syntax snippets here describe **targets**, not functions that currently compile. The existing Glaze parser recognizes `{% state %}`, `on:...` and hydration hints, but compiling interactive Embers remains future work.",
            "",
            "**Post-implementation correction (2026-10-26):** Phase 4 landed with *implicit* Embers (a `.glaze` containing state/handlers). That source model is historical: Phase 4.1 replaces it with explicit `.ember` files. [Phase 4.1](phase-4.1.md) is the authoritative follow-up for component file kinds; it supersedes conflicting implicit classification language below, not the WasmGC, RPC, hydration or interop design. Use the current [Glaze and Ember reference](../reference/glazes.md#embers) for implemented source syntax.",
            "",
            "**Goal:** Complete Kiln's browser-interactivity and typed client/server language capabilities **before** the database and authentication framework. Phase 3's compiled Glazes and styles and the existing HTTP/JSON runtime suffice for browser WasmGC, selective hydration, reactive state, and checked `server fn` RPC. Phase 5 will add persistent data, sessions and authorization on top of that boundary.",
            "",
            "**Done when:** A native Kiln server runs a genuine **database-free, interactive website** with ordinary SSR content, selective WasmGC islands, typed state/events, and a browser calling a **pure or ephemeral-state server function** through generated RPC. Invalid/oversized/cross-origin calls are rejected. No ORM, database, user accounts, authentication framework or handwritten application JavaScript/RPC is required.",
            "",
            "This plan refines the [vision for Embers](../vision.md#embers-interactivity-when-and-where-needed), the [Glaze reference](../reference/glazes.md#embers) and [Phase 3](phase-3.md). **Phase 3 is still in active development**; this roadmap adjustment does not mark it complete. [Phase 5's SQL/auth plan](phase-5.md) and [Phase 6's ecosystem plan](phase-6.md) follow, rather than gate, browser interactivity.",
            "",
            "## 1. Architectural decisions",
            "",
            "1. **SSR is the baseline.** Ordinary Glazes render using Kiln's compiled server-side pipeline, with no Wasm or JS bootstrap on pages without interactive components.",
            "2. **Embers are compiled, not interpreted.** Client-reachable Kiln source is checked, lowered through shared typed IR where feasible, then compiled for the browser WasmGC target; no runtime Kiln interpreter or unrelated reactive DSL.",
            "3. **Server/client are distinct trust zones.** Browser code cannot directly import server-only functionality, native filesystem/OS/FFI or secret configuration. Phase 5's future `sql` and `auth` modules also stay server-only.",
            "4. **Hydration is selective.** Existing `client:load`, `client:visible`, `client:idle` and `client:only` syntax should have defined loading/fallback semantics; choose a sensible default for an Ember used without a hint.",
            "5. **Typed reactivity.** `{% state %}` is local typed state; `on:event` triggers ordinary Kiln handler logic. Changes update only affected DOM while preserving focus, selection, list identity and nested state where possible.",
            "6. **`server fn` is a checked network boundary.** Derive client proxy, request/response types, codecs and server handler from one declaration. Validate every request, explicitly expose allowed functions and provide policy hooks. Phase 4 can use public pure functions; Phase 5 supplies identity and per-record authorization.",
            "7. **Explicit async state.** Network failures, cancellation, stale responses, optimistic updates and error recovery must be observable. Never silently repeat unsafe mutations.",
            "8. **Use browser standards and existing tools.** Reuse maintained Wasm and JS interop libraries/bundlers, and permit ordinary HTML/CSS/JS integrations. The implementation is replaceable; Kiln developer-facing semantics are the durable contract.",
            "9. **No premature ecosystem scope.** Local multi-folder modules and an optional minimal `kiln.toml` follow in [Phase 4.2](phase-4.2.md); Wares, official UI Ware, lockfiles, public `kiln test`, formatter and compiler MCP remain Phase 6.",
            "10. **Testing now, polished runner later.** Extend compiler conformance and real-browser CI during Phase 4. Phase 6 supplies the unified public test runner, **including Ember hydration/RPC from its first release**.",
            "11. **WasmGC-only browser target for Phase 4.** Do not build or require a JavaScript code-generation fallback in this phase. When browser feature detection shows missing WasmGC support, preserve usable SSR HTML and native link/form navigation wherever possible; Ember-only functionality may be unavailable. A JavaScript fallback backend is a [long-term vision item](../vision.md#future-javascript-compiler-fallback), not a 1.0 gate. The minimal JavaScript bootstrap/interop bridge for WasmGC is not such a backend.",
            "",
            "## 2. Target developer experience",
            "",
            "A plain SSR Glaze stays unchanged:",
            "",
            "~~~glaze",
            "{% props title: String %}",
            "<article>",
            "    <h2>{title}</h2>",
            "    <p>A plain Glaze needs no browser runtime.</p>",
            "</article>",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "An Ember uses familiar Glaze syntax:",
            "",
            "~~~glaze",
            "{# Illustrative Phase 4 target; async syntax is not finalized #}",
            "{% props label: String %}",
            "{% state count = 0 %}",
            "",
            "<button on:click={ count += 1 }>{label}: {count}</button>",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "A server function can be **pure** Kiln:",
            "",
            "~~~kiln",
            "// Proposed browser-callable contract, not currently executable.",
            "server fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {",
            "    a + b",
            "}",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "An Ember could call its generated proxy without needing a database:",
            "",
            "~~~glaze",
            "{# Proposed async handler syntax — to be finalized #}",
            "{% state value = 0 %}",
            "<button on:click={ value = add(value, 1) catch err { value } }>",
            "    Server result: {value}",
            "</button>",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "An SSR page selectively hydrates a component:",
            "",
            "~~~glaze",
            "{% props title: String %}",
            "<article>",
            "    <h1>{title}</h1>",
            "    <Counter label=\"Clicks\" client:visible />",
            "</article>",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "**Important:** Phase 4 must **not** implement ORM, durable sessions or authentication merely to demonstrate RPC. It must, however, define a safe request-bound transport and authorization extension points so Phase 5 can layer session identity and per-record permissions without replacing generated routes or codecs. Async syntax is not finalized.",
            "",
            "## 3. WasmGC compiler and runtime contract",
            "",
            "- Add a browser code-generation target from checked Kiln IR with semantics for structs/records/enums, optionals, generic specializations, closures, strings, collections and error paths. Use browser WasmGC allocation/collection rather than bundling native Boehm GC.",
            "- Build a **client-reachable** call graph separately from server-reachable code. Only needed browser types/functions, island logic and necessary minimal runtime helpers are shipped. This is early reachability/dead-code elimination, not the complete Phase 7 optimizer.",
            "- Track forbidden browser imports/effects during checking and emit source-linked diagnostics. The browser must never gain direct access to server secrets, operating-system resources or native handles; Phase 5's SQL and session APIs must also be server-only.",
            "- Define compiler-level interop and type conversion for JS/DOM strings, numbers, arrays, records, handles, callbacks and promises, including lifetime/GC and exception propagation.",
            "- Generate content-addressed Wasm and minimal JS bridge/assets through Kiln's existing asset pipeline. Release output must not require a separate JavaScript project or Node runtime on the deployed server.",
            "- Document a tested WasmGC browser support matrix and feature-detect unsupported platforms before initializing an Ember. Preserve usable SSR content and native navigation/form behaviour when interactivity cannot run. Do not implement a separate JavaScript code-generation fallback in Phase 4; that is a longer-term compatibility goal.",
            "- Source maps/error traces and useful debug symbols should point back to Kiln/Glaze where feasible; don't leak secret-only server code into browser assets.",
            "",
            "**Gate:** a real browser runs generated WasmGC from a Kiln program with representative data structures, errors, generic code and closures; C/native and Wasm outputs have shared semantic regression cases.",
            "",
            "## 4. Ember islands and hydration",
            "",
            "- A Glaze using `{% state %}` or `on:...` becomes an Ember and remains SSR-capable unless `client:only` explicitly opts out.",
            "- Define when `client:load`, `client:visible`, `client:idle` and `client:only` fetch/init an island, including fallback for browsers without the relevant scheduling API.",
            "- Server HTML contains stable island IDs and escaped, explicitly serializable public initial props. Protect against HTML/script context injection (including breaking out of `</script>`), unsafe URL/attribute and malicious user-provided content.",
            "- Browser reconciles SSR DOM with typed state. A mismatch is diagnosed, not silently duplicated; stable keys preserve per-item state in lists and avoid accidentally resetting unrelated islands.",
            "- Nested Embers share runtime assets where practical without unintentionally sharing local state. Listener/task cleanup runs when an island unmounts.",
            "- No Ember code must be loaded on an ordinary static-only Glaze route, and ordinary SSR navigation/form submissions should work without JavaScript.",
            "",
            "**Gate:** a server-rendered page has two independent Embers with different hydration hints alongside static content; real browsers exhibit correct timing, state isolation and fallback.",
            "",
            "## 5. Reactive state, handlers and forms",
            "",
            "- `{% state name = expr %}` creates local, typed state initialized consistently with SSR. Writes trigger minimal DOM updates and batched invalidation, with deterministic ordering and no hidden full-page rerender.",
            "- Typed event handlers initially cover click/input/change/submit/keydown and standard event data; provide explicit prevent-default/propagation semantics.",
            "- Respect native controls, labels, IME input, focus, cursor, selection, keyboard accessibility and HTML validation behaviour. Support conditional children, lists and keyed identity.",
            "- Lifecycle/effects/subscriptions need explicit cleanup so removed components do not leak listeners, timers or state updates. No global cross-user component state.",
            "- Phase 4 covers reactive input/event plumbing and basic typed value constraints **without** requiring a form/ORM framework. Phase 5 adds shared server-side validation and typed field-error feedback for Ember forms; ordinary POST fallback remains viable.",
            "- If reactive components need asynchronous work, represent pending/error/success and cancellation as typed, inspectable state rather than magical invisible promises.",
            "",
            "**Gate:** interactive form, counter, changing list and nested Ember respond properly; focus remains stable and disposed components don't continue updating.",
            "",
            "## 6. Typed server functions and generated RPC",
            "",
            "- `server fn` declarations are server-only implementations, with an explicit set of browser-callable exports. Do not accidentally expose every internal helper by generating guessable endpoints.",
            "- Compile serializers/codecs only for safe, serializable public types. Reject DB handles, file descriptors, closures/function pointers, secrets and unsupported native-only types in RPC arguments/results.",
            "- Generate typed client proxies and server routes with consistent message framing/versioning, clear errors, timeouts and cancellation. Runtime still validates untrusted client values, sizes and schema versions.",
            "- **Phase 4 baseline:** explicitly exposed functions, validated/bounded payloads, same-origin/origin checks, safe CORS/content-types, CSRF protection for credentialed mutations where applicable, rate limits/abuse controls and safe error messages. Public functions are deliberately marked as such, never implicitly granted privileges.",
            "- **Phase 5 extension point:** provide a secure request context and per-call policy hooks to add authenticated user/session/record authorization without changing client signatures or wire conventions.",
            "- Reject malformed, oversized, unauthorized, stale-version and untrusted inputs safely. Prevent accidental retry of non-idempotent writes.",
            "- Shared type definitions should eliminate duplicate DTOs, HTTP wrappers and JSON encoding glue; no invented client-only model semantics.",
            "",
            "**Gate:** a browser Ember receives a typed result from an explicitly exposed, pure `server fn`; an unexposed function, bad origin, malformed/oversized request or invalid codec is safely rejected. Phase 5 tests authenticated record mutations.",
            "",
            "## 7. Asynchrony, optimistic updates and consistency",
            "",
            "- Typed pending/success/failure states and explicit errors for `server fn` calls. Support cancellation on navigation/disposal and documented handling of concurrent responses arriving out of order.",
            "- Allow deliberate optimistic UI with rollback/reconciliation on server failure. Do not silently overwrite fresh state with stale network responses.",
            "- Provide observability for resource caching/invalidation if introduced; avoid a hidden browser ORM cache that users cannot inspect.",
            "- SSE/WebSocket subscription integration may follow as extension if required by dogfood apps, but isn't necessary to mark core typed RPC and state complete.",
            "",
            "**Gate:** double clicks, a delayed response, a failed optimistic mutation and navigation away leave client/server consistent and error feedback usable.",
            "",
            "## 8. JavaScript and browser interoperability",
            "",
            "**Finalized architecture (2026-10-25): Option B — strongly typed by default, with an explicit dynamic escape hatch.** Kiln authors must be able to use browser standards and compatible existing npm packages *without writing application JavaScript*. The compiler/toolchain generates the minimal JavaScript bootstrap, browser/WasmGC bridge and package bundling. This is **not** a JavaScript code-generation backend for Kiln; WasmGC remains the Phase 4 browser target.",
            "",
            "### 8.1 Typed browser APIs",
            "",
            "- Provide browser-targeted typed modules (initially `browser`) for representative DOM events, clipboard, storage, fetch and element references; expand to history/location, files, canvas, observers and other standards as needed. Wrap host APIs rather than reimplementing browser behavior.",
            "- Browser-only modules and functions are rejected when called from server-only code or during SSR evaluation. Browser effects belong in client lifecycle/tasks/event handlers. Browser permissions, secure-context requirements, same-origin and CSP rules continue to apply.",
            "- [Phase 4.1](phase-4.1.md), included in v0.9.0, replaces the original implicit Ember model with explicit `.ember` files. `.glaze` files are server-only and reject state and event handlers. Ordinary static Glazes continue to render without a browser bridge.",
            "",
            "### 8.2 npm packages with automatically generated bindings",
            "",
            "- Support direct, clearly namespaced npm imports in browser-targeted Kiln code, with a proposed convenience command such as `kiln add npm:canvas-confetti`; exact grammar/command naming remains a syntax/tooling decision, not a prerequisite to the architectural contract.",
            "- Resolve compatible browser exports and TypeScript declaration files (bundled types or `@types`) and translate **supported** types/signatures to Kiln bindings; compile those calls into the JS/WasmGC bridge and bundle only the required browser-side assets where possible.",
            "- Do not silently convert an unmappable typed API to an unchecked dynamic type. Explain unsupported overloads, complex TypeScript conditional types, Node-only built-ins, framework-specific runtime assumptions and missing declarations with source-linked diagnostics and suggested alternatives.",
            "- Use framework-independent browser libraries first. React-specific components, Node-only modules and arbitrary npm packages are **not** guaranteed to interoperate. Wares are Kiln-native packages; optional curated Wares may wrap popular npm libraries but are not required for ordinary supported npm imports.",
            "- Phase 4 needs a pinned, reproducible path for at least one small browser library, **without** requiring Phase 6's general Ware registry, multi-folder package system or public package-manager UX. Phase 6 can unify resolution, manifest/lockfile and dependency-management commands. Released builds must identify and integrity-check the resolved JS artifacts; installation must not execute arbitrary npm lifecycle scripts by default.",
            "",
            "### 8.3 Typed foreign values and explicit dynamic escape hatch",
            "",
            "- Provide opaque typed `js.Handle<T>`-style references for external JS/DOM object identities and an explicitly marked `js.Value`-style dynamic representation for APIs that generated bindings cannot model. These names describe the decided model; exact parser syntax and core type definitions remain to be finalized.",
            "- Dynamic calls/property access are opt-in and visibly foreign. **Never** make an unsupported operation implicitly dynamic. Converting `js.Value` into a Kiln value requires a checked, recoverable conversion; no unchecked casts by default.",
            "- Allow explicit Kiln-authored foreign binding declarations for awkward libraries. An escape hatch does not require developers to write JavaScript, although third-party JS implementations still execute in the browser.",
            "- Start with explicit conversion rules: JS `string`/boolean to Kiln `String`/`Bool`; JS `number` to `Float` unless a verified narrower conversion is requested; JS `bigint` to `Int` only after bounds checks; supported arrays/records to typed collections/records; `null` or `undefined` to optionals where their distinction is unimportant. Preserve their distinction explicitly if an API depends on it.",
            "- Specify copied values vs retained handles, equality/identity, handle table/rooting and cross-GC lifetime. No raw JavaScript object or host capability is treated as a serializable Kiln/server-RPC value by default.",
            "",
            "### 8.4 Async, callbacks, errors and DOM ownership",
            "",
            "- JS promises integrate with Kiln's agreed implicit async/task model and cancellation/error policies; no JavaScript `await` needs to appear in user code. Promise rejection and synchronous JS exceptions become typed, inspectable failures at the foreign boundary.",
            "- Callback closures can be passed to browser APIs with explicit rooting, lifetime and disposal semantics. Subscriptions, event listeners, observers, timers and retained JS handles must be released on Ember disposal/navigation where applicable.",
            "- JS-driven DOM libraries must integrate through an explicit owned/borrowed element boundary so they do not mutate Ember-owned reconciled DOM unpredictably. A JS library's mutations do **not** automatically become Kiln reactive state: foreign callbacks update Kiln state through explicit typed writes.",
            "- Respect browser security and permissions. Typed bindings improve developer confidence but do **not** sandbox third-party JS or eliminate supply-chain risk.",
            "",
            "### 8.5 Build and diagnostics",
            "",
            "- Generate deterministic, content-addressed Wasm and JS bridge/assets through Kiln's build pipeline, without a developer-maintained Vite/Webpack/npm project or a Node runtime on the deployed Kiln server. Existing build tools may be reused internally.",
            "- Produce diagnostics for server/browser misuse, failed type mapping, unsafe dynamic conversions, unsupported packages, stale/disposed handles, incompatible platform features, missing browser permissions and JS exceptions. Source locations should point back to Ember/Kiln where practical.",
            "- Static-only pages must not load an Ember bootstrap or third-party JS. Unsupported WasmGC browsers retain SSR content and native links/forms as far as possible; a separate fallback JS compiler remains a longer-term vision item.",
            "",
            "**Gate:** a real-browser Ember invokes a typed native browser API and a small pinned npm library without handwritten JavaScript; an explicitly dynamic `js.Value` call works and fails safely on a bad conversion; exception/promise/callback cleanup, Node-only import diagnostics, SSR/browser guardrails, CSP and production asset delivery pass conformance tests.",
            "",
            "## 9. Security, performance and cross-target CI",
            "",
            "| Domain | Positive cases | Negative and adversarial cases |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| Codegen/GC | Structs, optionals, closures, mutation, errors | Invalid captures, GC lifetime bugs, unsupported browser feature |",
            "| Hydration | SSR match, lazy island, nested list | Duplicate nodes, ID collisions, malformed state, script breakout |",
            "| Events/DOM | Focus, keyboard, conditional updates | Leaked listener, stale DOM, clobbered input |",
            "| RPC | Exposed typed pure function, result/error codec | Unexposed function, malformed/oversized input, invalid origin/CSRF, error leakage |",
            "| Async | Pending, cancellation, optimistic success | Stale response, duplicate mutation, failed rollback |",
            "| Interop | Typed browser API, pinned npm package, explicit dynamic call, JS callback/promise | Disposed handles, Node-only imports, failed checked conversions, SSR-only misuse, exception/cancellation |",
            "| Delivery | No Wasm on SSR-only page, Linux/Windows server | CSP breakage, wrong MIME, excessive unnecessary Wasm |",
            "",
            "- Real-browser smoke/regression testing belongs in Phase 4 despite the public `kiln test` runner arriving later.",
            "- Keep C/native server conformance green and test WasmGC semantics as a third target. Browser support is tested explicitly; standalone native application requirements remain.",
            "- Measure emitted Wasm size, initialization/hydration time, memory and DOM updates as a baseline, without requiring premature full compiler optimization.",
            "",
            "## 10. Implementation steps and gates",
            "",
            "| Step | Scope | Gate |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| **1 — WasmGC backend** | Typed IR/codegen, browser runtime subset and compatibility tests | Real-browser Kiln logic executes, comparing well with native |",
            "| **2 — SSR hydration** | Island identities, client asset emission, `client:*` policy and serialization | Two selective islands hydrate safely alongside static content |",
            "| **3 — State and events** | Reactive dependencies, typed handlers, DOM updates and disposal | Counter, list and form update without losing focus |",
            "| **4 — Server functions** | RPC contracts/codecs/server endpoints, validation/origin/CSRF hooks | Public pure function succeeds, unexposed/hostile calls fail without auth or a database |",
            "| **5 — Async UX** | Pending/cancel, stale results, optimistic rollback | Repeated actions/failure/reorder leave consistent state |",
            "| **6 — JS interoperability** | Typed browser API, TypeScript declaration bindings, pinned npm bundling, explicit dynamic escape hatch and JS bridge | Browser API + external library run without handwritten JS; invalid dynamic conversions and unsupported packages fail safely |",
            "| **7 — Polish** | Diagnostics/LSP, CSP/assets, cross-target CI, performance basics | Static pages ship no Ember runtime, platform suites pass |",
            "| **8 — Dogfood** | Real database-free SSR site with independent islands, reactive inputs and pure/ephemeral RPC | Full browser-server interaction works in Kiln without SQL/auth/JavaScript glue |",
            "",
            "Each step is a separately reviewable milestone with diagnostics, conformance/browser cases, documentation and acceptance. Avoid bundling the entire browser compiler/runtime into a single untestable PR.",
            "",
            "## 11. Definition of done",
            "",
            "- [ ] WasmGC executes checked Kiln browser code with coherent semantics for supported types, generics, closures, errors and GC.",
            "- [ ] Server-rendered Glazes remain default; Embers selectively hydrate and don't force full-page JavaScript.",
            "- [ ] Unsupported WasmGC browsers retain usable SSR markup and native navigation/form behaviour where possible, without a second JavaScript compiler target.",
            "- [ ] State/handlers update DOM predictably with safe lifecycle, list identity and keyboard/focus handling.",
            "- [ ] `server fn` calls compile into typed serialized RPC with failure, cancellation and version checks, without handwritten frontend route wrappers.",
            "- [ ] The RPC transport implements explicit export control, input validation, origin/CORS/CSRF protection as appropriate, bounded resources and request context/policy hooks ready for Phase 5 authentication.",
            "- [ ] Browser JS interoperability uses typed browser APIs and compatible npm bindings by default, provides an explicit checked dynamic escape hatch, bundles reproducibly without handwritten JavaScript and enforces browser-only/lifecycle constraints.",
            "- [ ] Deterministic Wasm/assets integrate with Phase 3 CSS/Finishes; static-only pages don't emit Ember code.",
            "- [ ] Real-browser/CI regression cases and Linux/Windows native server checks pass.",
            "- [ ] A database-free interactive Kiln app with SSR, islands, reactive state, and typed pure/ephemeral-state RPC works end-to-end without writing client JavaScript or transport glue.",
            "",
            "## 12. Explicit boundaries",
            "",
            "- **Phase 5:** SQL/ORM, schema import/migrations, typed forms/validation, sessions/auth, generated admin and authenticated database-backed Ember/RPC applications.",
            "- **Phase 4.2:** Local multi-folder modules, optional minimal `kiln.toml`, and compiler/editor module support.",
            "- **Phase 6:** Extended manifest dependency fields, `kiln.lock`, Wares/registry/global cache, official accessible UI Ware, public `kiln test` (with Ember tests **at launch**), formatter/live reload/refactoring and compiler MCP.",
            "- **Phase 7:** Deployment, static-site export, extensive optimization/hardening and long-term compatibility guarantees.",
            "- **Later:** Optional JavaScript fallback code generation for browsers without WasmGC (from the same checked Kiln semantics, subject to feasibility and cross-backend conformance), PWA offline/sync, advanced desktop reuse, arbitrary npm/React/Node compatibility, freestanding targets and complete debugger.",
            "- **Non-goals:** Forced SPA routing, client-side access to server data stores/secrets, runtime template interpretation or a mandatory second JavaScript framework.",
            "",
            "**In one sentence:** **Phase 4 completes Kiln's browser-interactivity and typed server/client language capabilities; Phase 5 then connects that boundary to persistence, accounts and authorization.**",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/phase-4.1", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Phase 4.1 — Explicit Ember components (`.ember`)",
            "",
            "**Status:** Complete; included in v0.9.0. The compiler, LSP and real-browser regression suites pass. This is the implementation contract for the corrective milestone after Phase 4 and before Phase 5. Phase 4 originally implemented Embers as `.glaze` files that implicitly became interactive when they contained `{% state %}` or `on:` handlers; Phase 4.1 replaces that source contract with explicit `.ember` files. The exit gate below is met.",
            "",
            "**Goal:** Make the server/client component boundary visible and enforceable at the file level: `.glaze` is a server-rendered template/component; `.ember` is a browser-capable component that may also render its initial HTML on the server. Preserve Phase 4's working WasmGC, hydration, state, typed RPC and JS/npm interoperability. **Do not rewrite or replace the runtime just to change the extension.**",
            "",
            "**Done when:** A project uses `.glaze` for SSR pages/layouts and `.ember` for reactive islands; the compiler, CLI, LSP, VS Code extension, conformance suite and browser tests all enforce and demonstrate the distinction. Static-only routes still ship no Ember runtime, and existing interactive examples work after an intentional migration.",
            "",
            "**Authority:** This document supersedes the *implicit Glaze-to-Ember classification* in [Phase 4](phase-4.md), notably its sections 2 and 4. All other Phase 4 contracts (WasmGC target, selective hydration, RPC, browser/npm interop, security and lifecycle) remain in force. [Phase 5](phase-5.md) may depend on this source model without taking ownership of it; [Phase 6](phase-6.md) owns general multi-folder modules and Wares.",
            "",
            "## 1. Decisions (not open design questions)",
            "",
            "1. **Extension declares component kind.** Only a `.ember` file defines an Ember. State or events **never implicitly promote** a `.glaze` file. No `client` keyword, special wrapper, or separate template language is required.",
            "2. **Shared declarative markup.** `.ember` uses the existing Glaze template grammar: typed `{% props %}`, `{% state %}`, `{% let %}`, control flow, HTML, components, slots, scoped styles, `ui`, and `on:` handlers where valid. `{% state %}` remains the explicit notation for mutable reactive values. The new suffix does **not** make ordinary `let` bindings mutable or reactive.",
            "3. **No state/handlers in `.glaze`.** A `.glaze` containing `{% state %}` or `on:` is a compile-time error with a source span and a suggestion to move/rename it to `.ember`. A `.glaze` remains a server-side component even if it uses a nested `.ember`.",
            "4. **`.ember` does not mean client-only.** By default an Ember produces SSR HTML and hydrates using the existing Phase 4 island mechanism. `client:only` keeps its existing explicit meaning. File kind does not imply loss of SSR, separate SPA routing, or access to server secrets in the browser.",
            "5. **Kind is stable even without state.** A `.ember` with no state/events is still an Ember source file, not silently downgraded to a Glaze. Do not choose component kind from template contents. Eliminate unnecessary assets only when this preserves all observable Ember semantics.",
            "6. **No permanent dual mode.** Kiln is pre-1.0. Migrate in-repository examples and tests rather than indefinitely accepting reactive `.glaze` files; keep diagnostics actionable instead of silently altering behavior.",
            "7. **Preserve isolation.** Browser-reachable code cannot use filesystem, server-only `web`/`sql`/`auth` capabilities or secrets. SSR evaluation of an Ember must not turn browser-only handler code into server-executed code. Maintain Phase 4 serialization and security restrictions.",
            "",
            "## 2. Developer-facing examples",
            "",
            "A server page and its layout remain ordinary Glazes:",
            "",
            "~~~glaze",
            "{# page.glaze — server-rendered only #}",
            "{% layout \"layout.glaze\" title: \"Counter\" %}",
            "<h1>Count</h1>",
            "<Counter label=\"Clicks\" client:visible />",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "An interactive component is a different source file:",
            "",
            "~~~glaze",
            "{# counter.ember — SSR initially, then WasmGC hydration #}",
            "{% props label: String %}",
            "{% state count = 0 %}",
            "<button on:click={ count += 1 }>{label}: {count}</button>",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "A plain `.glaze` containing that `state` directive or `on:click` must fail with an error pointing at the directive. Likewise, adding `client:visible` to a *plain Glaze component* must not quietly turn it into an Ember.",
            "",
            "These examples reuse current Phase 4 handler/state syntax; this milestone does **not** introduce a new reactivity model.",
            "",
            "## 3. Component lookup, compilation and build contract",
            "",
            "- **Discovery:** Include `.ember` alongside `.glaze` in the existing same-folder component discovery for `kiln check`, `run` and `build`. The current case-to-filename convention for tags (for example `<Counter>` to `counter.ember`) remains unchanged.",
            "- **Name conflicts:** If both `counter.glaze` and `counter.ember` would resolve to `<Counter>` in the same scope, give a deterministic ambiguity/error naming both files; never let directory iteration order choose one. Do not invent cross-folder namespace syntax in this milestone.",
            "- **Static vs interactive resolution:** A `.glaze` can contain a named `.ember`; a `.ember` may contain nested Embers and ordinary Glaze-provided content subject to the existing slot/rendering rules. Preserve state isolation, ownership, nesting and key identity in Phase 4.",
            "- **SSR and islands:** A referenced `.ember` uses the same SSR HTML, serialized public props/state, stable island identity, `client:load` (default), `client:idle`, `client:visible`, `client:only` and WasmGC bootstrap as the Phase 4 implementation. SSR-only output from a `.glaze` page is not itself an island.",
            "- **Asset reachability:** Emit browser Wasm/JS only for reachable/used Ember islands, not merely because `.ember` files exist in a project. Glaze-only pages must continue serving without client runtime assets.",
            "- **Guards:** Restrict hydration hints on component invocations to Ember targets; reject `client:*` on ordinary Glaze targets with a helpful error. Never infer client capabilities from `.glaze` source markup or from a filename that is not `.ember`.",
            "- **Layouts:** Keep layouts server-side (`.glaze`). Retain the existing error for assigning a page layout directly inside an Ember; Ember components can be placed inside Glaze pages/layouts.",
            "- **Shared types and RPC:** Keep current `.kiln` types and `server fn` RPC declarations. No duplicated DTOs, extra client imports, hand-written JS bridges, or separate browser frameworks are introduced.",
            "- **Source metadata:** Preserve the actual `.ember` source filename/line spans in parsing, checking, error traces, module discovery and emitted asset diagnostics. Avoid internally fabricating a `.glaze` path that confuses users.",
            "- **Cross-target support:** Compiler checking and native SSR must work on supported Linux/Windows backends; client compilation remains WasmGC. Do not regress the existing C/native/wasm conformance paths.",
            "",
            "## 4. Diagnostics and developer tools",
            "",
            "- Emit precise, actionable diagnostics for: `{% state %}` or `on:` inside `.glaze`; `client:*` targeting a Glaze; duplicate Glaze/Ember component names; invalid browser-only access across the server boundary; missing Ember component files; and unsupported Ember layouts.",
            "- Prefer a fix-it suggestion for renaming an interactive file to `.ember`; do **not** silently rename files or pick an extension based on syntax. Allocate final diagnostic codes consistently with the current catalog; do not reuse codes with different meanings.",
            "- Update the VS Code extension's file associations, icons, syntax highlighting, and editor commands so `.ember` is first-class. Ensure Kiln LSP check, completion, hover, go-to-definition, symbols, diagnostics and relevant source spans work in `.ember` and across `.glaze` -> `.ember` references.",
            "- Preserve existing `.glaze` highlighting, component navigation and formatter-compatible parsing where currently supported. Phase 6 can later extend these capabilities across multi-folder modules.",
            "",
            "## 5. Migration plan",
            "",
            "1. Inventory repository files using `{% state %}`, `on:` or implicit interactive component detection (including conformance fixtures, examples and the Kiln docs site's interactive components).",
            "2. Rename *only actually interactive components* from `.glaze` to `.ember`. Keep static pages, layouts and reusable SSR components as `.glaze`. Update any path-based includes/references and tests.",
            "3. Change parser/discovery/checker/compiler classification from \"state/handler makes a Glaze an Ember\" to \"`.ember` is an Ember source kind\"; remove the implicit promotion path and add errors for disallowed directives in Glazes.",
            "4. Reuse existing Phase 4 rendering and browser runtime code. Modify extension assumptions at the boundary rather than cloning Glaze and Ember compilers.",
            "5. Update docs reference, Getting Started examples, docs site's generated samples, vocabulary and CHANGELOG to reflect the new *implemented* syntax only when the code lands. Label existing Phase 4 behavior as historical if still referenced.",
            "6. Run baseline/regression tests, verify browser functionality manually, then release/tag. No database, ORM or broader project-system work is required to complete this change.",
            "",
            "## 6. Acceptance test matrix",
            "",
            "| Area | Positive case | Negative / regression case |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| Parsing and checking | `counter.ember` parses, checks and retains source spans | `{% state %}` and `on:click` in `counter.glaze` fail with helpful fixes |",
            "| Resolution | `<Counter>` in `page.glaze` resolves `counter.ember` | Same-name `counter.glaze` + `counter.ember` fails deterministically |",
            "| Hydration | SSR Ember hydrates for load/idle/visible; only skips SSR | `client:*` on a Glaze-only component is rejected |",
            "| Reactivity | Counter, form, list, nested islands, focus and keyed items work | No state leakage or duplicate listeners after dispose/navigation |",
            "| RPC and interop | Existing typed `server fn`, `browser` and npm paths still work | Browser cannot directly access server capabilities or secrets |",
            "| Static routes | Glaze-only docs/pages render with no Ember bootstrap | An unused `.ember` source does not force asset inclusion |",
            "| Tooling | Extension and LSP recognize/complete/navigate `.ember` | Missing file / wrong kind errors point at correct file and line |",
            "| Cross-target | Native server + browser tests; C/native/wasm conformance stays green | Generated assets and SSR serialization/security do not regress |",
            "",
            "**Required end-to-end fixture:** One app has a Glaze layout/page, a static child Glaze, at least two independent `.ember` components with different hydration hints, a typed RPC call, and a Glaze-only route. Run real-browser E2E checks for state, focus and RPC while asserting **zero Ember assets** on the Glaze-only route. The docs site's copy control is a useful real-world migration fixture; it is not a substitute for the full test matrix.",
            "",
            "## 7. Boundaries and handoff",
            "",
            "**In scope:** source classification, discovery/resolution, diagnostics, tests, first-class `.ember` editor/LSP support, repository migration and documentation updates.",
            "",
            "**Out of scope:** replacing WasmGC/reactivity/RPC, introducing a new client state syntax, changing option B browser/npm interop, Phase 5 SQL/ORM/auth/admin, Phase 6 multi-folder projects/Wares/test-runner, JS fallback targets, or broad performance optimization.",
            "",
            "**Implementation order:** (1) fail-first language + resolver tests; (2) source-kind/checker/compiler changes; (3) migration; (4) LSP/extension; (5) browser/security regression suite; (6) docs/release notes.",
            "",
            "**Exit gate:** A fresh developer can tell server-only and browser-capable source files apart by their suffix; compilation strictly enforces that intent; pre-existing Phase 4 capabilities still work; and the full CI/real-browser suite passes. **Only then mark Phase 4.1 complete and tag the corrected release.**",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/phase-4.2", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Phase 4.2 — Multi-folder projects and local modules",
            "",
            "**Status:** Implemented after [Phase 4.1](phase-4.1.md), before [Phase 5](phase-5.md).",
            "The [project reference](../reference/projects.md) documents the shipped syntax,",
            "root/entry precedence, strict initial manifest schema and component paths.",
            "",
            "**Goal:** Make Kiln practical for ordinary multi-folder applications *before* adding a database/ORM/admin framework. A developer can put routes, domain logic, types, server Glazes and browser Embers in different folders, import local modules, check and run the application from one root, and get useful cross-module diagnostics/editor navigation—with no Wares, registry, lockfile or Node project.",
            "",
            "**Done when:** A manifest-free multi-folder Kiln web app builds and runs on Linux and Windows; an optional minimal `kiln.toml` can select its entrypoint; visibility and import cycles are enforced; Glazes/Embers and static assets resolve correctly across module boundaries; native/C builds, LSP and conformance regression cases pass. Phase 5 uses this foundation for its database-backed dogfood app.",
            "",
            "**Ownership:** This milestone moves the **Phase 6.1 project foundation** out of [the Phase 6 plan](phase-6.md). It supersedes earlier documents assigning multi-folder local modules to Phase 6 (including historical Phase 4/4.1 boundary notes). Phase 6 retains Wares/external dependencies, `kiln.lock`, registry, integrated public `kiln test`, formatter/dev watcher, advanced refactoring and MCP. Phase 4.1's explicit `.glaze`/`.ember` source distinction is a prerequisite; do **not** modify the Ember reactivity/runtime contract while implementing folder modules.",
            "",
            "## 1. Required design decisions",
            "",
            "1. **Keep simple programs simple.** `kiln run hello.kiln` and `kiln run folder` continue to work with no project file, dependency installation or module declarations.",
            "2. **One folder, one module namespace.** Existing multiple `.kiln` files in a folder share declarations and types, while imports remain lexical/per-source-file. A child directory is a separate local module, **not** automatically merged into its parent.",
            "3. **Folders are imported explicitly.** `import posts` names a local folder module `posts/`; `import posts.search` names a nested module `posts/search/`. Use the same existing `import` form as standard modules. Names are resolved from the *project root* and stable in any importing source file, not dependent on the file's working directory.",
            "4. **Public means exported.** Declarations with `pub` are visible to importers; module-private declarations remain usable within that folder only. Support `pub fn`, `pub struct`, `pub enum` and `pub const`, and compatible existing public forms. Reject public signatures leaking inaccessible types.",
            "5. **Stable type identity.** Nominal types from separate module paths are distinct, even if names/fields happen to match. Qualified lookup cannot accidentally select a homonymous type or local declaration.",
            "6. **No hidden imports or initialization.** Importing a module makes its public API available but does not implicitly register routes, run database migrations, start jobs or execute top-level side effects. Caller explicitly invokes setup functions.",
            "7. **Optional project manifest only.** A tiny `kiln.toml` may identify a root and entrypoint, but is never required for local imports. Existing manifest-free programs are not forced to add one. Phase 6 extends this *same* file for external Wares/dependency configuration.",
            "8. **No silent ambiguity.** Duplicate public names, colliding module paths, unresolved imports, cyclic imports, bad root/entry and inconsistent path capitalization are compile-time diagnostics. The compiler never chooses based on filesystem enumeration order.",
            "9. **One dependency graph.** Module reachability, checking, codegen, Glaze/Ember assets and editor navigation share deterministic import and source-location information. This need not introduce a new runtime module loader.",
            "",
            "## 2. Target developer experience",
            "",
            "~~~text",
            "fieldnotes/",
            "  main.kiln",
            "  layout.glaze",
            "  posts/",
            "    models.kiln",
            "    routes.kiln",
            "    index.glaze",
            "    editor.ember",
            "    post.css",
            "  accounts/",
            "    users.kiln",
            "    routes.kiln",
            "    login.glaze",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "`main.kiln`:",
            "",
            "~~~kiln",
            "import web, posts, accounts",
            "",
            "fn main() ! {",
            "    let app = web.app()",
            "    posts.routes(app)",
            "    accounts.routes(app)",
            "    try app.listen(\":8080\")",
            "}",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "`posts/routes.kiln` can import `web`, declare `pub fn routes` and render a local `index.glaze`. All `.kiln` files inside `posts/` share the `posts` namespace; no `import posts.models` is needed inside that same folder merely to use its locally declared types. A different module importing `posts` sees only its public exports.",
            "",
            "Nested module imports use `import posts.search` for `posts/search/`; importing `posts` does not automatically expose its nested modules.",
            "",
            "An **optional** root `kiln.toml` may be used when a project has multiple candidate entrypoints:",
            "",
            "~~~toml",
            "[package]",
            "name = \"fieldnotes\"",
            "kind = \"app\"",
            "entry = \"main.kiln\"",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "These examples are implemented and covered by module conformance tests. A `[wares]` section, `kiln.lock`, registry source, `kiln add` and multi-package workspaces are expressly *not* required or implemented by this milestone.",
            "",
            "## 3. Project-root and module resolution",
            "",
            "- Explicit CLI file/folder target takes priority. For commands issued within a directory tree, the nearest **applicable** ancestor `kiln.toml` may establish project root and entrypoint; otherwise the explicitly selected folder is the root. Never walk past an explicitly selected root or unexpectedly capture an unrelated parent manifest.",
            "- `kiln run .` uses the manifest entry if present; otherwise a documented unambiguous conventional entry such as root `main.kiln`. If multiple plausible `main` entrypoints exist, diagnose and request explicit selection; do not choose arbitrarily.",
            "- Resolve local modules under the chosen root by folder segments, not arbitrary dynamic filesystem paths. A module exists when its target folder contains suitable Kiln source. A directory without source is not an implicitly executable module.",
            "- Resolve `import web` and other standard-library imports to their established modules, not a colliding local folder. A colliding reserved name must produce an actionable diagnostic or require a deliberate explicit disambiguation scheme; never silently shadow the standard library.",
            "- Normalize `.`/`..`/separators for canonical comparisons while prohibiting import escape outside the selected root. Reject symlink loops, traversal and duplicate case-folded paths; behavior must not diverge unpredictably on Linux vs Windows.",
            "- Record the complete import chain in cycle diagnostics; reject cycles for the initial deliverable. Do not introduce a half-specified type-only cycle mechanism or hidden module initialization ordering.",
            "- Import environments are per source file even when a folder shares a declaration namespace; exports are resolved from their defining module regardless of which sibling source file imported another dependency.",
            "- Determine graph ordering deterministically and share source file hashes/IDs across check, native/C lowering, browser reachability and LSP. Incremental caching is desirable, but correct one-graph compilation is the gate.",
            "",
            "## 4. Visibility, names and diagnostics",
            "",
            "- Map `pub` on exported declarations to explicit module-qualified symbols; unmarked declarations are private to their module. Do not permit unqualified access to non-exported declarations from other folders.",
            "- Make exported struct fields/constructors and enum variants follow existing access rules consistently; an exported function whose argument or result uses a private nominal type is an error.",
            "- Preserve nominal origin, type inference, generics/specializations, error types, serialization and `server fn` boundaries when types cross modules.",
            "- Actionable errors for unknown module, unknown exported member, private access, ambiguous name, duplicate export, import cycle, invalid nested path, invalid manifest/root, and case/path collision must include source spans and suggestions.",
            "- Diagnostic output and editor source navigation must retain the actual paths of `.kiln`, `.glaze` and `.ember` files; avoid generated aliases masking origin files.",
            "",
            "## 5. Glazes, Embers and assets in folder modules",
            "",
            "- Inside a module, same-folder component lookup continues to work as before. A route defined inside `posts/` can render its own `index.glaze` and its local `editor.ember` without moving components into the root.",
            "- Resolve template/component references with a stable **owning module context** (and project-root-qualified file paths where already supported), not the caller's current working directory. A bare same-name component in a different module must not hijack a lookup.",
            "- If another module needs to reference a component directly, require an explicit, checked qualified path/module reference; prefer reusing established render/component syntax and do not add ambiguous global component-name search. If the parser needs a new qualified component spelling, document it and cover it by conformance before claiming support.",
            "- `.glaze` remains SSR-only; `.ember` is browser-capable with optional SSR and hydration, per Phase 4.1. Do not reintroduce implicit state-triggered Ember classification.",
            "- CSS Modules, native `ui`, Finishes, layouts, slots, generated styles, static assets and WasmGC island assets must use module-aware paths without collisions. Ensure only reachable islands load browser assets; a Glaze-only route stays script-free.",
            "- `server fn` calls, generated typed RPC, serialization, browser/JS/npm interop and forbidden browser imports keep existing trust boundaries across modules; no client bundle may include private server logic or secrets.",
            "",
            "## 6. Minimal `kiln.toml` (Phase 4.2)",
            "",
            "- Support the minimal optional `[package]` fields above and a clear documented schema/version policy. Parse proper TOML with location-aware invalid-key, invalid-type and missing-entry diagnostics.",
            "- Explicit CLI options override manifest defaults. Use a valid canonical path *within the root* for the entry; no relative traversal or entry pointing to an unrelated project.",
            "- No plaintext secrets, custom executable setup/install scripts, compulsory per-folder manifests or dependency-specific lockfile behavior.",
            "- A single `.kiln` script or simple folder works with *zero* manifest; a multi-folder app also works manifest-free where entry/root is unambiguous.",
            "- **Phase 6** may later add `[wares]`, package versions, publishing metadata, build/test configuration and `kiln.lock` without changing basic root/module semantics. If currently unsupported future sections are present, report them clearly rather than pretending they were resolved.",
            "",
            "## 7. CLI, LSP and implementation steps",
            "",
            "1. **Fail-first conformance:** Build fixtures for multi-folder positive cases, private/export errors, imports, cycles, case collisions, nested folders and unchanged one-file/one-folder behavior.",
            "2. **Compilation graph:** Introduce folder module identities and deterministic traversal/resolution in syntax/checker/IR/compiler driver. Verify native + C semantics and unchanged standard-library imports.",
            "3. **Project entry:** Implement CLI root/entry resolution and optional minimal TOML support, with well-defined precedences and diagnostics. Existing `kiln check`/`run`/`build` should accept local module graphs.",
            "4. **Web integration:** Exercise module-local Glazes, Embers, styles, static assets, nested components, RPC and browser reachability in a real multi-folder HTTP application. Retain security and SSR-only behavior.",
            "5. **Editor integration:** LSP/VS Code identify the project root; provide cross-module completion, hover, definition, symbols and actionable diagnostics for local modules. Broader rename/refactor/test UI remains Phase 6.",
            "6. **Docs/verification:** Update syntax and Glaze/Ember reference, design notes, VS Code guides, conformance, native Linux/Windows cross-build/smoke and browser E2E. Update current-feature claims only as code lands.",
            "",
            "## 8. Acceptance matrix",
            "",
            "| Dimension | Must work | Must fail safely |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| Compatibility | `kiln run hello.kiln`; old same-folder projects without TOML | Existing lookup/order silently changes |",
            "| Module discovery | `import posts` and nested `import posts.search` from multiple folders | Missing folder, path escape, cycles, case/alias conflicts |",
            "| Visibility/type identity | Public functions/types used by other modules | Private member access, leaked private types, accidental nominal equivalence |",
            "| Entrypoint | Root app with or without optional `kiln.toml` | Ambiguous `main`, malformed TOML, entry outside root |",
            "| Web/source assets | Module-local Glaze, Ember, CSS, static assets and RPC | Collision, broken paths, accidental private/server code in Wasm |",
            "| Builds | Linux/Windows native builds, C-backend regression and Wasm browser checks | Differences in import/case semantics by OS |",
            "| Editor | Import completion, cross-folder definition/symbols, source-linked diagnostics | Stale or wrong source paths |",
            "",
            "**Required dogfood:** Implement a small `fieldnotes/` app with separate `posts/` and `accounts/` folder modules; two route registrations; an SSR Glaze page; an Ember form that calls a typed `server fn`; module-local styling; and at least one route loading **no Ember JS/Wasm**. Include a manifest-free run and optional-manifest entry test. Data may remain in memory—Phase 5 adds durable persistence.",
            "",
            "## 9. Definition of done and exclusions",
            "",
            "- [x] One-file/one-folder programs regressions pass unchanged.",
            "- [x] Multi-folder modules compile, check, run and import nested modules with precise, deterministic errors and `pub` visibility.",
            "- [x] Optional `kiln.toml` can select an entrypoint but is unnecessary for local imports or simple scripts.",
            "- [x] Glazes, Embers, scoped CSS/assets, browser target, server RPC and component source maps work across module boundaries.",
            "- [x] Project-aware LSP, cross-folder definitions/completion/diagnostics and CLI paths work.",
            "- [x] Multi-folder HTTP + SSR/Ember E2E dogfood and Linux/Windows native/C/wasm conformance pass.",
            "- [x] Docs and roadmap correctly describe Phase 4.2 vs Phase 5/6 ownership.",
            "",
            "**Explicit exclusions:** SQL/ORM/auth/admin (Phase 5); Wares/registry/lockfile/cache, fully developed manifest dependency schema, official component Ware, public `kiln test`, formatter/live rebuild, advanced refactoring and compiler MCP (Phase 6); deploy/SSG (Phase 7).",
            "",
            "**Exit gate:** A developer can organize and run a small full-stack Kiln app into sensible folders *before* adding a database, without configuring a dependency manager. Phase 5 must build on this rather than inventing a competing module/project system.",
            "",
            "## Verification",
            "",
            "- `conformance/modules`: public APIs, private helpers, distinct nominal types,",
            "  nested imports, generics, methods and enums on native Linux, C, Wasm and",
            "  Windows (Wine smoke execution).",
            "- `crates/kiln_cli/tests/modules.rs`: negative visibility and inferred-type",
            "  cases, method access, cycles/case/reserved names, lexical imports, manifest",
            "  precedence/validation, source maps, reachability and optional-manifest",
            "  Fieldnotes builds for C/Linux/Windows.",
            "- `crates/kiln_cli/tests/fieldnotes` and `tests/browser/fieldnotes.mjs`: real",
            "  Chromium SSR, hydration, typed RPC success/validation, qualified components,",
            "  module-local CSS/static files and a script-free route, on native and C.",
            "- LSP cross-folder definition/hover/completion tests, the existing editor suite,",
            "  qualified-tag grammar tests, repository tests, formatting and Clippy.",
            "",
            "Windows execution here is a Wine smoke test; native Windows CI remains the",
            "platform-specific gate. Phase 5 data/auth/admin and Phase 6 dependency/testing",
            "features remain outside this implementation.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/phase-5", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Phase 5 — Data, models, forms, authentication and admin",
            "",
            "**Status:** Planned; design and implementation contract, **not documentation of currently implemented APIs**. Phase 3 supplies compiled Glazes/styling; Phase 4 supplies WasmGC Embers and typed RPC; Phase 4.1 formalizes explicit `.ember` files and **Phase 4.2 supplies multi-folder projects before Phase 5**. The `sql`/`auth` standard-library files are checkable placeholders; `Request.form`, `App.mount`, `orm`, `form`, `validate`, `session` and `admin` still need implementation.",
            "",
            "**Goal:** A developer can use **an existing SQL database or a fresh SQLite file** with typed Kiln models, reviewed migrations, validated forms, secure identity and a customizable SSR admin. **Integrate Phase 4 Embers and RPC** with that same persistence/auth/validation model, rather than creating separate client and server APIs.",
            "",
            "**End-to-end completion gate:** Build and run a realistic **multi-folder** database-backed application that organizes routes, models and authentication into Phase 4.2 local modules. It must include working sign-in, sessions, role/record permissions, validation, migrations, generated CRUD admin, custom Glazes and an **authenticated Ember invoking an ORM-backed `server fn` through Phase 4 RPC**. It must work as a native standalone Kiln executable on supported Linux and Windows. SQLite needs no separate database service; PostgreSQL and MySQL use external database servers. Both C and native compiler backends remain tested where supported.",
            "",
            "This plan refines the existing [design overview](../design.md#orm), [product vision](../vision.md#data-forms-and-authentication), [language rules](../reference/types.md), [standard library placeholders](https://github.com/IZ737/kiln/blob/main/crates/kiln_check/std) and [Phase 3 Glaze plan](phase-3.md), following the [Phase 4 Ember/RPC plan](phase-4.md), [Phase 4.1 explicit Ember files](phase-4.1.md) and [Phase 4.2 local modules](phase-4.2.md). The [roadmap](../../README.md#roadmap) remains the source of phase sequencing.",
            "",
            "**Reading rule:** All snippets below are **target API illustrations**, not executable examples of today's Kiln. Exact signatures and compiler lowering may change during implementation; public documentation only claims an API after code, tests and conformance land.",
            "",
            "## 1. Non-negotiable design decisions",
            "",
            "1. **Ordinary Kiln structs remain models.** No separate model-definition language, schema file, compulsory decorators or required base classes. Inference works by convention; explicit mappings solve exceptions.",
            "2. **SQLite first, but not SQLite only.** Ship SQLite end to end before expanding the same user-facing abstraction to PostgreSQL and MySQL in Phase 5. Never silently pretend a dialect-specific feature works everywhere.",
            "3. **Two complementary database APIs.** Explicit, parameterized `sql` is always available; `orm` supplies a genuinely typed, compiler-understood query surface. Users may mix both in one transaction.",
            "4. **No surprising queries.** Relationships are typed, but fetching a row does not accidentally issue N extra requests. Loading relations is explicit; queries, limits and projections remain inspectable.",
            "5. **No runtime reflection dependency.** The compiler derives schemas, encoders, decoders, query plans and admin descriptions from checked program types; the runtime handles data, IO and crypto. No general reflection engine or magic interpreter.",
            "6. **Import is a first-class adoption path.** `kiln db pull` examines an existing database and creates editable models/mapping metadata. Re-import detects drift and proposes a reviewable update, never overwrites user code silently.",
            "7. **Migrations are reviewable artifacts.** `kiln db diff` proposes changes; `kiln db migrate` applies explicit versions with checksums and locking. Neither starting a production server nor deploying an executable runs destructive DDL automatically.",
            "8. **One type can inform the full data path.** Share field types and constraints between database persistence, form decoding, validation, admin input metadata and Glaze error display; allow separate input structs when an API should not expose every database field.",
            "9. **Authorization belongs on the server.** Hiding a button never grants security. Admin routes, reads, writes, actions and object selection enforce policies before performing work.",
            "10. **Secure defaults beat easy-to-misconfigure convenience.** Parameterized SQL, bounded input, signed opaque session IDs, protected cookies, Argon2id, CSRF protection, constant-time comparison where relevant, safe redirects, and explicit production secrets.",
            "11. **Use maintained native implementations.** Rust libraries/drivers in the runtime are pragmatic starting points, not immovable architecture. Keep drivers, password hashing and session stores replaceable without changing Kiln application APIs.",
            "12. **Respect phase boundaries.** Phase 5 builds on **Phase 4/4.1 Embers/WasmGC, typed RPC and Phase 4.2 folder modules with an optional minimal `kiln.toml`**. Preserve SSR forms and web routes. Do not reimplement module resolution or require Wares, a registry, dependency `kiln.lock` or public `kiln test`. Reuse the browser/server transport and extend request identity/policy hooks.",
            "",
            "## 2. Intended developer experience",
            "",
            "### A. A model is a struct",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import sql, orm, web, auth, admin",
            "",
            "struct Post {",
            "    id: Int",
            "    title: String",
            "    body: String",
            "    published: Bool = false",
            "    author_id: Int",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn main() ! {",
            "    let db = try sql.open(env(\"DATABASE_URL\") ?? \"sqlite:./data/app.db\")",
            "    let app = web.app()",
            "",
            "    app.get(\"/posts\", fn(req) {",
            "        let posts = try orm.find<Post>(db)",
            "            .where(.published)",
            "            .order_by(.id)",
            "            .limit(20)",
            "            .all()",
            "        web.render(\"posts.glaze\", { posts })",
            "    })",
            "",
            "    // An explicit permission policy is required before mounting admin.",
            "    let guard = auth.admin_policy(db)",
            "    app.mount(\"/admin\", admin.site(db, models: [Post], authorize: guard))",
            "    try app.listen(\"127.0.0.1:8080\")",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "`Post` as a model type, `.where(.published)` query lowering, `App.mount` and `admin_policy` are **target syntax/API**, not current features. The single type-list form `models: [Post]` is the intended convenient compiler-recognized admin syntax from the existing design; it is **not** a claim that types are ordinary runtime values. If compiler implementation benefits from typed descriptors such as `orm.model<Post>()`, those may be the underlying public form; preserve the one-line default experience.",
            "",
            "### B. A user-submitted form",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "import web, sql, orm, form, validate, auth",
            "",
            "struct PostInput {",
            "    title: String",
            "    body: String",
            "    published: Bool = false",
            "}",
            "",
            "fn create_post(req: web.Request, db: sql.Db) -> web.Response ! {",
            "    let actor = try auth.require(req)",
            "    try auth.authorize(actor, \"post:create\")",
            "",
            "    // Parsing produces typed data or a structured bad-input result.",
            "    let input = try req.form<PostInput>()",
            "    let checked = validate.check(input, {",
            "        title: [validate.required(), validate.length(min: 3, max: 120)],",
            "        body: [validate.required()]",
            "    })",
            "    if not checked.valid {",
            "        return web.render(\"posts/new.glaze\", {",
            "            values: input, errors: checked.errors",
            "        }, status: 422)",
            "    }",
            "    let post = try orm.insert<Post>(db, {",
            "        title: input.title,",
            "        body: input.body,",
            "        published: input.published,",
            "        author_id: actor.id",
            "    })",
            "    web.redirect(\"/posts/\${post.id}\", status: 303)",
            "}",
            "```",
            "",
            "Illustrative only: exact validation descriptors, checked-result shape, render-status argument, auth signatures and insert syntax are to be settled before those milestones. Form input is **not assumed trusted because it decoded successfully**.",
            "",
            "### C. Starting with somebody else's database",
            "",
            "```text",
            "DATABASE_URL=sqlite:./legacy.db kiln db pull --out models.kiln",
            "kiln check .",
            "DATABASE_URL=sqlite:./legacy.db kiln db status",
            "DATABASE_URL=sqlite:./legacy.db kiln db diff --name add_post_summary",
            "# Review the generated dialect-specific SQL and any warnings.",
            "DATABASE_URL=sqlite:./legacy.db kiln db migrate",
            "```",
            "",
            "No mandatory manifest. Credentials may come from environment variables or a CLI argument, but tools redact them in logging and diagnostics.",
            "",
            "## 3. Ownership, layers and boundaries",
            "",
            "| Layer | Responsibilities | Must not do |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| Parser/type checker | Schema derivation from structs, field/type validation, query-expression analysis, form/validation typing, generated-Glaze diagnostics | Open databases during ordinary `kiln check`; execute user SQL while compiling |",
            "| IR/codegen | Typed row codecs, static query descriptors, parameter binding, model metadata, admin route/Glaze generation | Emit ad-hoc SQL through interpolated user strings |",
            "| `kiln_rt` / drivers | Database IO, pooling, rows, transactions, encryption/hash verification, cookies/session persistence | Interpret arbitrary user-defined type declarations at runtime |",
            "| CLI | Introspection, migration generation/status/apply, drift reporting, safety prompts | Auto-apply unreviewed DDL during `build`, `run` or deployment |",
            "| Kiln stdlib | Composable typed `sql`, `orm`, `form`, `validate`, `session`, `auth`, `admin` APIs | Require users to adopt the ORM/admin for simple SQL or HTTP apps |",
            "",
            "The runtime should link or initialize only the database/auth functionality a program reaches, compatible with later standard-library dead-code elimination and replacement of implementation libraries. Keep driver-specific detail behind a stable error and capability surface.",
            "",
            "**Current source compatibility:** `crates/kiln_check/std/sql.kiln` already sketches `sql.open`, `Db.query<T>`, `one<T>`, `scalar<T>`, `exec` and `insert<T>`; `auth.kiln` sketches `User`, `current`, `require`, `hash_password`, `verify_password`; `web.kiln` sketches `Request.form<T>`. Evolve these deliberately. Breaking changes are acceptable before 1.0 but need changelog entries and conformance updates.",
            "",
            "## 4. SQL: driver and connection foundation",
            "",
            "### Connection schemes and support",
            "",
            "| Database | Target DSN | Initial profile |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| SQLite | `sqlite:./data/app.db`, `sqlite::memory:` | No external service, file and memory, transactions, optional WAL/busy timeout |",
            "| PostgreSQL | `postgres://user:pass@host:5432/app` | TLS-aware connections, bounded pool, transactions and prepared parameters |",
            "| MySQL | `mysql://user:pass@host:3306/app` | TLS-aware connections, bounded pool, transactions and prepared parameters |",
            "",
            "URLs are examples of the proposed contract; credential encoding and URL parser behavior must be specified and tested. Avoid logging plaintext DSNs. Explicit TLS validation is mandatory in production remote-DB configurations; never silently weaken certificate verification.",
            "",
            "### Minimum `sql` API",
            "",
            "- `sql.open(url, options: ...)` returns a reusable `Db` handle, not an open socket per query.",
            "- `db.query<T>(sql, args...)` decodes a list of typed rows; `db.one<T>` returns `T?`; `db.scalar<T>` decodes exactly one scalar or reports a deliberate cardinality error; `db.exec` returns affected-row count where the driver can supply it.",
            "- Use **positional `\$1`, `\$2`, ...** in Kiln source across all backends, with safe dialect translation/binding under the hood. Placeholders are never replaced using string concatenation. Identifier/table names cannot be bound values.",
            "- Literal SQL with known row types gets compile-time compatibility checks when a pinned local schema snapshot is available. Otherwise validate typed decoding at runtime with useful column/type diagnostics. Never require a live database during compilation.",
            "- Expose explicit `DbError` classes: connection/timeout, syntax, constraint (unique/FK/not-null/check), decode, transaction conflict, unsupported dialect/operation, cancellation and migration drift. Preserve safe database error metadata, redact secrets and avoid emitting SQL parameter values by default.",
            "- Support nullable columns ↔ Kiln optionals, integer bounds, `String`, `Bool`, `Float`, `Date`/time forms where representable, and typed row records. Preserve unsupported binary/decimal/UUID/JSON-native types losslessly or report unsupported mapping; **never silently round money/decimal values through Float**. If a dedicated `Decimal` or `Bytes` type is introduced, require both backends, documentation and conformance.",
            "- Disallow automatic implicit conversions that could truncate identifiers, dates or numbers; use explicit conversion helpers.",
            "",
            "### Transactions and concurrency",
            "",
            "Target shape:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "try db.transaction(fn(tx) ! {",
            "    let account = try tx.one<Account>(\"select * from accounts where id = \$1\", id)",
            "    try tx.exec(\"update accounts set balance = balance - \$1 where id = \$2\", amount, id)",
            "    try tx.exec(\"insert into transfers (account_id, amount) values (\$1, \$2)\", id, amount)",
            "})",
            "```",
            "",
            "- Closure receives a transaction-scoped handle supporting both `sql` and `orm`.",
            "- Commit on success; roll back on error, panic/cancellation or dropped scope; no silent partial commit.",
            "- Connection acquisition and database operations must not block Kiln's HTTP task scheduler indefinitely. Use bounded pools/driver IO workers appropriately.",
            "- SQLite: document one-writer behavior, locking, busy timeout and WAL; never claim server DB concurrency is identical. Postgres/MySQL: sensible pool bounds and idle/connection timeouts. Instrument query latency/errors without logging sensitive parameters.",
            "- First pass may restrict nested transactions; explicit savepoint support is an extension, not a hidden magic nesting model.",
            "",
            "## 5. ORM: schema metadata and typed queries",
            "",
            "### Model conventions",
            "",
            "- `Post` → `posts`; `author_id` → `author_id`; `id: Int` is the default primary key.",
            "- Field defaults are application-level defaults unless a DB default is explicitly represented. They are **not** automatically equivalent to SQL `DEFAULT`.",
            "- `String?` ↔ nullable column; required fields stay non-null unless import reveals a mismatch.",
            "- Define deterministic casing, pluralization, reserved-word quoting and collision rules. Names that cannot be inferred unambiguously require explicit mapping.",
            "- Explicit per-model mapping can override table/column names, key (including non-`Int` keys), uniqueness, indexed fields, server-generated defaults, relation foreign keys and read-only/imported columns.",
            "- Mapping metadata is checked and represented by compiler-recognized typed descriptors, not a second mapping DSL or runtime-only string dictionary.",
            "- Relationships can be expressed as typed `User` / `List<Comment>` model relationships, per the design vision, backed by explicit mapped foreign keys. Relation navigation **does not trigger transparent lazy IO**: access is valid only after an explicit preload/join, or through an explicit relation query. The compiler should diagnose unclear unloaded relation access where possible.",
            "- Generated/imported structs are editable Kiln source. Persistence metadata and relation declarations must remain separately inspectable.",
            "",
            "### Typed queries",
            "",
            "Target experience:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "let posts = try orm.find<Post>(db)",
            "    .where(.published)",
            "    .where(fn(p) { p.author_id == author_id })",
            "    .order_by(.id)",
            "    .limit(20)",
            "    .all()",
            "let one = try orm.find<Post>(db).where(fn(p) { p.id == id }).first()",
            "```",
            "",
            "- `where`, comparisons, `and`/`or`/`not`, `order_by`, `limit`, `offset`, explicit `count`, `first`, `all`, and primary-key `get` are the first query operators. Pagination must be bounded on admin routes.",
            "- Typed field-shorthand syntax (e.g. `.published`) may reuse existing closure shorthand; query expressions are a **restricted compiler-recognized subset**, not arbitrary closures executed on every fetched row. Unsupported expressions produce a compile diagnostic.",
            "- Captured runtime values become bound SQL parameters, not injected SQL syntax. Query values have checked field types. Misspellings and nonsensical comparisons fail during compilation.",
            "- Explicit projection to typed records and join/preload plans follow once single-table query and CRUD semantics are sound; required before completing the full ORM milestone.",
            "- Relationships must avoid N+1 queries by providing explicit batched `include`/preload with deterministic limits. Never issue hidden work during field access.",
            "- Explain/inspect generated SQL in a development mode so users can investigate performance and correctness.",
            "- Direct raw SQL and ORM calls can share `Db` and transaction handles without conversions.",
            "",
            "### Mutation",
            "",
            "- `insert`, `update`, `delete`, `upsert` (where supported) and atomic conditional updates.",
            "- Specify generated-key retrieval by dialect; never assume `RETURNING` works everywhere.",
            "- Mutating models requires explicit writable columns; protect generated/readonly keys and guard optimistic conflict/version fields when configured.",
            "- ORM does **not** automatically authorize a caller; that remains the auth/admin/application layer.",
            "- No implicit cascade delete in application code. Database-declared cascades must be discoverable and shown in diff/admin deletion confirmation.",
            "",
            "## 6. Database-first schema import: `kiln db pull`",
            "",
            "This is a central adoption feature, not an afterthought.",
            "",
            "1. Connect and inspect tables, views, columns, nullability, primary/foreign keys, unique constraints, indexes, server defaults and dialect-specific enum/check information.",
            "2. Produce ordinary editable `.kiln` model declarations and a **deterministic sidecar schema snapshot/mapping** sufficient to preserve names and types the Kiln structs alone cannot represent. Include source dialect and a sanitized schema fingerprint, **not credentials**.",
            "3. Identify relationships, including join tables, without inventing a false `belongs_to` for ambiguous keys; report manual resolution requirements.",
            "4. Generate valid names for otherwise invalid/reserved Kiln identifiers while recording the original SQL identifier; show collisions clearly.",
            "5. On a second pull, diff the remote schema, last-known snapshot and edited source; propose changes and highlight conflicts. **Do not overwrite edited code silently**.",
            "6. Views and tables without a supported primary key remain queryable/read-only where practical. Unsupported constraints, triggers, generated columns, custom types, partitions, stored routines and exotic indexes are reported rather than dropped from the developer's mental model.",
            "7. No schema mutation occurs during pull. A successful pull cannot break the source database.",
            "",
            "Proposed CLI:",
            "",
            "```text",
            "kiln db pull [--url URL] [--out models.kiln] [--schema NAME]",
            "kiln db pull --check          # non-mutating drift / re-import report",
            "```",
            "",
            "Environment `DATABASE_URL` is the default. Missing parameters should yield actionable diagnostics, not prompts in CI.",
            "",
            "## 7. Migrations: `kiln db diff` and `kiln db migrate`",
            "",
            "### Workflow",
            "",
            "1. Compiler derives desired schema from selected model types/mapping metadata, and CLI reads the prior **versioned snapshot** (not an arbitrary guessed production baseline).",
            "2. `kiln db diff --name ...` emits a proposed, dialect-specific SQL migration plus metadata: stable revision ID, parent revision, checksum, dependencies, affected objects and warnings for destructive operations.",
            "3. Developer reviews SQL and any data backfill instructions and commits migrations in source control.",
            "4. `kiln db migrate` checks migration ledger/checksums, takes a per-database migration lock, verifies expected ancestry, then applies outstanding versions in order.",
            "5. `kiln db status` reports applied/pending versions and drift; `kiln db verify` checks snapshots and migration checksums; `--dry-run` prints operations without changing the database.",
            "6. If the live schema diverges from the expected migration state, **stop** and require reconciliation. Never infer that an undocumented production change is safe to overwrite.",
            "",
            "Use paths such as `migrations/sqlite/`, `migrations/postgres/` and `migrations/mysql/` for distinct DDL dialects, within a normal app folder. A single model may describe portable features, but migrations are generated/reviewed per target database.",
            "",
            "### Destructive changes and dialect realities",
            "",
            "- Renames are ambiguous: require an explicit rename decision rather than silently implementing as DROP+ADD. Warn and require an interactive confirmation/explicit CI flag for drop, type narrowing, nullability tightening and cascade changes.",
            "- Include backups/data-loss guidance for destructive operations. SQL down migrations may be authored where safe, but **automatic rollback of arbitrary production DDL is not promised**.",
            "- SQLite table rebuilds need special review, transactional safeguards and foreign-key checks.",
            "- Postgres transactional DDL is used where available; MySQL DDL often auto-commits; compensate with explicit state/recovery instructions rather than pretending transactions protect every migration.",
            "- Migrations do not run automatically on server start, deployment, build or schema import.",
            "",
            "## 8. Forms and validation",
            "",
            "### Parsing and binding",
            "",
            "- Implement `req.form<T>()` for `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` first, then multipart form data for bounded text fields and explicitly configured uploads. Malformed encoding, unexpected content type, duplicate scalar names, excessive fields/bytes and type-conversion errors have clear structured outcomes.",
            "- Distinguish **decode failure** (invalid transport/input shape, generally 400) from **validation failure** (well-formed fields that violate app rules, generally 422). Users may re-render a Glaze with field errors and preserved safe input.",
            "- Typed structs provide field names, optionals, booleans, basic input kinds and defaults. Passwords, permission fields and generated IDs must not automatically become user-writable just because they appear on a persisted model.",
            "- `validate` exposes type-checked rules (required, length, bounds, format, choices, matches/cross-field, and custom ordinary Kiln predicates) and structured errors keyed by typed field. Model uniqueness checks may require DB access; race-free uniqueness remains a database constraint.",
            "- Validate on the server on **every write**. Client HTML constraints and existing Phase 4 Embers are conveniences, never trusted security checks; derive interactive field feedback from the same server-side rules where appropriate.",
            "- For SSR, keep form rendering in Glazes and reusable components; provide field-value/error helpers and generated admin field components, not an entirely new template language.",
            "- File uploads: safe names, content-type skepticism, bounded size/count, temporary-file cleanup, no automatic public serving and explicit destination policy. Streaming uploads and external storage integration can be later enhancements if the first safe bounded path suffices.",
            "",
            "### Form security",
            "",
            "- State-changing cookie-authenticated requests require CSRF verification; generated admin/forms include tokens automatically, custom form helpers expose tokens clearly.",
            "- No CSRF exemptions for JSON just because a request says it is JSON; base policy on authentication mechanism and actual cross-origin attack surface.",
            "- Escape reflected field values and errors in Glazes; avoid reflecting raw submitted HTML, headers or SQL errors into trusted markup.",
            "- Form-bound types should support opt-in allowlists to prevent mass assignment, not blanket `save(req.form<Model>())`.",
            "",
            "## 9. Sessions, identity and authorization",
            "",
            "### API boundary",
            "",
            "- `session`: create, rotate, read, invalidate and expire opaque sessions; server-side storage supports DB-backed persistence, with in-memory storage clearly marked local/dev-only.",
            "- `auth`: password hashing/verification, signed-in identity, login/logout, current/require, permission enforcement, auth middleware/hooks and provider adapters.",
            "- The existing no-argument `auth.current()` / `auth.require()` stubs may be redesigned as **request-explicit** `auth.current(req)` / `auth.require(req)` (or an equally explicit checked request context). Avoid ambient global identity when multiple HTTP tasks run concurrently.",
            "- Application `User` ORM models remain owned by the application; `auth.Identity` or an adapter maps IDs/roles/claims to user records. Never impose a single fixed `auth.User` database schema on pre-existing systems.",
            "- Use ordinary Kiln functions for authorization policies: route/action/model/record checks. Offer `auth.require_role`/permission helpers as conveniences, not a fixed RBAC-only model.",
            "- Failed permissions yield 403 (or deliberately 404 for sensitive object existence), missing login normally 401/redirect as selected by the route. No leakage of protected fields in serialized results.",
            "",
            "### Secure defaults",
            "",
            "- Passwords: maintained **Argon2id** implementation with calibrated parameters and upgrade-on-login policy. Use cryptographically secure random values; constant-time verification APIs; generic login failures; throttle attempts and make account enumeration difficult.",
            "- Sessions: **opaque, unpredictable identifiers** stored server side (or hashed identifiers in DB), rotated at login/privilege changes, invalidated on logout, bounded by idle and absolute expiration. Prevent session fixation and stale privilege elevation.",
            "- Cookies: `HttpOnly`, appropriate `Secure` in HTTPS environments, `SameSite=Lax` default unless a deliberate cross-site flow requires otherwise, explicit `Path` and prefix constraints, no secrets in URLs. Account for trustworthy reverse-proxy TLS configuration; never infer HTTPS from spoofable untrusted forwarded headers.",
            "- CSRF: generated forms/admin include tokens; verify before mutations, including logout and privileged account changes. Enforce appropriate Origin/Referer checks as defense in depth.",
            "- Authorization: deny by default for admin. Distinguish read/list/create/update/delete and optional per-object policy. Check again at mutation time to avoid TOCTOU where applicable.",
            "- Redirects: do not accept untrusted external `next` URLs without an allowlist. Login/logout endpoints should not enable open redirects.",
            "- Secrets/keys: environment or explicit secret source, secure defaults in local development, no hardcoded shared production secret. If signing keys are used for tokens, support versioned rotation; never store passwords in reversible form.",
            "- Security logs: record authentication and privileged changes without exposing passwords, tokens, cookies, database credentials or personal form contents.",
            "",
            "### Integrations",
            "",
            "Local/password login with session-backed SSR and authenticated Ember RPC is required for the Phase 5 completion gate. Design a provider boundary for OIDC/OAuth 2 and API tokens/JWT, but **do not require every identity protocol** to ship before the integrated ORM/admin experience. External-provider features must get their own threat model and protocol conformance before being called supported. Avoid homegrown crypto or identity protocols.",
            "",
            "## 10. Generated, customizable admin",
            "",
            "Target convention from the established design:",
            "",
            "```kiln",
            "app.mount(\"/admin\", admin.site(db, models: [Post, User, Comment], authorize: policy))",
            "```",
            "",
            "### Required default UX",
            "",
            "- SSR list/search/filter/sort/pagination and create/detail/edit/delete pages for supported writable models.",
            "- Readable relation selectors, input widgets generated from typed fields and declared validation rules, useful empty/loading/error states (server-side), and accessible keyboard/focus/labels.",
            "- Clearly show destructive confirmation, validation errors, uniqueness/FK constraint failures and optimistic conflicts.",
            "- Allow admin operators to inspect safe audit events and understand errors without seeing sensitive SQL/stack traces.",
            "- No user-supplied runtime template interpreter: admin is generated/compiled with the same Glaze and styling pipeline, with safe value escaping.",
            "",
            "### Customization in layers",
            "",
            "1. **Model registration:** title/label, columns, search fields, filters, sort, pagination, editable/read-only fields, validation and per-action visibility.",
            "2. **Field components:** override input/display widget with typed Glaze components and validation metadata.",
            "3. **Page overrides:** use app-owned Glazes at conventional paths such as `admin/post/edit.glaze`; checked props and source maps apply exactly as to ordinary Glazes.",
            "4. **Custom actions:** named, typed server functions with explicit permissions, CSRF, idempotence/destructive confirmation policies.",
            "5. **Theming:** Phase 3 CSS/Ui/Finishes; no mandatory third-party component Ware (the flagship `kiln/ui` Ware belongs to the Phase 6 ecosystem).",
            "",
            "### Permission and safety rules",
            "",
            "- `admin.site` **requires an explicit policy**; in dev, an opt-in safe local-only bootstrap flow may create the initial privileged account, but production refuses an unauthenticated mount.",
            "- Authorization applies to list queries and individual reads, then again to writes/actions; filter query results by accessible scope rather than merely hiding inaccessible rows in templates.",
            "- Password hashes, reset tokens, session IDs, secrets, internal audit metadata, server-only config and generated keys are omitted/read-only by default.",
            "- Use POST for destructive actions, validate CSRF, avoid GET mutations, use safe redirects and anti-clickjacking/security headers.",
            "- Admin handles large tables with bounded server-side pagination, indexed search warnings and no unbounded `select all` by default.",
            "",
            "## 11. Compiler, diagnostics and LSP",
            "",
            "- Derive row codecs, model metadata, query expression lowering, form decoders and admin descriptors at compile time. Do not add a new generated source language or a compulsory reflection runtime.",
            "- Typed query errors point to **Kiln source spans** for unknown field, wrong comparison type, unsupported expression, unknown relation, dialect-only operator or unsafe SQL identifier use.",
            "- Add actionable error codes for unknown model/table mapping, invalid relation/key, incompatible form field/rule, unavailable driver, migration mismatch and admin authorization misconfiguration. Document codes in the diagnostics reference as implemented.",
            "- LSP supports completion/hover/definition for ORM model fields and query shorthands, validation rules, mapped columns, generated form/admin props and admin Glaze override files; diagnostics should work without a live DB.",
            "- Avoid cross-compiler/backend divergence: derived codecs and queries should flow from shared typed IR; no handwritten parallel semantics in C vs native codegen.",
            "",
            "## 12. Reliability, operations and performance contracts",
            "",
            "- **Bounded resource use:** connection pools, request body size, upload size, statement timeout, result/pagination caps and sensible defaults. Expose tuning only when needed.",
            "- **Cancellation:** handler cancellation and timeouts must release DB resources; transactional scopes must not leak locks.",
            "- **Storage:** SQLite files must have explicit path semantics, backup and WAL/checkpoint guidance. Running a binary does not recreate/delete an existing DB unexpectedly.",
            "- **Observability:** stable SQL/query error classification, timings, pool metrics and safe debug SQL preview; redact secrets and bind values unless developer explicitly opts in within a safe environment.",
            "- **Portability:** native Linux/Windows builds, cross-platform path/encoding tests and representative C backend cases. A standalone program bundles client libraries where possible; remote database servers remain external.",
            "- **Performance:** benchmarks include query throughput/latency, row decoding, ORM query generation, transactions, rendered admin pages and app binary size. Record baseline measurements; optimize after semantics and correctness are established.",
            "- **Replaceability:** treat chosen database drivers, session stores and crypto crates as replaceable private runtime implementations while retaining conformance suites as behavioural contracts.",
            "",
            "## 13. PR-sized implementation steps and review gates",
            "",
            "Each step must include tests and docs; do not reimplement Phase 4 Ember reactivity, Phase 4.1 file kinds or Phase 4.2 module resolution, or expand into Phase 6 ecosystem tooling. Reuse Ember RPC for integration coverage.",
            "",
            "### Step 1 — SQLite SQL foundation",
            "",
            "- Implement `sql.open`, typed `query/one/scalar/exec`, placeholders, row encoders/decoders, optionals, error categories, file/memory databases, pool/connection lifecycle.",
            "- Add `db.transaction` with commit/rollback, timeouts, constraints and cancellation cleanup.",
            "- **Gate:** a real SQLite-backed Kiln app performs safe CRUD and transactions; edge cases and failure paths pass on native, C and supported Windows target.",
            "",
            "### Step 2 — Struct-based ORM + typed query lowering",
            "",
            "- Schema/model metadata from structs, conventions and explicit overrides; generated row codecs.",
            "- Typed filtering/order/limits/count/get/CRUD, constrained query expression lowering, parameterized captures, explicit relations/preloading and readable SQL inspection.",
            "- **Gate:** a related multi-table model round-trips, invalid field/type queries fail during `kiln check`, no hidden N+1 and raw SQL interop works inside a transaction.",
            "",
            "### Step 3 — Schema import and migration tooling (SQLite)",
            "",
            "- `db pull` introspection/import with deterministic snapshots and drift reports.",
            "- `db diff/status/verify/migrate`, reviewed SQL migrations, checksum ledger, destructive-operation safeguards, SQLite-specific rebuild checks.",
            "- **Gate:** import a pre-existing SQLite database, preserve its data, generate/edit models, make an additive migration, and detect a manual schema drift or migration checksum modification without overwriting anything.",
            "",
            "### Step 4 — PostgreSQL and MySQL parity",
            "",
            "- Reuse SQL/ORM public APIs with maintained Rust database drivers; TLS, pooling, dialect-aware bindings, transaction and DDL behavior.",
            "- Add schema import/migration support for both dialects and explicit feature/capability diagnostics.",
            "- **Gate:** same portable application logic performs CRUD, relations and forms-independent transactions on SQLite, Postgres and MySQL; each dialect has a tested pull→diff→migrate path in CI/service tests. Fail clearly for unsupported portable mappings.",
            "",
            "### Step 5 — Forms, typed binding and validation",
            "",
            "- `req.form<T>()`, safe URL-encoded parsing, bounded multipart uploads, model/input distinctions, typed validation rules and structured errors.",
            "- Glaze helpers/components for values, field errors, CSRF-ready forms and constraints; preserve accessibility and safe escaping.",
            "- Bind existing Phase 4 Ember input/state and typed RPC contracts to these **same validation results**, avoiding duplicate client and server field schemas.",
            "- **Gate:** successful and invalid inputs re-render correctly; malformed/hostile payloads are bounded and rejected, not panics or unsafe HTML/SQL.",
            "",
            "### Step 6 — Sessions, local authentication and policies",
            "",
            "- DB-persisted sessions, cookies, expiry/rotation/logout, Argon2id, verified login/logout flows, CSRF and permission policies with request-explicit identity.",
            "- Secure configuration, rate limiting/abuse controls, safe auth logs and explicit bootstrap.",
            "- Add request-bound identity to Phase 4 generated RPC handlers; test authenticated and per-record server-function access even when the browser bypasses UI controls.",
            "- **Gate:** unauthenticated/unauthorized users cannot retrieve or mutate protected data; login survives server restart; logout and rotation invalidate old session IDs; CSRF/session fixation/redirect regression cases pass.",
            "",
            "### Step 7 — Generated Glaze admin and overrides",
            "",
            "- Compile SSR CRUD screens from typed models, auto fields/search/filter/pagination, validation, auth/CSRF, audit-safe errors.",
            "- Typed model options, field/page Glaze overrides, custom actions and Phase 3 theme integration. SSR remains the admin default, with optional reactive enhancements through existing Phase 4 Embers.",
            "- **Gate:** mount a secure usable multi-model admin in one app without writing CRUD pages, then replace one field widget and one whole page using ordinary Glazes.",
            "",
            "### Step 8 — Integrated dogfooding, docs and cross-target gates",
            "",
            "- Build a substantial **multi-folder, database-backed Kiln application** with route/model/auth modules from Phase 4.2, users, posts, comments, foreign keys, public read routes, edit forms, login, per-record authorization, schema import, committed migrations and custom admin.",
            "- Include an authenticated Ember form calling an ORM-backed `server fn` over the Phase 4 RPC transport, with shared type-checked validation and hostile request regressions.",
            "- Dogfood both a new SQLite app and a representative pre-existing imported database; exercise Postgres/MySQL profiles.",
            "- Expand conformance/CI and LSP smoke tests, audit docs/API consistency, inspect native self-contained deployment, measure baseline performance and binary size.",
            "- **Gate:** the full definition of done below passes. Mark Phase 5 complete in README **only after** this gate.",
            "",
            "## 14. Conformance and test matrix",
            "",
            "| Domain | Positive cases | Negative/edge cases |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| SQL codecs | Scalars, records, optional NULL, dates, long text, Unicode | Overflow, mismatched column count/type, unsupported binary/decimal mapping, malformed UTF-8 |",
            "| SQL operations | Bind params, affected counts, insert keys, query ordering | Injection attempts, duplicate column aliases, syntax errors, uniqueness/FK violations |",
            "| Transactions | Commit, explicit rollback, nested restriction, error propagation | Failure mid-write, server task cancellation, busy lock, dropped handle |",
            "| ORM | Default/overridden model names, joins/preloads, filters, pagination | Unknown field, invalid comparison, ambiguous relation, N+1 regression, type mismatch |",
            "| DB import | Existing SQLite/Postgres/MySQL with keys/indexes/relations | Names colliding with Kiln keywords, views/composite keys, unsupported types, repeat pull after edits |",
            "| Migrations | Fresh/apply/pending/verify, additive change, data preserved | Checksums changed, conflicting drift, destructive prompt, concurrent migration, failed partially committed DDL |",
            "| Forms | URL encoding, UTF-8, optional/checkbox, field errors, multipart | Oversize uploads, duplicate scalars, invalid encodings, mass assignment, error HTML injection |",
            "| Auth/session | Login, logout, expiry, rotation, role and record policy | CSRF, fixation, forged/expired cookies, open redirect, brute force, unauthenticated admin |",
            "| Admin | CRUD, field/page overrides, filters, safe pagination | Inaccessible record, forged action, IDOR, readonly mutation, XSS via database values |",
            "| Cross-target | C/native, Linux/Windows app builds; all supported DB dialects | Driver missing, unsupported dialect option, no compiler/linker installed for native output |",
            "",
            "Use a mix of compiler/unit tests, source-level diagnostic fixtures and `conformance/` applications with expected outputs/HTTP responses. For SQL integration, create disposable databases with deterministic fixtures. Start real Postgres/MySQL test services in CI when their milestone lands; do not simulate parity with SQLite alone. Use real-browser checks for relevant CSRF/XSS/admin behaviour as supplemental CI, while the **public `kiln test --browser` command remains Phase 6**.",
            "",
            "Never equate passing tests with a guarantee of no bugs; the suite is an increasingly strong regression contract.",
            "",
            "## 15. Definition of done",
            "",
            "- [ ] A standalone Kiln binary can open SQLite without another service and perform typed, parameterized SQL, transactional operations and structured error handling.",
            "- [ ] The same supported SQL/ORM surface works against PostgreSQL and MySQL with documented dialect distinctions, TLS configuration and meaningful conformance coverage.",
            "- [ ] Models are normal structs; schema mapping, primary keys, nullability, defaults and relations are understandable and compiler-checked.",
            "- [ ] Typed `orm` queries reject unknown fields, invalid comparisons and unsupported expressions at compile time; runtime values are bound rather than concatenated; relation loads are explicit and bounded.",
            "- [ ] `kiln db pull` imports existing databases to editable model source plus mapping/snapshot, detects drift and never silently overwrites edits or mutates the source DB.",
            "- [ ] `kiln db diff` produces reviewable versioned, dialect-specific SQL; `status`/`verify`/`migrate` preserve integrity and do not silently apply destructive schema changes.",
            "- [ ] Typed form parsing and validation work with SSR Glazes and Phase 4 Embers, field errors, safe escaping, CSRF, bounded input and shared application/model field rules.",
            "- [ ] Secure local auth and durable sessions work with HTTP handlers; identity is request-bound, cookies are safe, logins rate-limited, passwords hashed with Argon2id, and authorizations enforced on every privileged server action.",
            "- [ ] A functional generated admin provides list/search/filter/create/edit/delete for several models, explicit protection, safe field defaults and per-model/field/page customization using Glazes.",
            "- [ ] An authenticated Ember invokes an ORM-backed typed `server fn` via Phase 4 RPC, with tested session identity, CSRF, per-record authorization and shared validation.",
            "- [ ] Real **multi-folder** SQLite and imported-schema dogfood apps run on Linux and Windows using Phase 4.2 local modules; C/native checks and Postgres/MySQL CI suites pass; diagnostics and LSP understand model/query/form fields.",
            "- [ ] API/reference documentation distinguishes implemented features from planned syntax, includes migration/auth threat-model guidance and teaches both raw SQL and ORM workflows.",
            "- [ ] Phase 5 needs no Wares, **mandatory** project manifest, external reactive framework or Node toolchain. Existing Phase 4 Embers/RPC are reused, while SSR-only CRUD and admin remain usable without client scripts.",
            "",
            "## 16. Explicitly deferred or optional",
            "",
            "- **Earlier prerequisites:** Phase 4 provides Embers/WasmGC and typed RPC; Phase 4.1 explicit `.ember` files; **Phase 4.2 local folder modules and an optional minimal `kiln.toml`**. Phase 5 adds durable data and authenticated authorization.",
            "- **Phase 6:** Extended manifest dependency fields and `kiln.lock`, Wares and registry, official `kiln/ui` component Ware, global cache, formatter/hot reload, public integrated `kiln test` runner and broader agent tooling.",
            "- **Phase 7:** One-command deploy, advanced observability/optimization, SSG, stability commitments, broad cross-architecture compiler/runtime specialization.",
            "- **Separate opt-in extension work:** OIDC/OAuth 2 providers, JWT/API token issuance, WebAuthn/passkeys/MFA, distributed rate limiting, streaming cloud uploads, cross-region migrations and advanced enterprise DB features. Keep extension points; **do not claim any of these exists until tested and documented**.",
            "- **Non-goals:** Inventing an ORM-only SQL dialect; implicit database queries when reading a normal field; auto-generating production migrations at startup; storing passwords or sessions insecurely to simplify demos; forcing one canonical user schema; enforcing Wares adoption; inventing a second template language for admin.",
            "",
            "## 17. Final architectural review principles",
            "",
            "- A simple SQLite-backed site should be almost as easy to start as a file-backed one.",
            "- Existing database owners should be able to adopt Kiln incrementally and without schema surprises.",
            "- Every database query and permission boundary should be explainable, inspectable and testable.",
            "- Models, forms, auth and admin should compose through ordinary Kiln types/functions rather than hidden configuration frameworks.",
            "- Security is a **compiler/runtime/application contract**, not merely a set of HTML controls.",
            "- Established maintained database and crypto libraries are replaceable implementation choices; capability, stable behaviour and good DX are the product.",
            "",
            "**In one sentence:** **Phase 5 turns Kiln into a complete database-backed application platform: typed data, reviewed migrations, shared SSR/Ember validation, protected RPC, secure identities and customizable admin—all without the glue.**",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/phase-6", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Phase 6 — Wares, testing, tooling and agent integration",
            "",
            "**Status:** Planned specification. This is an implementation contract and reviewable proposal for API details; **Phase 6 commands and syntax are not claimed to be implemented** unless later marked in reference documentation. The preceding [Phase 4 plan](phase-4.md) covers Embers and typed RPC, [Phase 4.1](phase-4.1.md) explicit `.ember` files, **[Phase 4.2](phase-4.2.md) local multi-folder modules and optional minimal project metadata**, and [Phase 5](phase-5.md) data/forms/auth/admin.",
            "",
            "**Goal:** Build on Phase 4.2's **already-available multi-folder project/module foundation** and Phase 5's data framework to deliver a practical package and developer ecosystem: reproducible third-party Wares in a safe shared cache, an official UI Ware, a public unit/integration/real-browser test command, formatter/dev workflows, deeper editor refactoring and agent-friendly tooling. General local-module semantics and the basic optional project manifest are **not** Phase 6 deliverables.",
            "",
            "**End-to-end gate:** A developer can take an existing Phase 4.2 multi-folder Kiln app and add a versioned UI Ware from a **locally hosted registry**, commit a reproducible lockfile, build without a network connection after cache priming, run Kiln-authored unit/database/real-browser tests, edit safely with formatter/LSP/live reload, and use a structured local MCP tool interface—**without writing Node scripts, per-project dependency installation directories or custom glue**. Its native release output remains self-contained.",
            "",
            "This implements previously agreed direction in the [design overview](../design.md#programs-and-folders), [vision](../vision.md#7-wares-templates-and-a-real-ecosystem) and [integrated testing plan](testing.md). Phase 4's [Embers/RPC plan](phase-4.md), [Phase 4.2 local modules](phase-4.2.md) and Phase 5's [data/auth/admin plan](phase-5.md) together make full-stack acceptance tests possible. Phase 6 packages and tests that foundation without reimplementing it.",
            "",
            "> **Design status:** Local folder modules, their import/visibility model and minimal optional `kiln.toml` belong to the **planned Phase 4.2** implementation. Phase 6 designs Ware dependencies, lockfiles, registry/security policies, public testing and deeper tools. Proposed command flags, external Ware manifest fields, registry endpoints and test APIs here are not claims of currently executable syntax.",
            "",
            "## 1. Scope and governing decisions",
            "",
            "1. **Phase 4.2 is the foundation.** Local folder modules, `pub`, source-root discovery and a minimal optional `kiln.toml` are prerequisites; preserve them, do not reimplement them.",
            "2. **Manifest grows incrementally.** Phase 6 extends the existing optional `kiln.toml` with dependency/publishing/build/test fields and uses `kiln.lock` for external Wares; simple scripts and local modules remain manifest-free. `kiln-deploy.toml` stays separate (Phase 7).",
            "3. **Compatible imports.** Wares reuse the Phase 4.2 module/export model with explicit package identity, not a new local module system.",
            "4. **One lockfile, deterministic dependency graph.** `kiln.lock` pins exact immutable versions, source identities, hashes and transitive edges. Build/release can require locked resolution.",
            "5. **Global, per-user Ware cache, not `node_modules`.** Immutable source is stored once by content hash and shared across projects; versions and dependency choices belong to each project.",
            "6. **Wares have no install-time execution hooks.** Fetching, resolving and checking a dependency does not run arbitrary package-provided shell commands.",
            "7. **Registry trust is layered, not magical.** Publisher verification, review status and hashes communicate different claims. A trusted-looking label is never a promise of sandboxed or vulnerability-free code.",
            "8. **Wares should feel like local modules.** One namespace/import model, no separate runtime package loader, no compulsory publish step for ordinary project structuring.",
            "9. **First official Ware is useful and inspectable.** A Kiln-maintained accessible UI component collection uses Glazes, CSS, typed Ui, Finishes, themes and Phase 5 forms; source-first editing/copy is supported.",
            "10. **One test workflow from function to browser.** `kiln test` owns Kiln-written unit, integration and real-browser E2E tests and uses maintained browser tooling internally—not a separate developer-visible JavaScript project.",
            "11. **Tooling is incremental and ordinary.** A deterministic formatter, cross-module LSP/refactoring, watcher/rebuilder and editor tasks share the existing compiler. Do not invent a new language service.",
            "12. **MCP reuses CLI/compiler semantics.** Provide useful local agent tools with explicit side-effect boundaries; do not expose unguarded shell/credential/network access to arbitrary clients.",
            "13. **No premature compatibility freeze.** Kiln is pre-1.0; capture the correct developer contracts now, then evolve internals behind conformance and end-to-end tests.",
            "14. **No forced cloud service.** Local modules, path/Git Wares, and a local or static registry must remain useful even if the eventual public registry is unavailable.",
            "",
            "## 2. Organize Phase 6 as four deliverable subphases",
            "",
            "| Subphase | Main deliverable | Independent gate |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| **6.1 — Ware ecosystem** | Resolver, `kiln.lock`, global cache, local/Git/registry sources, publish/verify tooling, first official accessible UI Ware | Two apps resolve reproducibly, offline, from a trusted local registry with shared cached contents |",
            "| **6.2 — Integrated testing** | `kiln test` unit/integration/real-browser including Embers, fixtures, reporting and CI | Real browser submits a form, exercises an Ember and typed RPC, and validates results, focus and reactive state |",
            "| **6.3 — Developer workflow** | Formatter, live rebuild/restart, deeper LSP and editor integration | Safe format/watch/refactor cycle across Kiln, Glaze, CSS and imported Wares |",
            "| **6.4 — Agent experience and dogfooding** | Compiler MCP, machine-readable tool contracts, docs/agent guide, full project example | A human or agent can inspect/check/test a full multi-folder app without hidden deployment or shell behaviour |",
            "",
            "Treat subphase numbers as **planning subdivisions**, not new language versions; each should break into reviewable PRs. The public registry's large-scale hosting, social/discovery features and organizational billing are not Phase 6 blockers.",
            "",
            "## 3. Developer walkthrough (future API)",
            "",
            "~~~text",
            "kiln run hello.kiln                  # no manifest",
            "kiln new fieldnotes --template web   # optional starter; no scripts executed",
            "cd fieldnotes",
            "kiln add kiln/ui                     # update kiln.toml and kiln.lock",
            "kiln check .",
            "kiln test --unit",
            "kiln test --integration",
            "kiln test --browser",
            "kiln fmt --check",
            "kiln build --locked --offline",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "`kiln new` and `kiln add` are proposals; the exact starter scaffolding and package name are not decided. Browser provisioning may require a one-time external binary download, but never a hand-maintained Node test project.",
            "",
            "Example larger app:",
            "",
            "~~~text",
            "fieldnotes/",
            "  kiln.toml",
            "  kiln.lock",
            "  main.kiln",
            "  posts/",
            "    queries.kiln",
            "    routes.kiln",
            "    post.glaze",
            "  accounts/",
            "    auth.kiln",
            "    login.glaze",
            "  assets/",
            "    styles.css",
            "  tests/",
            "    unit/",
            "      test_posts.kiln",
            "    integration/",
            "      test_routes.kiln",
            "    browser/",
            "      test_login.kiln",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "Sample target import experience:",
            "",
            "~~~kiln",
            "// main.kiln",
            "import web, posts, accounts",
            "",
            "fn main() ! {",
            "    let app = web.app()",
            "    posts.routes(app)",
            "    accounts.routes(app)",
            "    try app.listen(\"127.0.0.1:8080\")",
            "}",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "Files inside `posts/` share their folder's namespace, and `import posts` references public exports. Nested modules may be addressed with `import posts.search` if the design needs deeper packages. **The local-module import spelling and visibility contract are defined in Phase 4.2**; Phase 6 must reuse those semantics for Wares, not redefine them.",
            "",
            "## 4. Phase 6.1 — Ware identity, imports and packaging",
            "",
            "### Package identity and namespaces",
            "",
            "- Adopt a unique, normalized **publisher/package** identity, illustratively `kiln/ui` and `example/markdown`; require explicitly resolved names to avoid typo-confused package lookalikes.",
            "- A Ware is **source code plus declared static resources and a manifest**. It is not a dynamically loaded shared library or an opaque binary copied into deployed applications.",
            "- Wares re-use local module exports and `pub` visibility. Importing from another Ware must specify the package namespace or an explicit alias, so two versions/owners never silently collide.",
            "- Specify case rules, Unicode normalization, reserved names, max lengths and names that cannot masquerade as the Kiln standard library.",
            "- Package archives/indexes must have reproducible canonical content hashing, stable file ordering, normalized path handling and forbidden symlink/traversal rules. A hash covers **all** source/metadata that affects compilation, not just one manifest.",
            "- For a source package, protect against path references outside its package root unless the dependency is an explicitly authorized local-path source; no arbitrary filesystem escape via Glaze/CSS imports.",
            "- A Ware may contain Kiln, Glazes, CSS, assets, tests, examples and documentation. Document which files are published and which are excluded (secrets, build artifacts, lockfiles, private keys).",
            "",
            "### SemVer and resolution",
            "",
            "- Prefer SemVer constraints, pre-release semantics and explicit 0.x compatibility expectations. Lockfile pins **exact** releases; manifest ranges are not a source of nondeterministic fresh resolution during locked builds.",
            "- Resolve transitive dependencies with deterministic, documented conflict behaviour. Support distinct versions of the same Ware where possible by package/instance identity; if that requires disambiguated imports, make diagnostics explicit rather than collapsing versions incorrectly.",
            "- No silent preference for cache content over the lockfile. Resolver identity should include source URL/registry + package + release/revision + integrity hash.",
            "- Git dependencies pin immutable **commit SHAs** in the lockfile, even when the manifest mentions a branch or tag. Clearly warn that a moving Git ref is not deterministic until locked.",
            "- Path dependencies remain live source; the lockfile must reflect their presence without pretending they have an immutable registry digest. Document whether `--locked` permits changes in a local path package (source changes are intentional, unlike remote dependency drift).",
            "- Resolve upgrades with a dedicated command such as `kiln update [name]`; `kiln upgrade` remains exclusively for upgrading the **installed compiler**.",
            "- Unknown/unavailable versions, source conflicts, duplicate identities, cycles and policy-denied dependencies receive actionable resolution traces.",
            "",
            "### CLI shape",
            "",
            "~~~text",
            "kiln add kiln/ui@0.1",
            "kiln add example/widgets --git https://example.com/widgets.git --rev COMMIT",
            "kiln add local/widgets --path ./vendor/widgets",
            "kiln remove kiln/ui",
            "kiln update                 # update lockfile within allowed manifest constraints",
            "kiln update kiln/ui         # specific Ware",
            "kiln tree                   # show resolved dependency graph",
            "kiln tree --why kiln/ui     # explain why a dependency is present",
            "kiln build --locked",
            "kiln build --offline --locked",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "Exact flags and import naming remain proposed. `kiln add/remove/update` must modify manifest and lockfile together atomically; a failed resolve cannot leave the two inconsistent.",
            "",
            "## 5. Phase 6.1 — Lockfile, cache and offline builds",
            "",
            "### Lockfile guarantees",
            "",
            "- Machine-readable, documented `kiln.lock` with a schema version, root identity, exact graph edges, source identity, exact version/commit and cryptographic content hash.",
            "- Deterministically serialized: equivalent resolution produces byte-identical lockfiles across Linux/Windows (modulo documented local path normalization).",
            "- A locked build rejects manifest/lock mismatch; an offline build never contacts any network endpoint for Wares, advisory databases or tool updates.",
            "- Verify package integrity **before** placing an immutable source tree into the shared cache, and re-verify/correctly invalidate corrupted cache contents when used.",
            "- Source and compiled objects are cached separately; compiled artifacts are keyed by source hash + compiler/runtime version + target triple + backend + relevant features/build options.",
            "- Do not reuse compiled artifacts across incompatible compiler versions or target ABIs.",
            "",
            "### Per-user global cache",
            "",
            "Default path per platform: Linux `\$XDG_CACHE_HOME/kiln` or `~/.cache/kiln`; Windows appropriate LocalAppData path; macOS the platform's user cache directory. Allow documented override suitable for CI and air-gapped systems.",
            "",
            "- No `node_modules`-style or `ware_modules`-style copies in each project. Projects store their manifests, lockfiles and normal source only.",
            "- Atomic downloads/extraction with temp staging + integrity validation + atomic publish. Prevent Zip Slip/path traversal, crafted symlinks, files masquerading as directories, oversized decompression and stale temporary files.",
            "- Concurrent compilers/processes must safely coordinate cache writes and reads without deadlock or partially visible packages.",
            "- Read-only use of cache is supported for CI; downloading into a writable cache remains a separate explicit action.",
            "- Source package contents are immutable after verification. Local-path sources are **not** copied into the global store.",
            "- Cache pruning must never damage active in-progress builds; expose a dry run. It is not required to know every external project that might someday need an offline version.",
            "",
            "Proposed commands:",
            "",
            "~~~text",
            "kiln cache dir",
            "kiln cache stats",
            "kiln cache verify",
            "kiln cache prune --dry-run",
            "kiln cache prune",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "**Cache gate:** two unrelated apps using one hash share one cached copy; a second pinned version coexists; an offline build succeeds after priming and fails clearly when required content is missing; corrupted archives and parallel fetches cannot yield a silently compromised build.",
            "",
            "## 6. Phase 6.1 — Ware registry and publication",
            "",
            "### Bootstrap strategy",
            "",
            "Start with a **locally hosted registry implementing the same protocol as the future public service**. Because Phase 5 supplies SQL, auth and an admin (on top of earlier HTTP), the initial registry itself can be built **in Kiln** and serve as a substantial dogfooding app. A static index/Git source can be a simpler transitional read-only transport. Do **not** require a globally hosted public service or community accounts to finish Phase 6.",
            "",
            "Separate three roles:",
            "",
            "- **Compiler/client:** resolves index metadata and downloads immutable archives by hash.",
            "- **Registry server:** authenticates publishers, records immutable releases, serves metadata/blobs and yank/advisory status.",
            "- **Trust/review metadata:** describes publisher identity and independent review status. Registry claims must be verifiable, sourced and presented accurately.",
            "",
            "### Minimum registry protocol (target, versioned)",
            "",
            "- Read: package identity/version listing, exact release metadata, dependency manifest, checksums, archive download, yank status, optional trust/advisory metadata.",
            "- Write: authenticated upload/publish with immutable version and verified content hash; publisher ownership checks; idempotent retry semantics that reject content mismatch.",
            "- Use a versioned `/v1/` API (exact endpoint naming to settle during implementation) and stable schema; treat index metadata and blob payloads as **untrusted network input**.",
            "- Server pagination, cache headers/ETags, size limits, request limits, integrity verification, event/audit logs, auth token scopes and secure error handling. No credentials in lockfiles or logs.",
            "- A **yank** hides a version from new resolutions but does not break an existing valid lockfile without explicit policy. An issue/security warning can be surfaced separately. Do not silently delete an immutable release.",
            "- Publisher names cannot be taken over through package deletion or an expired account without a deliberate, auditable policy.",
            "- Local dev registry may use plaintext HTTP **only on explicit loopback endpoints**; remote credentials/publishing require TLS unless the user explicitly opts into an unsafe development mode with a warning.",
            "",
            "Proposed CLI:",
            "",
            "~~~text",
            "kiln publish --registry http://127.0.0.1:8081",
            "kiln publish --dry-run",
            "kiln login --registry https://registry.example",
            "kiln add kiln/ui --registry http://127.0.0.1:8081",
            "kiln ware verify kiln/ui",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "`kiln login` should use secure platform credential storage or a scoped environment token; never embed tokens in a manifest. The actual registry URL/command spellings are provisional.",
            "",
            "### Package permissions, security and trust tiers",
            "",
            "**Policy model to design and test:**",
            "",
            "1. `Unverified` — provenance or ownership has not received elevated verification.",
            "2. `Verified publisher` — an organization/publisher identity has been checked; **not** a full security review of code.",
            "3. `Peer-reviewed` — a specific immutable release passed a documented independent process; does not guarantee zero vulnerabilities. Review badges must be bound to exact release hashes.",
            "",
            "- Require explicit, machine-readable capability declarations for relevant effects (filesystem, network, process execution, environment/secrets, native/FFI, future plugins). Reuse or introduce compiler effect analysis to the extent sound; otherwise call out incomplete inference instead of presenting static detection as complete.",
            "- A project can choose an allowlist or policy file that restricts dependency publishers, sources and declared capabilities; evaluate **transitive** dependencies against it.",
            "- Running arbitrary scripts at fetch/install time is prohibited. Compilation/build may still process untrusted text, and using a Ware can execute its code at runtime: hashes and policy **do not amount to sandboxing**.",
            "- Surface permissions, trust tier, resolved source, changed capabilities and transitive dependency changes before install/update; get explicit approval for escalations where interactive, fail safely in CI without an approved policy.",
            "- Protect cache/manifest paths and lockfile integrity. Package source must not gain privileged compiler hooks or unrestricted custom build code as an accidental loophole.",
            "- Any sandboxed execution or enforced OS-level capability restrictions are separate future security engineering, not implied by labels or manifest permission metadata.",
            "",
            "**Registry gate:** a Kiln-authored local server can publish a Ware and serve a second project; publish conflicts, mismatched hashes, unauthorized accounts, revoked/yanked versions, policy violations and offline/locked consumers have conformance/integration tests.",
            "",
            "## 7. Phase 6.1 — First official Ware: accessible Kiln UI",
            "",
            "The flagship first Ware is a **source-first collection** of reusable Glaze/CSS/Ui/Finish components. `kiln/ui` is a **working package identity** pending final namespace ownership and version selection.",
            "",
            "~~~glaze",
            "{# Illustrative target syntax, subject to module/import resolution #}",
            "<form method=\"post\">",
            "    <Input",
            "        name=\"email\"",
            "        type=\"email\"",
            "        label=\"Email address\"",
            "        required",
            "    />",
            "    <Button type=\"submit\" tone={.Primary}>Save</Button>",
            "</form>",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "- Initial SSR components: `Button`, `Input`, `Textarea`, `Select`, `Checkbox`, `Radio`, `FormField`, `Label`, `Card`, `Alert`, `Badge`, `Table`, `EmptyState`, `Tabs` where CSS/native HTML can provide the intended behavior. Start with a small reliable subset; defer difficult interactive widgets.",
            "- Correct HTML semantics, label/input association, consistent field descriptions/errors, keyboard focus, contrast, disabled states, reduced-motion preference and responsive default styles.",
            "- Typed props for common variants (e.g. tone, size, error, disabled) and passthrough of safe ordinary attributes; integrate Phase 5 form field errors without making plain Input dependent on a database.",
            "- Use standard Phase 3 Glazes, CSS, Ui, Finishes and typed tokens. Users can override a theme, component style, selected field widget or authored Glaze. Avoid independent theme or runtime styling machinery.",
            "- Offer **dependency use** and a **source-copy** path, e.g. proposed `kiln ui add button`, so developers can own and modify individual component source without fork-heavy workflows. Copying must show provenance/license and should not overwrite local edits.",
            "- Publish docs/examples and compile conformance; tests need keyboard/focus/ARIA smoke plus manual accessibility review. **Do not claim automated checks prove accessible UX**.",
            "- Phase 4 supplies native browser interactivity through Embers, so the initial UI Ware may include tested interactive widgets rather than requiring an unrelated JavaScript framework.",
            "",
            "**UI gate:** a real Phase 5 form/admin page can use this Ware (or copied source), compile for native Linux/Windows, render a consistent accessible page with two different Phase 3 themes, and remain editable without dependency lock-in.",
            "",
            "## 8. Phase 6.2 — One `kiln test` runner",
            "",
            "Detailed requirements remain in [testing.md](testing.md); this section fixes its dependencies, delivery order and full Phase 6 gate.",
            "",
            "### Discovery and authoring",
            "",
            "- Tests are Kiln code, compiled by the ordinary parser/type checker/backends. No separate testing language, JavaScript config project or hidden interpretation of Glazes.",
            "- Proposed convention: `tests/unit/`, `tests/integration/` and `tests/browser/`, using `test_*.kiln` files and ordinary functions whose names begin `test_`. The final scheme must coexist with per-folder namespaces and package access rules.",
            "- A manifest may choose test roots/filters/fixtures, but **manifest-free projects can run basic unit tests** using the same discovery defaults.",
            "- Assertions are normal Kiln standard-library calls with typed failure messages and source spans. Return values/errors integrate with Kiln's `!` propagation; no invented `test` keyword needed.",
            "- Fixture setup/teardown is explicit and deterministic. Tests can access public app APIs; a mechanism for testing module-internal functions must not accidentally make those functions public to ordinary dependencies.",
            "",
            "Illustrative unit API:",
            "",
            "~~~kiln",
            "import test",
            "",
            "fn test_addition() {",
            "    test.equal(2 + 2, 4)",
            "    test.not_equal(\"x\", \"y\")",
            "}",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "### CLI and process contract",
            "",
            "~~~text",
            "kiln test",
            "kiln test --unit",
            "kiln test --integration",
            "kiln test --browser",
            "kiln test --filter login",
            "kiln test --watch",
            "kiln test --json",
            "kiln test --list",
            "~~~",
            "",
            "- Consistent counts, timings, typed assertion failures, expected/actual diffs, exit statuses (including a distinct infrastructure-failure category), filters and deterministic ordering.",
            "- Reuse normal project root/dependency resolution and a locked/offline mode for tests.",
            "- Parallelize isolated cases with explicit resource locks and test-specific temporary directories, ports and databases. Use a fresh DB fixture/migration state per integration suite where feasible.",
            "- Make retries **opt-in and visible**, never silently greenwash intermittent failures; timeouts and cancellation always reap child processes and release ports.",
            "- JSON event/summary format has a version, source URIs/spans and stable IDs suitable for CI/IDE/MCP consumption. Offer familiar JUnit XML format as an integration format when practical.",
            "- Code coverage measurement can begin with precise unit/function and file summaries; do not block the rest of testing on perfect coverage from every native codegen optimization level.",
            "",
            "### Browser mode: no Node project",
            "",
            "- Use maintained browser automation transport internally (WebDriver, CDP or Playwright-compatible engine); this is **not** a mandate to reimplement a browser or embed Chromium in app binaries.",
            "- Provision/cache pinned compatible browser executables separately. Explain first-download behaviour, platform support, offline setup and explicit version pinning.",
            "- Auto-launch a compiled Kiln HTTP server on a safe free port; wait for readiness, pass a typed base URL fixture and clean up on failures. Prevent accidental testing against production external URLs by default.",
            "- Kiln-authored browser APIs: navigate, find by accessible role/label/text, fill, click, submit, keyboard input, inspect URL/text/attributes, check focus, viewport, computed CSS and console/network errors.",
            "- Capture screenshot on failure and meaningful logs/traces when available; use source-linked failure diagnostics and reproducible artifact locations.",
            "- Test forms, cookie sessions, CSRF, permission checks and responsive Glaze/Finish themes with **real** browser semantics.",
            "- Interactive Ember state, hydration and typed server RPC must be supported by the Phase 6 public runner from its first release.",
            "",
            "**6.2 gate:** one command runs (a) a pure function test, (b) an isolated SQL/HTTP/auth integration test and (c) a headless browser scenario creating a record via form and an Ember server function, checking reactive updates, validation, keyboard focus, navigation, content, CSS and login/logout. Failures produce screenshots/logs, meaningful exit codes and machine-readable summaries on Linux and Windows.",
            "",
            "## 9. Phase 6.3 — Formatter, live reload and editor workflows",
            "",
            "### `kiln fmt`",
            "",
            "- Parse and format existing Kiln source through a deterministic, stable, idempotent layout engine. Establish canonical whitespace, imports, line wrapping, multiline structs/calls, closures and match formatting. Preserve comments, diagnostics and literal content.",
            "- Proposed modes: `kiln fmt`, `kiln fmt --check`, and optional `kiln fmt PATH`. Provide editor format-on-save through LSP using **the same** formatter.",
            "- **Glaze whitespace is semantic.** Never casually normalize newlines/indentation inside HTML text, CSS, `<pre>`, `<script>` or `<style>`. Start by formatting embedded Kiln code regions safely and add conservative markup formatting only with byte/render-equivalence tests.",
            "- CSS formatting should reuse recognized CSS tooling; no need to implement an entirely new CSS pretty printer. Do not overwrite malformed text when the formatter cannot preserve semantics reliably.",
            "- Golden fixtures + property/idempotence testing + compile/render equivalence checks; never turn a valid Kiln program into an invalid one.",
            "",
            "### `kiln dev` / watch",
            "",
            "- Provide a development watcher that rebuilds changed Kiln/Glaze/CSS/assets and **restarts** the running native server when necessary; keep existing successful server running if the new build fails.",
            "- No requirement for in-process native code hot patching, special runtime interpreter or Node build system.",
            "- Detect dependency/inclusion graph changes and invalidate only affected units, debounce editor save bursts, avoid file watcher loops on generated outputs; survive rename/delete.",
            "- Preserve clear logs, show diagnostics from source spans and expose what process/port is active. Graceful restart and session persistence follow the app's actual server/session semantics; do not promise live local-variable retention.",
            "- Optional browser live-reload refresh may use a dev-only channel injected into served pages; never bundle that mechanism into release applications. Document security of binding dev servers to localhost unless explicitly configured.",
            "- Watcher must be interruptible and clean child processes, sockets and temp outputs on Linux/Windows. Handle occupied ports with useful suggestions.",
            "",
            "### LSP and VS Code",
            "",
            "- Multi-root/project graph diagnostics, import completion, go-to-definition and references across local modules, Wares, Glazes, Finish functions, CSS modules, model/form fields.",
            "- Find references and rename respect public/private boundaries and avoid rewriting vendored immutable cached Ware source. Warn when an external/public rename would break dependants.",
            "- Formatter integration, test discovery/run status, error code actions, quick import fixes, manifest/lockfile validation, dependency tree view as feasible. VS Code should orchestrate CLI/LSP, **not fork language semantics in TypeScript**.",
            "- Refactor/format changes must be previewable where risky. Editor does not secretly add a Ware to resolve an ambiguous import.",
            "",
            "**6.3 gate:** a multi-folder app with a Ware survives format/check/watch cycles and cross-module rename; a broken source edit reports diagnostics while the last-known-good server remains available; Linux/Windows smoke tests pass.",
            "",
            "## 10. Phase 6.4 — Agents, MCP and documentation",
            "",
            "### Compiler/tooling MCP",
            "",
            "Proposed `kiln mcp` runs a local Model Context Protocol server giving an agent structured access to the **same** compiler, package and test APIs:",
            "",
            "| Tool type | Example | Safety level |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| Introspection | Project graph, exports, compiler version, dependency tree, source docs | Read-only |",
            "| Check | Type-check changed files, structured diagnostics, explain errors | Read-only with bounded compilation resources |",
            "| Test | Run named test suites and retrieve artifacts | **Executes app/test code**: requires an explicitly trusted project context |",
            "| Build | Compile target with explicit settings | Writes artifacts, may consume resources; request explicit workspace scope |",
            "| Format | Preview formatting diff or apply requested formatting | Diff/preview first, explicit write permission |",
            "| Add/update Ware | Propose manifest/lock changes, present capability diff | Network/filesystem mutation; require explicit approval |",
            "| Publish/deploy/migrate | Reserved for later, separate permissions and confirmations | **Never implicitly enabled** |",
            "",
            "- MCP is transport and structured tooling **not** an alternative compiler and not an application-runtime MCP standard library. Application-side MCP APIs can arrive separately.",
            "- Expose only allowed workspace roots; protect secrets, environment vars, tokens and arbitrary filesystem paths. Do not turn a “check” tool into shell execution or silently run project code. Compilers still parse untrusted input: resource limits matter.",
            "- Distinguish **safe to read/parse** from **may execute code**; running tests can contact a database or external system. Scope execution to a trusted local project and display side effects.",
            "- Use versioned tool schemas, stable diagnostic/error types, bounded outputs with pagination/links to artifacts, cancellation and clear capability boundaries.",
            "- No hidden background automation: tools respond to explicit requests and report completion/errors accurately.",
            "",
            "### Agent guide and benchmark",
            "",
            "- Document an idiomatic minimal Kiln app, language pitfalls, Glaze styling, SQL/ORM/auth, local module conventions, add/update Wares and how to use `kiln test` to validate agent output.",
            "- Provide a concise machine-friendly guide (e.g. `llms.txt` and optional proposed `kiln docs --llm`). A generated starter `AGENTS.md` is convenience, not a required part of app compilation.",
            "- Maintain realistic benchmark tasks: simple CLI tool; HTTP API; SSR page; imported DB query; auth-protected form; use of a Ware; broken compilation and test repair.",
            "- Measure compile/run success, number of corrective iterations, failure quality and changes to existing code; do not make unsupported claims about token reduction. Gate regressions after baseline stabilizes.",
            "",
            "### Dogfooding criteria",
            "",
            "- Complete a genuine multi-folder Kiln site, ideally the docs site plus a **Kiln-authored local Ware registry** built on Phase 5 storage/auth and a separate small application consuming it.",
            "- Publish the initial UI Ware to the local registry, consume it from a separate project, switch themes, replace one component with local source and demonstrate locked offline builds.",
            "- Drive a full `kiln test` suite including real browser tests and deliberately failing tests; exercise LSP/formatter/dev watch while changing imports and Glazes.",
            "- Have an agent use `kiln mcp` to get project graph, check and run authorized tests. Inspect outputs, error reports and permissions.",
            "",
            "**6.4 gate:** A newcomer (human or agent) can follow one documented path from an empty Kiln install to a multi-folder website with a package, authenticated form, deterministic build and browser-tested behaviour.",
            "",
            "## 11. Testing, CI and security matrix",
            "",
            "| Area | Must succeed | Must fail safely |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| Module graph | Cross-folder import, public exports, Glaze/CSS references | Private access, unresolved modules, cycles, case/path collisions |",
            "| Manifest | Optional scripts, correct root/entry selection, config precedence | Broken TOML, contradictory keys, secret exposure, invalid entry |",
            "| Resolver | Local/Git/registry Wares, transitive version resolution | Conflict, mismatched lock, moving refs in locked build |",
            "| Cache | Identical hash shared, offline build, concurrent writers | Corruption, archive escape, size bombs, stale partial download |",
            "| Registry | Local authenticated publish, immutable versions, yanks | Unauthorized publish, hash mismatch, replacement of existing release |",
            "| Permissions | Declared capabilities, transitive policy checks, tier metadata | Unapproved escalation, inaccurate verified/review labels, install hooks |",
            "| UI Ware | SSR rendering, safe overrides, two themes, accessible form | Invalid props, unsafe attribute injection, broken label/focus |",
            "| Unit tests | Discover, assert, filter, run fast, readable failures | Hidden panics, unreaped processes, flaky retries marked green |",
            "| Integration | Isolated database/auth/API tests and cleanup | Accidental production URL, leaked credentials, leftover test DB |",
            "| Browser | Actual navigation/forms/focus/CSS, screenshot failures | Flaky unbounded wait, browser missing without guidance, unsafe targets |",
            "| Formatter/dev | Idempotent formatting, last-good server, cross-file rename | Whitespace/Glaze mutation, watcher loop, corrupt auto-rename |",
            "| MCP/agents | Read/check/test structured tools within scope | Test execution treated as read-only, silent package mutation, credential leaks |",
            "",
            "CI must test native Linux/Windows and the C backend where supported, plus cross-module project conformance, locked/offline builds, registry/local protocol fixtures, malformed/untrusted metadata, and pinned real-browser tests. Use disposable servers and databases, including negative authentication tests. No test should require arbitrary external public service availability.",
            "",
            "## 12. Definition of done",
            "",
            "- [ ] Phase 4.2's manifest-free programs, deterministic folder imports, `pub` visibility, source-linked module errors and cross-folder compiler/LSP behavior remain passing regression prerequisites.",
            "- [ ] The **Phase 4.2 optional `kiln.toml`** gains backward-compatible Ware/dependency fields, while `kiln.lock` supplies exact repeatable graphs for local/Git/registry Wares.",
            "- [ ] Package name/source identities, SemVer constraints, transitive resolution, namespace collisions and locked/offline behaviour are documented and have regressions.",
            "- [ ] Immutable **per-user global Ware source cache** works with integrity verification, concurrency safety, optional compiled-object caching, inspect/verify/prune CLI and no project-local dependency tree.",
            "- [ ] A **locally hosted registry**, preferably built in Kiln, can accept authenticated immutable Ware releases and serve clients using a documented protocol with hash, yank and trust/permission metadata.",
            "- [ ] Package installs do not execute arbitrary scripts; capability reports and transitive policy checks give honest security boundaries, with no claim of perfect sandboxing.",
            "- [ ] The first official accessible UI Ware is publishable, usable in real SSR/forms/admin flows, customizable through normal Glaze/CSS/Ui/Finish patterns and optionally source-copied.",
            "- [ ] `kiln test` discovers/runs Kiln unit, integration and **real-browser** tests in one workflow, supports isolated resources and reports failures/screenshots/logs/CI results on Linux and Windows.",
            "- [ ] `kiln fmt` is deterministic/idempotent and preserves Glaze output; the LSP/VS Code support modules, Wares, references/rename and tests; a live rebuild/restart loop is safe.",
            "- [ ] `kiln mcp` exposes bounded, versioned tooling without silently authorizing filesystem writes, arbitrary project-code execution, credential reads, publish or deployment.",
            "- [ ] A full dogfood project demonstrates the local registry, UI Ware, locked offline rebuild, auth/forms/Glazes, editor workflow and browser suite.",
            "- [ ] Every implemented feature has reference docs, conformance/CI checks, clear unsupported-case diagnostics and no regression to compiler backends or standalone target deployment.",
            "",
            "## 13. Explicit deferrals and non-goals",
            "",
            "- **Earlier foundations:** Phase 4 supplies Embers/WasmGC, reactive hydration, generated RPC and state; **Phase 4.2 supplies local folder modules and an optional minimal project manifest**; Phase 5 supplies persistence/auth/forms/admin. Phase 6 integrates and tests them without moving module development back into this phase.",
            "- **Phase 7:** One-command deploy/release, static site export, broad stability/performance hardening, arbitrary code optimization or a mandatory new native backend.",
            "- **Later registry scaling:** Commercial hosting, public social discovery, web search/rankings, organization billing, cross-user global cache, complete security auditing/review infrastructure at industrial scale.",
            "- **Later tooling:** Full debugger/DAP and variable inspection, deep time-travel debugging, arbitrary live-patching of native code, self-hosting of all compiler internals.",
            "- **Optional later work:** Full multi-package workspace publishing, global dependency feature unification, a universal WASI/container sandbox, all-language FFI bindings, automatic use of arbitrary npm modules.",
            "- **Non-goals:** Mandatory manifests for scripts; checked-in downloaded Wares; install-time shell scripts; a Node/JS testing repository; a custom browser implementation; automatic publishing/deploying from `build`; pretending metadata labels enforce OS-level permissions.",
            "",
            "## 14. Review principles",
            "",
            "- Adding a local module must be as unsurprising as adding a folder.",
            "- One app must never be able to corrupt another app's locked dependency selection.",
            "- A verified download is not automatically a trustworthy application capability.",
            "- Tests should encourage quick experimentation and leave permanent regression coverage as Kiln's internals evolve.",
            "- A formatting or editor convenience must not silently change Glaze output or authorization semantics.",
            "- AI tooling should expose the same intelligible operations and errors a human developer sees, with **more explicit**, not fewer, safety boundaries.",
            "- Phase 6 should **reduce tooling glue without replacing sound existing tools purely for novelty**.",
            "",
            "**In one sentence:** **Phase 6 makes the already-multi-folder Kiln platform a practical ecosystem: reuse trusted Wares, build reproducibly, test from function to browser, and develop confidently in one coherent toolchain.**",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/runtime-rust", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Plan: less C in the runtime",
            "",
            "**Status: accepted; steps 0, 1 and 2 done.** Jake chose option A (hyper on",
            "Kiln's own tasks; see [The decision](#the-decision-hyper-and-kilns-tasks))",
            "on 2026-10-25. The work follows the [migration order](#migration-order):",
            "`crates/kiln_rt` is linked into every program, and JSON runs on",
            "serde_json. HTTP is next.",
            "",
            "## The problem",
            "",
            "Every Kiln program carries a runtime written in C: `kiln.c` (about 3,400",
            "lines, shared by both backends) and `native.c`. Most of it is ordinary",
            "value code (strings, lists, maps, numbers, time). But two parts read bytes",
            "straight from the network:",
            "",
            "- **HTTP** (`web`, about 900 lines): the request line and header parser,",
            "  body framing, keep-alive, percent-decoding, query strings and cookies.",
            "- **JSON** (`json`, about 600 lines): the decoder that `req.json<T>()`",
            "  runs on request bodies.",
            "",
            "Hand-written parsers of untrusted input, in C, are where small projects get",
            "security bugs: buffer overruns, request smuggling through conflicting",
            "`content-length` and `transfer-encoding`, integer overflow in sizes, deep",
            "nesting. Later milestones add more of the same: TLS, an HTTP client, form",
            "bodies, multipart uploads, cookies with signing.",
            "",
            "Kiln's vision already says *integrate before reinventing* (principle 6).",
            "This plan applies it to the runtime: hand the network-facing parts to",
            "widely used, fuzzed, maintained libraries, behind small interfaces so each",
            "one can be swapped later.",
            "",
            "## The proposal in one paragraph",
            "",
            "Add a Rust crate, `crates/kiln_rt`, compiled as a static library and linked",
            "into every Kiln program next to what is there today. It exports `k_*`",
            "functions with the C ABI, so generated code (Cranelift or C) calls it",
            "exactly as it calls `kiln.c`, and `gen.rs` does not change. It builds",
            "HTTP on [`hyper`](https://hyper.rs) (HTTP/1.1 server, later client), JSON on",
            "[`serde_json`](https://github.com/serde-rs/json), and later TLS on",
            "[`rustls`](https://github.com/rustls/rustls). Hyper runs **on Kiln's own",
            "tasks**, not on tokio. The Boehm collector, the task switch and the core",
            "value code stay in C for now. Users still download one `kiln` and need no C",
            "compiler, Rust or anything else.",
            "",
            "## The decision: hyper and Kiln's tasks",
            "",
            "Hyper is the obvious HTTP library, but most Rust code runs it on tokio,",
            "and Kiln already has a scheduler: cooperative, single-core, stackful tasks",
            "in `kiln.c`, where a handler is ordinary straight-line code that may wait",
            "(for a socket, `time.sleep`, later a database) and switch stacks in the",
            "middle. Four ways to fit them together:",
            "",
            "### A. Hyper on Kiln's tasks (recommended)",
            "",
            "Hyper 1.x does not depend on tokio. It asks for an I/O type that implements",
            "its `Read` and `Write` traits, and optionally a `Timer`. HTTP/1 connections",
            "need no executor at all.",
            "",
            "Each connection still gets a Kiln task, as now. On it, the runtime drives",
            "`hyper::server::conn::http1::Builder::serve_connection` with a tiny",
            "`block_on`:",
            "",
            "1. Poll the connection future.",
            "2. If it is pending, the socket wrapper has recorded what it was waiting",
            "   for (readable or writable) and the timer wrapper any deadline. Call",
            "   `k_task_wait(fd, write, deadline)`, which parks this task and runs the",
            "   others, exactly as the C server does today.",
            "3. When the task wakes, poll again.",
            "",
            "The waker does nothing: the only things that can make a connection ready",
            "are the ones `k_task_wait` already watches. Hyper's service function",
            "collects the body (within `max_body`), builds the `Request` slot array in",
            "GC memory, calls the Kiln handler **synchronously** through a small C",
            "shim, and copies the `Response` out. The handler runs on the same task",
            "stack, so it can wait and switch tasks in the middle of the poll: the",
            "future is pinned on that stack and nothing else touches it meanwhile.",
            "",
            "Why this one: one scheduler, the stackful-task model and its future",
            "multicore path stay as decided for phase 2, handlers stay plain code, and",
            "hyper is in charge of everything that touches raw bytes (parsing, chunked",
            "encoding, framing, keep-alive, `100-continue`, header limits and read",
            "timeouts). It is the least code to write that still removes the parser.",
            "",
            "Costs: an adapter of perhaps 300 lines of Rust (socket wrapper, timer,",
            "`block_on`, service shim), and the rules in [GC and panics](#gc-and-panics).",
            "`k_task_wait` may need to wait for read *or* write at once if hyper ever",
            "asks for both; HTTP/1 serving should not.",
            "",
            "### B. Parsing libraries only",
            "",
            "Use `httparse` (the parser inside hyper) for request heads and",
            "`serde_json` for JSON, and rewrite the connection loop (body framing,",
            "chunked decoding, keep-alive, limits) by hand in Rust.",
            "",
            "Smaller and simpler to wire up, and memory-safe. But the framing logic is",
            "where request smuggling lives, and it stays ours to get right. B is a",
            "reasonable fallback if A's adapter turns out awkward.",
            "",
            "### C. Tokio as the event loop",
            "",
            "Run a tokio current-thread runtime as the reactor and make each Kiln task a",
            "stackful coroutine that tokio resumes (for example with `corosensei`).",
            "",
            "This would give the whole tokio ecosystem (hyper-util, tower, database",
            "drivers) without adapters, and tokio's multi-threaded scheduler is one",
            "possible multicore path. But it replaces the scheduler phase 2 just built,",
            "adds a second model of concurrency to reason about, adds most of a",
            "megabyte to every program, and needs coroutine switching inside tokio",
            "tasks to cooperate with Boehm's stack scanning. Too much change for the",
            "problem; worth revisiting when multicore is planned.",
            "",
            "### D. Stay in C with C libraries",
            "",
            "Use [`llhttp`](https://github.com/nodejs/llhttp) (Node's parser) and",
            "[`yyjson`](https://github.com/ibireme/yyjson), and later mbedTLS.",
            "",
            "The fewest build changes, and these are battle-tested. But the connection",
            "handling around the parser is still hand-written C, it does nothing about C",
            "being the language new runtime code is written in, and the compiler is",
            "already a Rust project, so Rust libraries fit the repo better.",
            "",
            "## What stays in C",
            "",
            "- **The Boehm collector.** It is C, vendored, and long-lived; a precise GC",
            "  is a phase 7 item and would replace it then, not now.",
            "- **The task switch** (the assembly on x86-64 ELF, fibers on Windows) and",
            "  the scheduler around it. It is small, does not parse input, and Boehm's",
            "  stack hooks are C. It could move to Rust later with `naked` functions;",
            "  there is no hurry.",
            "- **Core value code**: strings, lists, maps, numbers, `show`/`eq`/`hash`,",
            "  `k_desc_*`. It handles Kiln values, not network bytes, and moving it is",
            "  a lot of churn for little safety gain.",
            "",
            "The rule from then on: **new runtime code goes in Rust**, and C code moves",
            "when it starts handling untrusted input (for example RFC 3339 parsing if it",
            "gets exposed to request data) or when it needs touching anyway.",
            "",
            "## Interfaces that keep each library replaceable",
            "",
            "- **Generated code ↔ runtime**: unchanged `k_*` C functions, listed in",
            "  `kiln_native/src/runtime.rs` and in a header for the C backend. Which",
            "  language implements a function is invisible to both backends.",
            "- **Inside `kiln_rt`**: one module per concern, each with one file that",
            "  touches the library.",
            "  - `web/http1.rs` is the only file that names hyper. The rest of `web`",
            "    (routing, building `Request`, error responses, the access log) works on",
            "    `http::Request` and `http::Response`, the shared types crate that hyper,",
            "    axum and others all use.",
            "  - `json/serde.rs` is the only file that names serde_json. The encoder and",
            "    decoder walk a `kdesc` (as today) and talk to a small \"write a JSON",
            "    value / read the next token\" interface. Decoding uses",
            "    `DeserializeSeed` driven by the `kdesc`, so there is no intermediate",
            "    `serde_json::Value`.",
            "  - Later, `tls/` behind a `TlsAcceptor`-shaped function, and `http`",
            "    client behind `http.client`'s API.",
            "",
            "Swapping a library means rewriting one file, not the runtime.",
            "",
            "## GC and panics",
            "",
            "Rust code inside a Boehm-collected program needs three rules:",
            "",
            "1. **Boehm does not scan Rust's heap.** It scans task stacks, registers and",
            "   static data conservatively, but a `Vec` or `Box` allocated by Rust lives",
            "   in `malloc` memory it never looks at. A Kiln pointer that is only held",
            "   there can be collected. So `kiln_rt` never stores a Kiln value in Rust",
            "   heap memory across an allocation: it builds lists and strings directly",
            "   in GC memory through runtime calls (as the JSON decoder must), and",
            "   copies a handler's response out into Rust bytes before going on. A",
            "   debug build can enforce this with a wrapper type that is only valid on",
            "   the stack.",
            "2. **No `longjmp` across Rust frames.** A Kiln panic in a handler jumps to",
            "   `k_panic_jmp` with `__builtin_longjmp`, which skips any frames in",
            "   between. The panic trap is therefore set in the C shim that calls the",
            "   handler, below every Rust frame, so the jump never crosses Rust code.",
            "   The per-task state already saved in `k_task_switch` covers the rest.",
            "3. **Rust panics abort.** `kiln_rt` builds with `panic = \"abort\"`; a Rust",
            "   panic is a runtime bug, prints a message and exits, and never unwinds",
            "   into C or Cranelift frames.",
            "",
            "## Builds, targets and size",
            "",
            "**Users** see no change: `kiln` still carries a prebuilt runtime per target,",
            "and programs are still single static executables. The phase 1.5 guarantee",
            "(no C compiler, linker, Rust or GC to install) holds, and `clean-check.sh`",
            "and `windows-check.sh` keep proving it.",
            "",
            "**Building `kiln` from source** gains a step (built in step 0).",
            "`crates/kiln_rt/staticlib.rs` runs `cargo build -p kiln_rt --profile",
            "runtime --target <t>` with a target directory of its own, so the nested",
            "build does not wait on the outer build's lock, and the build scripts that",
            "already compile the C runtime call it:",
            "",
            "- Linux (`kiln_native`): `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`. Rust's std for musl",
            "  reads stack traces with the `libunwind.a` Rust ships for that target, so",
            "  that goes in too. The libc stays the system musl's `libc.a`.",
            "- Windows (`kiln_native`): `x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`. The library joins",
            "  `native.o` and `gc.o` in the mingw link of `runtime.exe`, with `ntdll`,",
            "  `userenv` and `dbghelp`, all part of Windows, which phase 1.5 allows.",
            "- The C backend (`kiln_codegen`): the target `kiln` is built for, with",
            "  glibc instead of musl, since that is what the system's C compiler makes",
            "  programs for. It is linked next to `-lgc`.",
            "",
            "`rust-toolchain.toml` and the CI workflows list the two extra targets.",
            "",
            "**Size.** The `runtime` profile (in the workspace's Cargo.toml) builds with",
            "`panic = \"abort\"` and fat LTO, so the library's Rust code is one object",
            "holding only what the `k_*` functions reach. The library also holds all of",
            "Rust's compiler builtins and std's debug information, about 7.5 MB, so the",
            "build scripts link it with `cc -r` into one object of the members it needs,",
            "without debug information, and `kiln` carries that: 850 KB for Linux and",
            "650 KB for the C backend. Windows embeds its linked `runtime.exe`, which",
            "holds only what was linked in. mingw's linker cannot make one object of a",
            "library that uses thread-local storage, so `kiln.exe` carries the C",
            "backend's library stripped of debug information instead (1.5 MB). The",
            "stripped release `kiln` grew from 22.0 MB to 23.4 MB, and `kiln.exe` from",
            "25.9 MB to 28.3 MB.",
            "",
            "Measured in step 0, with only `k_web_reason` in Rust: a program grows by",
            "about 400 KB on Linux (130 KB to 530 KB for `conformance/basics`) and",
            "230 KB on Windows (230 KB to 460 KB). That is Rust's standard library's",
            "floor: panics, formatting and the stack-trace reader its panic message",
            "can use. It does not grow again with more Rust code, which costs what it",
            "uses. serde_json added about 115 KB on Linux and 98 KB on Windows (step",
            "1). Hyper's HTTP/1 server added 300 KB to a server program on Linux (560 KB",
            "to 865 KB for `conformance/web_server`) and 250 KB on Windows (560 KB to",
            "810 KB), and nothing measurable to a program that does not serve; the",
            "stripped release `kiln`, which carries three runtimes, grew from 24.0 MB to",
            "25.5 MB (step 2). Still to come: rustls with the `ring` provider, 1 to 2",
            "MB when TLS arrives.",
            "",
            "For TLS the `ring` provider is preferred over `aws-lc-rs`, which needs",
            "CMake and is harder to cross-compile for musl and mingw. HTTPS clients use",
            "bundled roots (`webpki-roots`) so programs need no system certificate",
            "store.",
            "",
            "## Effect on the backends",
            "",
            "- **Cranelift**: none beyond `runtime.rs` listing any new or changed",
            "  signatures. Calls stay C ABI.",
            "- **C backend**: still prepends the C part of the runtime, and now also",
            "  links `kiln_rt` for the system it runs on. It remains the reference backend, and",
            "  conformance still runs every program through both.",
            "- **Behaviour**: error responses, limits and the access log must not",
            "  change. The `web_*` conformance programs and `tests/server.rs` are the",
            "  check. Hyper's responses differ in small ways (header case, `date`",
            "  header); the conformance harness may need to ignore those.",
            "",
            "## Supply chain",
            "",
            "Depending on libraries is only safer if they are kept up to date:",
            "",
            "- `cargo-deny` in CI for RustSec advisories and licences (all candidates",
            "  are MIT or Apache-2.0).",
            "- Dependabot for Cargo, so security releases arrive as PRs.",
            "- Features kept minimal (`hyper` with `http1` and `server` only, no",
            "  `tokio`; `serde_json` without `arbitrary_precision`).",
            "- Exact versions in `Cargo.lock`, which is already checked in.",
            "",
            "## Migration order",
            "",
            "Each step is one PR, on both backends and both targets, with the C code it",
            "replaces deleted in the same PR.",
            "",
            "0. **Groundwork (done).** `crates/kiln_rt`, the nested build, prelinking,",
            "   the declarations in `kiln.c`, `cargo-deny` (`deny.toml`, which at once",
            "   found advisories in the `zip` crate `kiln upgrade` used, now updated)",
            "   and Dependabot. `k_web_reason` moved, through clean-check and wine.",
            "   Sizes are measured above.",
            "1. **JSON on serde_json (done).** No I/O and no scheduler involvement, so",
            "   it proves the GC rules on their own. `kiln_rt/src/json.rs` encodes with",
            "   a `Serialize` over a value and its `kdesc` (with a formatter that",
            "   writes floats as before) and decodes with a `DeserializeSeed` that",
            "   builds Kiln values through the C runtime's constructors, which are",
            "   `K_EXPORT` in `kiln.c` so the C backend links them too. Errors keep",
            "   their wording, with paths, and serde_json's syntax errors are put in",
            "   the same words; `conformance/json_untrusted` covers malformed, deep and",
            "   out-of-range input. What changed: text must be UTF-8 and escapes well",
            "   formed, a trailing comma is reported as such, and typed values nest at",
            "   most 128 deep (skipped members have no limit, and skipping does not",
            "   recurse).",
            "2. **HTTP on hyper (done).** `kiln_rt/src/server.rs` is the adapter from",
            "   option A: `k_rt_web_serve` drives hyper's HTTP/1 connection on the",
            "   connection's task with a `block_on` whose socket records what it waits",
            "   for, and `k_task_wait` waits for it. The timeouts are the socket's",
            "   (hyper needs no timer): the head of a request must arrive within",
            "   `timeout`, an idle kept-alive connection waits `keep_alive`, and any",
            "   other read or write may stall for `timeout`; `max_header` is hyper's",
            "   `max_header_size`. The service collects the body within `max_body`",
            "   with nothing of Kiln's held across the wait, then builds the `Request`,",
            "   calls `k_web_handle` in `kiln.c` (routing, handlers and error pages,",
            "   with the panic trap below them, as before) and copies the `Response`",
            "   out. Query strings, cookies and path segments are decoded on",
            "   `form_urlencoded`, `cookie` and `percent-encoding`. The C parser,",
            "   chunked decoding and connection loop are deleted; the accept loop and",
            "   the graceful close stay in C. What changed for clients: a request whose",
            "   head cannot be read gets hyper's bare 400, 414 or 431 (no body, and not",
            "   through `app.on_error`), an HTTP/1.0 request gets an HTTP/1.0 status",
            "   line, a request may have at most 100 headers, response header names",
            "   are lower case, and decoded request data that is not UTF-8 gets U+FFFD.",
            "   `tests/server.rs` gained a test of conflicting lengths, malformed and",
            "   oversized chunked bodies, `expect: 100-continue` and a bad header.",
            "3. **Later, with the features that need them**: `req.form` (phase 3 or 4)",
            "   on `form_urlencoded`, multipart on `multer`, the HTTP client and TLS on",
            "   hyper and rustls, signed cookies for sessions (phase 4).",
            "",
            "Steps 0 to 2 fit before phase 3, which adds form handling and would",
            "otherwise add more C parsing.",
            "",
            "## Risks",
            "",
            "- **Build complexity.** A nested cargo build and two extra rustup targets.",
            "  If that proves fragile, a fallback is a script that builds `kiln_rt`",
            "  first and points build.rs at the output.",
            "- **The adapter is ours.** Option A's `block_on` and socket wrapper are new",
            "  code, but small, and they handle readiness, not bytes.",
            "- **Hyper's defaults** (for example its idle connection behaviour) may not",
            "  match the current server exactly; the conformance tests find that.",
            "- **Binary size** grows; prelinking keeps it in check, and step 0 measures",
            "  it before anything else depends on it.",
        ]),
        DocSource(path: "plans/testing", section: "Plans", lines: [
            "# Integrated testing — planned Kiln toolchain capability",
            "",
            "**Status:** Planned. The public-facing development workflow is a single",
            "`kiln test` command for unit, integration and **automated real-browser",
            "frontend/end-to-end tests**. This is distinct from Kiln's existing compiler",
            "conformance tests and CI checks: application developers should not need to",
            "assemble a separate test framework and browser automation stack.",
            "",
            "**Roadmap:** Phase 4 delivers Embers with compiler conformance and real-",
            "browser CI smoke tests; Phase 5 adds SQL/auth and associated browser",
            "integration regressions. **Phase 6** delivers the public `kiln test` runner",
            "after Glazes (3), Embers (4) and database/auth (5); its initial browser",
            "mode must support hydration, events, reactive state and typed RPC.",
            "Do not change earlier phases' acceptance gates to depend on this public CLI.",
            "",
            "## What one runner means",
            "",
            "| Kind | Runs | Example responsibilities |",
            "| --- | --- | --- |",
            "| Unit | Kiln functions/modules in a controlled test process | Assertions, errors, fallible calls, deterministic inputs, table/parameterized cases |",
            "| Integration | Real Kiln modules and bounded application resources | HTTP routes, JSON, database queries/migrations, auth/session flows, jobs, fixtures and setup/teardown |",
            "| Browser / end-to-end | A **real browser** against a launched Kiln app | Navigation, forms, inputs, layout/state, keyboard focus, selectors, network interactions, accessible roles and responsive viewports |",
            "",
            "One runner discovers test suites by a predictable convention, compiles",
            "Kiln-authored tests with the normal type checker and reports results",
            "consistently. The three modes may share assertions, fixtures, setup,",
            "teardown and mock/test-data helpers. Assertions should show actual versus",
            "expected values and link to original Kiln/Glaze source spans. Tests should",
            "have reproducible results and clear failure causes.",
            "",
            "## Target CLI (illustrative, not implemented)",
            "",
            "```sh",
            "kiln test                  # all configured suites",
            "kiln test --unit           # fast pure/business-logic checks",
            "kiln test --integration    # app/API/database tests",
            "kiln test --browser        # headless real-browser end-to-end tests",
            "kiln test --filter login   # focused iteration",
            "kiln test --watch          # rerun relevant tests during development",
            "kiln test --json           # machine-readable output for IDEs/CI/agents",
            "```",
            "",
            "Exact CLI flags and Kiln test-file syntax are not fixed until implemented.",
            "Avoid inventing a parallel assertion DSL if ordinary Kiln functions and",
            "types can express the needed behaviour.",
            "",
            "## Crucial: browser testing is built into the Kiln experience",
            "",
            "Browser testing must be a **first-class mode of `kiln test`**, rather",
            "than an optional instruction to install Playwright, Node, Jest or a",
            "different developer-facing JavaScript test project.",
            "",
            "- Start/stop the built Kiln web server automatically where possible,",
            "  select an unused port, make its URL available as a typed fixture, and",
            "  clean up even when tests fail.",
            "- Provide ergonomic Kiln APIs for browser navigation, querying the DOM,",
            "  interacting with elements, typing, submitting forms, keyboard input,",
            "  checking text/attributes and asserting URL/state changes.",
            "- Prefer **accessible-role/label-based selectors** over brittle CSS paths.",
            "  Assert focus order, error messages and semantic accessibility where",
            "  relevant; automated tests assist but cannot prove full accessibility.",
            "- Support headless local/CI runs by default and opt-in headed debugging.",
            "  On failures provide reproducible logs plus **screenshots**, and where",
            "  practical traces/video and relevant HTTP/console errors.",
            "- Exercise screen sizes/media queries and CSS/themed Glaze pages. Add",
            "  screenshot-based *visual regression* as an optional later mode with",
            "  managed baselines and platform/rendering variance controls, not a",
            "  mandatory brittle screenshot assertion in every test.",
            "- From Phase 6's first release, the same test syntax covers Ember",
            "  behaviour, hydration and browser/server RPC alongside SSR Glazes.",
            "- Keep it possible to test an existing externally hosted site when",
            "  explicitly configured, but never accidentally run destructive tests",
            "  against production by default.",
            "",
            "**Reuse a maintained automation protocol/engine** (e.g. an established",
            "WebDriver or Chrome DevTools/Playwright-compatible backend), not a custom",
            "browser implementation. The Kiln CLI/API owns discovery, fixtures,",
            "test orchestration, assertions, diagnostics, environment isolation,",
            "configuration and results. Browser binaries are external platform assets;",
            "provide a predictable install/provisioning command or automatic setup",
            "with pinned browser versions and cache support. \"Built-in\" refers to",
            "the **developer workflow**, not embedding Chromium inside every compiled",
            "Kiln application or claiming no browser download is ever required.",
            "",
            "## Test infrastructure and reliability",
            "",
            "- Isolated test data/config, fixtures, disposable databases, deterministic",
            "  clocks and network mocks where needed; explicit opt-in for real external",
            "  services.",
            "- Parallel runs where tests are independent; resource locking and",
            "  test-specific ports/databases where they are not.",
            "- Retries are visible and opt-in; don't hide flaky or nondeterministic",
            "  failures behind automatic success.",
            "- Timeouts, process cleanup, failure summaries and CI exit codes should",
            "  work on Linux and Windows.",
            "- Compatible code coverage/report formats, machine-readable output,",
            "  IDE links and easy CI integration; unit suites should remain fast.",
            "- Document trust boundaries: browser tests can exercise live systems,",
            "  so external URLs, credentials and potentially destructive operations",
            "  should be explicit and constrained.",
            "",
            "## Delivery checkpoints",
            "",
            "**Phase 6 acceptance**",
            "",
            "- [ ] A `kiln test` invocation discovers and executes Kiln-written",
            "      unit tests, reports useful failures and gives a nonzero exit code.",
            "- [ ] Integration tests can provision/teardown an isolated Kiln app",
            "      with its HTTP/API and a test database.",
            "- [ ] Browser tests can launch a real headless browser, visit a compiled",
            "      Kiln/Glaze page, fill a form, submit it, navigate, assert content,",
            "      keyboard/focus behaviour and CSS theme differences.",
            "- [ ] The runner handles a failing browser test with screenshots/logs,",
            "      source-linked diagnostics and nonzero CI exit status.",
            "- [ ] Developers can run all three kinds via the same CLI with **no",
            "      hand-maintained Node or JS testing project**; browser setup and",
            "      caching are documented.",
            "- [ ] The implementation reuses maintained browser engines/protocols",
            "      and passes reproducible smoke suites on Linux and Windows.",
            "",
            "**Ember coverage within Phase 6 acceptance**",
            "",
            "- [ ] The browser suite verifies an Ember's hydration, reactive updates,",
            "      events and calls to typed server functions using the same runner",
            "      and assertions as other application and Glaze tests.",
            "",
            "## Boundaries",
            "",
            "This does **not** require a custom JS execution engine, a new browser,",
            "a separate testing language, automatic screenshot testing of every Glaze,",
            "or shipping browser binaries inside final server executables.",
            "",
            "**Principle:** From a pure function to a database-backed HTTP route to",
            "the page someone clicks, **one language and one test command** should",
            "provide the developer's complete confidence loop.",
        ]),
    ]
}
